window.UNITS=[{"id":"u1","title":"Data Foundations","day":1,"summary":"What data-driven decision making is, and the vocabulary for describing data: types, levels of measurement, populations vs samples, and how data is stored and collected.","lesson":[{"h":"1. What is data-driven decision making?","body":"<p><strong>Data-driven decision making (DDDM)</strong> means choosing a course of action because evidence supports it, instead of relying only on gut feeling, habit, or the opinion of the loudest person in the room.</p><p>A simple DDDM cycle looks like this:</p><ol><li><strong>Define the question</strong> (for example: why are late deliveries rising?).</li><li><strong>Collect the right data</strong> (delivery times, carrier, route, weather).</li><li><strong>Clean and organize</strong> the data so it can be trusted.</li><li><strong>Analyze</strong> it (summaries, charts, statistics).</li><li><strong>Decide and act</strong> (switch carriers, change routes).</li><li><strong>Measure the result</strong> and repeat.</li></ol><p><em>Business example:</em> A bakery owner feels that Tuesdays are slow. Instead of cutting Tuesday hours on a hunch, she pulls twelve weeks of sales by weekday. The data shows Tuesday is actually her third-best day, but she was running out of bread by noon. The decision changes from 'close early' to 'bake more on Tuesdays'. Data did not just support a decision, it prevented a bad one.</p><p>Key idea: data helps, but it does not decide for you. Good decisions combine data with business judgment, and they depend on data being <strong>relevant, accurate, and timely</strong>.</p>"},{"h":"2. Data, information, knowledge (the DIKW pyramid)","body":"<p>These four words are often confused. Think of them as steps up a ladder, each one more useful than the last.</p><ul><li><strong>Data</strong>: raw, unprocessed facts with no context. Example: 42, 37, 55, 48.</li><li><strong>Information</strong>: data that has been organized or given context so it answers who, what, where, or when. Example: 'Store 7 sold 42, 37, 55 and 48 units on Monday through Thursday.' or 'average daily sales at Store 7 were 45.5 units'.</li><li><strong>Knowledge</strong>: information combined with experience and understanding of patterns, so you know how and why. Example: 'Our sales spike on Wednesdays because of the local farmers market.'</li><li><strong>Wisdom</strong>: using knowledge to make good decisions. Example: 'Staff up and stock more on Wednesdays.'</li></ul><p>Memory trick: remember the order <strong>D-I-K-W</strong>, and that each level answers a bigger question: raw facts, then meaning, then understanding, then action.</p><p>Exam tip: if a question shows a plain list of numbers with no labels or summary, the answer is <strong>data</strong>. If it shows a summary, report or chart that gives meaning, the answer is <strong>information</strong>.</p>"},{"h":"3. Types of data: categorical vs quantitative, discrete vs continuous","body":"<p>The first question to ask about any variable is: <strong>is it a label or a number I can do math on?</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Qualitative (categorical) data</strong> describes qualities or categories: eye color, product type, state, 'defective or not'. You can count how many are in each category, but averaging makes no sense.</li><li><strong>Quantitative (numerical) data</strong> are real numbers where arithmetic is meaningful: price, weight, number of units, minutes.</li></ul><p><strong>Watch out:</strong> numbers can be labels! A ZIP code, phone number, employee ID or jersey number is categorical, because adding or averaging those numbers is meaningless.</p><p>Quantitative data splits again:</p><ul><li><strong>Discrete</strong>: values you <em>count</em>, usually whole numbers with gaps between them. Number of defects, customers, or employees. You cannot have 3.5 defects.</li><li><strong>Continuous</strong>: values you <em>measure</em> that can take any value in a range, including decimals. Weight, time, temperature, length, revenue per hour.</li></ul><p>Quick test: <em>Could the value reasonably be 2.5?</em> If yes, it is continuous. If it can only be counted in whole steps, it is discrete. Rounding a measurement (a weight of 12 pounds) does not make it discrete.</p>"},{"h":"4. Levels of measurement: NOIR","body":"<p>The <strong>level of measurement</strong> tells you what math and comparisons are allowed. Remember the word <strong>NOIR</strong> (French for 'black'): Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, Ratio, from weakest to strongest.</p><ul><li><strong>Nominal</strong>: categories with names only, <em>no natural order</em>. Examples: department, product color, payment method, ZIP code. Only counts and the mode make sense.</li><li><strong>Ordinal</strong>: categories that <em>can be ranked</em>, but the gaps between ranks are not equal or measurable. Examples: shirt size (S, M, L), survey scale (poor to excellent), finishing place (1st, 2nd, 3rd), Likert ratings. The median is meaningful.</li><li><strong>Interval</strong>: numeric, with <em>equal gaps</em>, but <em>no true zero</em> (zero does not mean 'none'). Examples: temperature in Celsius or Fahrenheit, calendar year, IQ score. You can add and subtract, but ratios are meaningless: 80 degrees F is not 'twice as hot' as 40 degrees F.</li><li><strong>Ratio</strong>: numeric, equal gaps, and a <strong>true zero</strong> meaning none. Examples: weight, height, revenue, age, time, number of defects. 'Twice as much' makes sense: 20 pounds is twice 10 pounds.</li></ul><p><strong>Three-question test:</strong></p><ol><li>Can the values be put in a meaningful order? If <strong>no</strong>, it is <strong>nominal</strong>.</li><li>Ordered, but are the gaps between values equal and measurable? If <strong>no</strong>, it is <strong>ordinal</strong>.</li><li>Equal gaps: does zero mean 'none of it'? If <strong>no</strong>, <strong>interval</strong>. If <strong>yes</strong>, <strong>ratio</strong>.</li></ol><p>Shortcut: if you can say 'this is twice that', it is ratio.</p>"},{"h":"5. Population vs sample, parameter vs statistic","body":"<p>A <strong>population</strong> is the entire group you want to know about: every customer, every part produced this year, every employee. A <strong>sample</strong> is a smaller group taken from the population that you actually measure.</p><p>Why sample? Measuring everything (a <strong>census</strong>) is often too slow, too costly, or impossible (testing every light bulb until it burns out would leave you with none to sell).</p><p>The numbers computed from each have different names:</p><ul><li>A <strong>parameter</strong> is a number that describes a <em>population</em> (for example, the true average weight of all 50,000 boxes shipped).</li><li>A <strong>statistic</strong> is a number that describes a <em>sample</em> (the average weight of the 100 boxes you weighed).</li></ul><p>Memory trick: <strong>P</strong>arameter goes with <strong>P</strong>opulation, <strong>S</strong>tatistic goes with <strong>S</strong>ample.</p><p>We usually cannot know the parameter, so we use a statistic to estimate it. This is the heart of statistics: using a sample to learn about a population. A good sample is <strong>representative</strong> (it looks like the population). Notation: population mean is the Greek letter mu, sample mean is x-bar; population standard deviation is sigma, sample standard deviation is s.</p><p><em>Business example:</em> A plant makes 12,000 parts this week. An inspector checks 200 and finds 14 defective. The sample proportion 14 / 200 = 0.07 (7 percent) is a statistic. The real defect rate among all 12,000 parts is the parameter, and we estimate it at about 7 percent.</p>"},{"h":"6. How data is stored and collected: structured vs unstructured, primary vs secondary, cross-sectional vs time-series","body":"<p><strong>Structured vs unstructured</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Structured data</strong> fits neatly into rows and columns with fixed fields: spreadsheets, SQL databases, sales tables. Easy to sort, filter and analyze.</li><li><strong>Unstructured data</strong> has no predefined format: emails, customer review text, photos, audio, video, social media posts. It needs special tools (like text analysis) to extract meaning. (There is also <em>semi-structured</em> data such as JSON or XML files, which has tags but no rigid table.)</li></ul><p><strong>Primary vs secondary</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Primary data</strong> is collected firsthand by you for your own purpose: your own survey, your own experiment, your own measurements on the line. It fits your question exactly but costs time and money.</li><li><strong>Secondary data</strong> was collected by someone else for another purpose: census data, industry reports, a competitor's published figures, government statistics. It is faster and cheaper but may not fit your question, and may be out of date.</li></ul><p><strong>Cross-sectional vs time-series</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Cross-sectional data</strong> is a snapshot: many subjects measured at one point in time (satisfaction scores of 500 customers in March).</li><li><strong>Time-series data</strong> follows the same thing over many time points (monthly sales for 36 months in a row). It lets you see trends and seasonality.</li><li>Data combining both (many subjects tracked over time) is called <strong>panel</strong> or longitudinal data.</li></ul>"},{"h":"7. Variables: independent and dependent","body":"<p>A <strong>variable</strong> is any characteristic that can take different values across the people or items you study (price, region, defect count). The values you record are the data.</p><p>When you ask 'does one thing affect another?', the two variables play different roles:</p><ul><li>The <strong>independent variable</strong> (also called the predictor, explanatory variable, or input) is the one you change or suspect is the cause. It goes on the x-axis.</li><li>The <strong>dependent variable</strong> (also called the response, outcome, or output) is the one you measure to see the effect. It 'depends on' the independent variable. It goes on the y-axis.</li></ul><p><em>Example:</em> A manager wants to know whether hours of training reduce the number of assembly errors. Training hours is the independent variable. Errors per week is the dependent variable.</p><p>Memory trick: ask 'what depends on what?' The thing that depends on the other is the dependent variable. In a sentence 'X affects Y', X is independent and Y is dependent.</p><p>There are also <strong>control variables</strong>, which you hold constant so they do not muddy the result (same machine, same shift), and <strong>confounding variables</strong>, which affect both and can fool you (covered in Unit 2). Finally, identify each variable's data type (categorical or numeric, and its NOIR level), because that decides which summaries and charts are appropriate.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Data driven decision making means using evidence from data, together with business judgment, to choose actions instead of relying only on gut feeling.","Data are raw facts, information is data organized with context, knowledge is understanding of patterns, and wisdom is using that knowledge to make good decisions. This ladder is called the D I K W pyramid.","Qualitative or categorical data describes labels and categories, while quantitative data are numbers on which arithmetic makes sense. Numbers used as labels, like ZIP codes, are still categorical.","Discrete data are counted in whole steps, such as the number of defects. Continuous data are measured and can take any value in a range, such as weight or time.","The four levels of measurement, remembered as N O I R, are nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio, ordered from weakest to strongest.","Nominal data are unordered categories. Ordinal data are ordered categories with unequal or unknown gaps, like survey ratings or shirt sizes.","Interval data have equal gaps but no true zero, like temperature in Fahrenheit. Ratio data have equal gaps and a true zero, so statements like twice as much make sense, as with weight, revenue and age.","A population is the entire group of interest and a sample is the smaller group you actually measure. A parameter describes a population and a statistic describes a sample.","Structured data fits into rows and columns, like a spreadsheet or database. Unstructured data, like emails, photos and reviews, has no fixed format and needs special tools to analyze.","Primary data are collected firsthand for your own purpose, while secondary data were collected by someone else for a different purpose, such as census or industry reports.","Cross sectional data capture many subjects at a single point in time, while time series data follow the same measure across many points in time so you can see trends.","The independent variable is the input you change or suspect is the cause, and the dependent variable is the outcome you measure that depends on it."],"examples":[{"q":"A quality manager collects four variables for each shipment: (A) the carrier name (Alpha, Beta, Gamma), (B) a customer satisfaction rating from 1 (very unhappy) to 5 (very happy), (C) the temperature inside the truck in degrees Celsius, and (D) the number of damaged boxes. Classify each variable by level of measurement.","steps":["Use the three-question test on each variable: ordered? equal gaps? true zero?","A, carrier name: the names have no natural order, so it is NOMINAL.","B, satisfaction rating 1 to 5: the values are ordered, but the gap between 'happy' and 'very happy' is not guaranteed to equal the gap between 'unhappy' and 'neutral', so it is ORDINAL.","C, temperature in Celsius: ordered with equal gaps, but 0 degrees C does not mean 'no temperature' (it is just the freezing point of water), so it is INTERVAL.","D, number of damaged boxes: ordered, equal gaps, and zero really means none, so it is RATIO. (It is also discrete because it is counted.)"],"answer":"A = nominal, B = ordinal, C = interval, D = ratio (and D is discrete)."},{"q":"A plant produced 12,000 parts this week. An inspector randomly selects 200 parts and finds 14 defective. (a) Identify the population and the sample. (b) Compute the sample defect proportion. (c) Is that number a parameter or a statistic?","steps":["Population = all 12,000 parts produced this week. Sample = the 200 inspected parts.","Sample proportion = number defective / sample size = 14 / 200.","14 / 200 = 0.07, which is 7 percent.","Because this number describes the sample, it is a STATISTIC. The true defect rate of all 12,000 parts would be a parameter, and it is unknown unless every part is checked."],"answer":"Sample proportion = 0.07 (7 percent), which is a statistic that estimates the unknown population parameter."},{"q":"A company wants to know if the number of hours of safety training (0, 2, 4, or 8 hours) affects the number of workplace incidents per employee over the next year. Identify the independent variable, the dependent variable, and the data type and level of measurement of each.","steps":["Ask 'what depends on what?' The number of incidents is expected to depend on the training, so incidents is the DEPENDENT variable and training hours is the INDEPENDENT variable.","Training hours (0, 2, 4, 8) is numeric, measured in time, and zero means no training, so it is quantitative, ratio. It is continuous in principle (it could be 2.5 hours), even though only four values are used here.","Incidents per employee is a count, so it is quantitative, discrete, and ratio (zero incidents is a true zero).","Because the company is controlling and assigning training hours and then measuring incidents, the data would be collected going forward (primary data)."],"answer":"Independent = training hours (quantitative, ratio, continuous). Dependent = incidents per employee (quantitative, ratio, discrete)."}],"videos":[{"title":"Levels of measurement (nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio) explained","query":"levels of measurement nominal ordinal interval ratio explained simply"},{"title":"Population vs sample, parameter vs statistic","query":"Khan Academy population vs sample parameter statistic"},{"title":"Types of data: categorical, numerical, discrete, continuous","query":"types of data categorical numerical discrete continuous statistics for beginners"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Data-driven decision making (DDDM)","b":"Using evidence from data, combined with business judgment, to choose actions instead of relying on gut feeling alone."},{"f":"DIKW pyramid","b":"Data (raw facts) -> Information (organized with context) -> Knowledge (understanding patterns) -> Wisdom (acting well on it)."},{"f":"Data vs information","b":"Data are raw, unprocessed facts. Information is data organized or summarized so it has meaning, such as an average or a report."},{"f":"Categorical (qualitative) data","b":"Labels or categories (color, department, yes/no). You count them but do not do arithmetic on them."},{"f":"Quantitative (numerical) data","b":"Numbers on which arithmetic is meaningful, such as price, weight, or number of units."},{"f":"Discrete data","b":"Quantitative data that is COUNTED in whole steps (number of defects, customers). Cannot be 2.5."},{"f":"Continuous data","b":"Quantitative data that is MEASURED and can take any value in a range (weight, time, temperature)."},{"f":"Nominal level","b":"Categories with names only and no natural order (department, color, ZIP code). Mode only."},{"f":"Ordinal level","b":"Ordered categories where the gaps between ranks are not equal or known (survey scale, shirt size, 1st/2nd/3rd)."},{"f":"Interval level","b":"Numeric with equal gaps but NO true zero (Celsius, Fahrenheit, calendar year). Ratios are meaningless."},{"f":"Ratio level","b":"Numeric with equal gaps AND a true zero (weight, revenue, age, defects). 'Twice as much' makes sense."},{"f":"How to tell NOIR levels apart","b":"Ordered? No = nominal. Equal gaps? No = ordinal. True zero? No = interval, Yes = ratio."},{"f":"Population vs sample","b":"Population = the entire group of interest. Sample = the smaller subset actually measured."},{"f":"Parameter","b":"A number that describes a POPULATION (usually unknown). Think P with P."},{"f":"Statistic","b":"A number that describes a SAMPLE, used to estimate the parameter. Think S with S."},{"f":"Structured data","b":"Data organized in fixed rows and columns, such as spreadsheets and SQL databases."},{"f":"Unstructured data","b":"Data with no predefined format: emails, reviews, photos, audio, video, social posts."},{"f":"Primary data","b":"Data you collect firsthand for your own purpose (your own survey or experiment)."},{"f":"Secondary data","b":"Data collected by someone else for another purpose (census, industry reports, published studies)."},{"f":"Cross-sectional vs time-series data","b":"Cross-sectional = many subjects at one point in time (snapshot). Time-series = same measure tracked across many time points (trend)."},{"f":"Independent variable","b":"The input or suspected cause that you change or use to predict. Plotted on the x-axis."},{"f":"Dependent variable","b":"The outcome you measure that depends on the independent variable. Plotted on the y-axis."}],"questions":[{"q":"A retailer records each customer's shirt size as Small, Medium, Large, or Extra Large. What is the level of measurement of this variable?","o":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"a":1,"e":"Shirt sizes can be ranked from smallest to largest, but the gaps between sizes are not equal or measurable, so the variable is ordinal. Nominal is tempting because sizes are labels, but nominal data has no natural order.","d":2},{"q":"A weather analyst records the daily high temperature in degrees Fahrenheit. Why is this data interval rather than ratio?","o":["The values cannot be ranked from low to high","The values are counted rather than measured","The values have unequal gaps between them","Zero degrees does not mean there is no temperature, so ratios are not meaningful"],"a":3,"e":"Fahrenheit has equal gaps but an arbitrary zero. Because zero does not mean 'none', saying 80 degrees is twice as hot as 40 degrees is meaningless. Temperature can certainly be ranked and measured, which rules out the other options.","d":2},{"q":"A manufacturer records the number of defective units found in each batch. Which description is most accurate?","o":["Quantitative, discrete, ratio","Quantitative, continuous, interval","Qualitative, nominal","Quantitative, continuous, ratio"],"a":0,"e":"A count of defects is numeric, takes whole-number values (discrete), and zero defects truly means none (ratio). It is not continuous because you cannot have 2.5 defective units.","d":2},{"q":"A company has 4,500 employees. HR surveys 300 of them and finds the average commute is 27 minutes. The 27 minutes is best described as a:","o":["Population","Parameter","Variable","Statistic"],"a":3,"e":"The 27 minutes is computed from a sample of 300 employees, so it is a statistic. A parameter would be the average commute of all 4,500 employees, which is unknown unless everyone is measured.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following variables is continuous?","o":["Number of customer complaints per day","Number of employees in a department","Number of items in a shopping cart","Time, in seconds, to complete an online checkout"],"a":3,"e":"Time is measured and can take any value in a range (for example 47.3 seconds), so it is continuous. The other three are counts of whole things, which are discrete.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of unstructured data?","o":["A SQL table of customer orders","An Excel sheet of monthly revenue","A collection of free-text customer reviews","A database table of employee IDs and hire dates"],"a":2,"e":"Free-text reviews have no fixed rows-and-columns format, so they are unstructured. Spreadsheets and SQL tables are structured.","d":2},{"q":"A marketing analyst downloads publicly available census data to size a new market. This is an example of:","o":["Primary data","Secondary data","Experimental data","Unstructured data"],"a":1,"e":"Census data was collected by the government for other purposes, so for the analyst it is secondary data. Primary data would be data the analyst collected personally for this specific study.","d":2},{"q":"A company tracks its total monthly sales for 36 consecutive months in order to identify seasonal patterns. What type of data is this?","o":["Cross-sectional data","Time-series data","Qualitative data","Primary data only"],"a":1,"e":"The same measure (sales) is followed across many consecutive time points, which is time-series data. Cross-sectional data would be many stores measured in a single month.","d":2},{"q":"A study examines whether the number of hours of employee training affects the number of errors made per week. Which variable is the dependent variable?","o":["The number of hours of training","The employee's name","The number of errors per week","The length of the study"],"a":2,"e":"Errors are the outcome that is expected to depend on training, so they are the dependent variable. Training hours is the independent variable (the suspected cause).","d":2},{"q":"A column in a spreadsheet contains the values 41, 73, 52, 38, 66 with no labels, units, or context. In the DIKW hierarchy this is best described as:","o":["Data","Information","Knowledge","Wisdom"],"a":0,"e":"A bare list of numbers with no context is raw data. It becomes information once it is labeled and summarized (for example, 'average wait time was 54 minutes').","d":2},{"q":"Customers rate service as Poor, Fair, Good, or Excellent. An analyst wants to report a typical response. Which measure of center is the most appropriate and allowable?","o":["Mean, because the ratings can be averaged like numbers","Median or mode, because the data is ordinal","Only the range, because the data is nominal","Geometric mean, because the data is ratio"],"a":1,"e":"Ratings are ordinal: they can be ranked, but the gaps are not equal, so a mean is not strictly meaningful. The median (middle rating) and mode (most common rating) are appropriate.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is measured at the RATIO level?","o":["Calendar year of manufacture","Customer ZIP code","Weight of a package in pounds","Temperature in degrees Celsius"],"a":2,"e":"Weight has equal intervals and a true zero (zero pounds means no weight), so a 20-pound package is truly twice a 10-pound package. Calendar year and Celsius temperature are interval, and ZIP code is nominal.","d":2},{"q":"An HR team lists employee ID numbers (for example 10482, 20917, 33054). How should this variable be classified?","o":["Quantitative, ratio","Quantitative, interval","Qualitative (categorical), nominal","Quantitative, continuous"],"a":2,"e":"ID numbers are just labels. Adding or averaging them is meaningless, so they are categorical and nominal even though they are written with digits.","d":2},{"q":"A quality engineer measures the diameter of 50 parts from a single production run that happened this morning. What type of data is this, and how was it obtained?","o":["Time-series data, secondary","Unstructured data, secondary","Cross-sectional data, primary","Qualitative data, primary"],"a":2,"e":"Many items were measured at roughly one point in time (cross-sectional), and the engineer collected them firsthand (primary). The diameters are numeric, not qualitative, and there is no sequence over time that would make it time-series.","d":2},{"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a parameter from a statistic?","o":["A parameter describes a sample and a statistic describes a population","A parameter describes a population and a statistic describes a sample","A parameter is always categorical and a statistic is always numeric","They are the same thing; the terms are interchangeable"],"a":1,"e":"A parameter is a numerical characteristic of an entire population, and a statistic is a numerical characteristic of a sample. Remember: P with P (parameter, population) and S with S (statistic, sample).","d":2},{"q":"A hospital records each patient's blood type (A, B, AB, or O). What is the level of measurement of this variable?","o":["Ordinal","Interval","Nominal","Ratio"],"a":2,"e":"Blood types are categories with no natural order or ranking, so the variable is nominal. Ordinal is tempting only because the labels look like a sequence, but type A is not 'higher' or 'lower' than type B.","d":1},{"q":"Which of the following is a qualitative (categorical) variable?","o":["The job title of each employee","The number of employees in each department","The annual salary of each employee","The age of each employee in years"],"a":0,"e":"Job title places each employee in a named category. Headcount, salary, and age are all numeric measurements or counts, so they are quantitative. Headcount is the tempting one because it is a count, but a count is still quantitative.","d":1},{"q":"A supplier's system exports purchase orders as JSON files. Each record has tagged fields such as vendor, items, and total, but the number and names of fields vary from order to order. How is this data best classified?","o":["Fully structured data stored in fixed rows and columns","Unstructured data","Time series data","Semi-structured data"],"a":3,"e":"Tagged but flexible formats such as JSON, XML, and email headers are semi-structured. Unstructured is the tempting choice because the layout varies, but unstructured data (free text, images, audio) has no tags or schema at all.","d":1},{"q":"A company has 8,000 customers and draws a sample of 400. What percentage of the population is in the sample?","o":["20%","5%","2%","50%"],"a":1,"e":"Sample size divided by population size: 400 / 8,000 = 0.05, or 5%. The tempting wrong answer, 20, comes from dividing the other way (8,000 / 400 = 20), which gives the number of customers each sampled customer represents, not a percentage.","d":1},{"q":"A retailer compares the third-quarter 2026 revenue of each of its 50 stores. Which data type best describes this data set?","o":["Time series data","Cross-sectional data","Longitudinal data","Unstructured data"],"a":1,"e":"Cross-sectional data measures many subjects (50 stores) at a single point or period in time. Time series is tempting because a quarter is mentioned, but time series follows the same subject across many periods, and here only one period is used.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following operations is meaningful for ordinal data but NOT for nominal data?","o":["Counting how many responses fall in each category","Finding the mode","Computing the mean of the categories' numeric codes","Ranking the responses and finding the median"],"a":3,"e":"Only ordinal data has a meaningful order, so ranking and the median work. Counting and the mode are valid for nominal data too. Averaging numeric codes is tempting but is not meaningful for either level because the gaps between ordinal categories are not equal.","d":2},{"q":"In a customer file, the variable Region is stored as 1 = North, 2 = South, 3 = East, 4 = West. An analyst reports a mean Region of 2.4. What is wrong with this?","o":["Region is nominal, so the numbers are only labels and their average has no meaning","Region is ordinal, so the median should be reported instead of the mean","Region is discrete, and means cannot be computed for discrete variables","Nothing is wrong; any numeric column can be averaged meaningfully"],"a":0,"e":"The codes are labels for unordered categories, so arithmetic on them is meaningless. Ordinal is tempting, but North/South/East/West have no order. Discrete variables can have means (for example, average defects per batch), so that option is also false.","d":2},{"q":"A call center records (1) the number of calls each agent handles per day and (2) the time, in seconds, that each caller waits on hold. Which classification is correct?","o":["(1) continuous, (2) discrete","Both are discrete","(1) discrete, (2) continuous","Both are continuous"],"a":2,"e":"Calls handled are counted in whole numbers, so they are discrete. Hold time is measured and can take any value within a range, so it is continuous. Both-discrete is tempting because both are numbers, but being numeric does not decide the type.","d":2},{"q":"A dashboard reports that the scrap rate rose from 2% to 5% after a supplier change. In the DIKW hierarchy, this statement is best described as:","o":["Data","Knowledge","Wisdom","Information"],"a":3,"e":"The statement organizes raw scrap counts into a rate and places them in context (before versus after a change), which is information. Knowledge would explain why scrap rose, such as the new material's tolerance, and wisdom would be the decision to act. Data would be the raw counts themselves.","d":2},{"q":"A manufacturer streams readings from 2,000 machine sensors every second and must act on them within moments. Which characteristic of big data does this most directly describe?","o":["Volume","Velocity","Variety","Veracity"],"a":1,"e":"Velocity is the speed at which data is generated and must be processed. Volume is tempting because 2,000 sensors produce a lot of data, but the emphasis here is the per-second arrival and need to respond quickly.","d":2},{"q":"An HR analyst records four variables: (i) employee loyalty tier (Bronze, Silver, Gold), (ii) calendar year of hire (for example 2019 or 2023), (iii) annual salary in dollars, and (iv) office location (Birmingham, Mobile, Huntsville). Which option lists their levels of measurement in order (i) to (iv)?","o":["Ordinal, interval, ratio, nominal","Ordinal, ratio, ratio, nominal","Nominal, interval, ratio, nominal","Ordinal, interval, interval, ordinal"],"a":0,"e":"Tiers are ordered categories (ordinal). Calendar years have meaningful differences but an arbitrary zero (year 0 is not 'no time'), so they are interval. Salary has a true zero (ratio). Locations are unordered (nominal). Treating the year as ratio is the tempting error.","d":3},{"q":"An HR director pulls the records of all 500 employees at the Mobile branch and finds an average tenure of 6.2 years. She presents it as a figure for the company's 10,000 employees overall. Relative to the company, the 6.2 years is a:","o":["Parameter, because every Mobile employee was measured","Parameter, because it is a mean","Statistic, because the Mobile branch is only a subset of the company","Census value for the whole company"],"a":2,"e":"Parameter versus statistic depends on the population of interest. For the company, Mobile is a sample (and a non-random one), so 6.2 is a statistic. It would be a parameter only if the question were about the Mobile branch alone.","d":3},{"q":"Eleven customers rate a product: 4 rated Poor, 1 rated Fair, 3 rated Good, and 3 rated Excellent. What is the median rating?","o":["Poor","Good","Fair","Excellent"],"a":1,"e":"Ordered from lowest to highest, the 6th of 11 ratings is the median. Cumulative counts: Poor 4, Fair 5, Good 8, so the 6th rating is Good. Poor is the mode (most frequent), which is the tempting wrong answer.","d":3},{"q":"A hospital reviews every one of its 12,000 patient records from 2026 and finds the average length of stay was 4.2 days. If the hospital's question concerns only its 2026 patients, the 4.2 days is best described as:","o":["A statistic, because it is an average","A sample estimate that has sampling error","An unstructured data value","A parameter, because it describes the entire population of interest"],"a":3,"e":"When every member of the population of interest is measured (a census), the summary value is a parameter. Being an average does not make something a statistic; a statistic is a summary of a sample.","d":3},{"q":"A survey collects each respondent's exact annual income, but the analyst recodes it into Low, Medium, and High income groups before storing it. What is the consequence?","o":["The variable drops from ratio to ordinal level, and the exact dollar amounts cannot be recovered","The variable becomes nominal because the group names are text","The variable stays ratio because it still originated as dollars","Nothing is lost as long as the groups have equal width"],"a":0,"e":"Recoding to ordered groups converts ratio data to ordinal, discarding information. Nominal is tempting because the labels are words, but Low < Medium < High is ordered, so ordinal is correct. You can always move down the hierarchy, never back up.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Nominal","Categories with no natural order"],["Ordinal","Ordered categories, unequal gaps"],["Interval","Equal gaps, no true zero"],["Ratio","Equal gaps with a true zero"],["Parameter","Number describing a population"],["Statistic","Number describing a sample"],["Discrete","Counted, whole-number values"],["Continuous","Measured, any value in a range"]],"traps":["Trap: Anything made of digits is quantitative (IDs, zip codes, jersey numbers). Truth: numbers used as labels are nominal; only measurements or counts are quantitative.","Trap: Celsius and Fahrenheit are ratio because they have a zero. Truth: their zero is arbitrary, so they are interval; only Kelvin has a true zero.","Trap: A number is a parameter or statistic by its formula alone. Truth: it depends on whether the group measured is the whole population of interest or a sample of it.","Trap: Discrete data cannot be averaged. Truth: discrete means countable values; a mean such as 2.4 defects per batch is perfectly valid.","Trap: Ordinal ratings can be averaged like any numbers. Truth: the gaps between ranks are not equal, so use the median or mode."],"mnemonic":"NOIR (French for black): Nominal = Names, Ordinal = Order, Interval = equal Intervals, Ratio = Real zero. Each level adds one power, and if 'twice as much' makes sense, it is ratio. Also: Parameter = Population, Statistic = Sample.","cram":["NOIR adds powers: names, order, equal gaps, true zero.","Celsius and Fahrenheit are interval; Kelvin, dollars, and counts are ratio.","IDs, zip codes, and jersey numbers are nominal, not quantitative.","Parameter describes a Population; statistic describes a Sample.","Discrete data is counted; continuous data is measured on a range.","Structured: rows/columns. Semi-structured: JSON/XML. Unstructured: text, images, audio.","DIKW: Data, Information, Knowledge, Wisdom; each level adds context.","Ordinal data supports the median and mode, not a meaningful mean."],"ytv":[{"title":"Nominal, Ordinal, Interval & Ratio Data: Simple Explanation With Examples","channel":"Grad Coach","id":"5Yh-9xdJzAs","why":"Plain-English walkthrough of the four levels of measurement with examples."},{"title":"Types of Data: Nominal, Ordinal, Interval/Ratio - Statistics Help","channel":"Dr Nic's Maths and Stats","id":"hZxnzfnt5v8","why":"Shows how to classify variables and why the level decides which statistics are valid."},{"title":"Sample vs. Population and Statistic vs. Parameter","channel":"Zach's Math Zone","id":"gpt7hkEnH5I","why":"Clarifies the population/sample and parameter/statistic distinctions with examples."}]},{"id":"u2","title":"Collecting Data & Avoiding Bias","day":1,"summary":"How to pick a sample, design a study that can show cause and effect, spot the common types of bias, clean messy data, and handle data ethically.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Why how you collect data matters","body":"<p>Even the most advanced analysis cannot rescue bad data. The saying is <strong>garbage in, garbage out</strong>. If the sample is lopsided, the conclusions will be too, no matter how many people you survey.</p><p>Two terms to know first:</p><ul><li>The <strong>sampling frame</strong> is the actual list or source you draw your sample from (a customer database, a payroll list). It should match the population as closely as possible.</li><li>A sample is <strong>representative</strong> when it mirrors the population on the characteristics that matter. <strong>Random selection</strong> is the best tool for getting a representative sample, because it gives every member a fair chance and removes the researcher's personal choices.</li></ul><p>Methods that use randomness are called <strong>probability sampling</strong>. Methods that rely on convenience or volunteers are <strong>non-probability sampling</strong>; they are cheaper and faster, but you cannot trust them to generalize to the population.</p>"},{"h":"2. Probability sampling methods","body":"<p>For each method, learn the <strong>trigger phrase</strong> you will see in an exam scenario.</p><ul><li><strong>Simple random sample (SRS):</strong> every member of the population has an equal chance, and every possible group of the same size is equally likely. <em>Trigger:</em> 'names drawn from a hat', 'random number generator picks 100 customer IDs'.</li><li><strong>Systematic sample:</strong> choose a random starting point, then take <strong>every kth</strong> member. The interval k = population size / sample size. <em>Trigger:</em> 'every 20th invoice', 'every 10th person to enter'. Danger: if the list has a repeating pattern that lines up with k, the sample is biased.</li><li><strong>Stratified sample:</strong> split the population into non-overlapping subgroups (<strong>strata</strong>) that share a characteristic (department, age group, region), then take a random sample <strong>from every stratum</strong>. Usually proportional to each stratum's size. <em>Trigger:</em> 'divide by..., then randomly select from each'. Guarantees every subgroup is represented.</li><li><strong>Cluster sample:</strong> split the population into naturally occurring groups (<strong>clusters</strong>: stores, schools, city blocks, shifts), randomly pick <strong>some clusters</strong>, and then survey <strong>everyone (or a sample) inside the chosen clusters</strong>. <em>Trigger:</em> 'randomly selected 5 of the 30 clinics and surveyed all patients there'. Cheap when the population is spread out.</li></ul><p><strong>Stratified vs cluster (a favorite exam trap):</strong></p><ul><li>Stratified: groups are <em>similar inside, different from each other</em>; you sample from <strong>all</strong> groups.</li><li>Cluster: each group is a <em>mini version of the whole population</em>; you sample <strong>only some</strong> groups.</li></ul>"},{"h":"3. Non-probability sampling methods","body":"<p>These are easy but risky, because the people who end up in the sample are not chosen by chance.</p><ul><li><strong>Convenience sample:</strong> whoever is easiest to reach. <em>Trigger:</em> 'surveyed the first 50 people she met', 'asked coworkers on his floor'.</li><li><strong>Voluntary response sample:</strong> people <strong>choose themselves</strong> to take part, usually by responding to an open invitation. <em>Trigger:</em> online poll, call-in vote, 'click here to rate us'. These attract people with strong opinions, so results are usually extreme.</li><li><strong>Snowball sample:</strong> early participants recruit more participants from their acquaintances. <em>Trigger:</em> 'each participant referred two friends'. Useful for hard-to-find groups (such as rare-disease patients), but the sample ends up as one connected social circle.</li><li><strong>Judgment (purposive) sample:</strong> the researcher hand-picks people thought to be typical or informative.</li><li><strong>Quota sample:</strong> the researcher fills set quotas (50 men, 50 women) but picks people non-randomly to fill them.</li></ul><p>Rule of thumb: if a human being chose who gets in, or the subjects chose themselves, it is non-probability and you cannot confidently generalize to the population.</p>"},{"h":"4. Observational studies vs designed experiments","body":"<p>The big question is: <strong>did the researcher impose a treatment?</strong></p><ul><li>In an <strong>observational study</strong> the researcher only watches and records what happens, without assigning anything. Example: comparing sales of stores that already chose to extend hours with those that did not. It can show an <strong>association</strong> but cannot prove <strong>cause and effect</strong>.</li><li>In a <strong>designed experiment</strong> the researcher deliberately applies a <strong>treatment</strong> to subjects and measures the response. Only a well-designed experiment can support a cause-and-effect conclusion.</li></ul><p><strong>Ingredients of a good experiment:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Control group:</strong> a comparison group that does not get the new treatment (or gets the current standard), so you know what would have happened anyway.</li><li><strong>Random assignment:</strong> subjects are placed in groups by chance. This spreads out all the other factors, even unknown ones, so the groups start out alike.</li><li><strong>Placebo:</strong> a fake treatment that looks real, given to the control group, so the 'feeling of being treated' does not skew results.</li><li><strong>Blinding:</strong> keeping people from knowing who got what. <em>Single-blind</em>: subjects do not know. <em>Double-blind</em>: neither the subjects nor the people measuring results know. This prevents expectations from changing behavior or measurements.</li><li><strong>Replication:</strong> enough subjects (and repeating the study) so that chance differences average out.</li></ul><p><strong>Confounding (lurking) variable:</strong> a third factor related to both the treatment and the outcome that makes it impossible to tell which one caused the effect. Classic example: ice cream sales and drownings both rise in summer, because hot weather drives both; ice cream does not cause drowning. Random assignment is the main defense against confounding.</p><p>Memory aid: <strong>Correlation is not causation.</strong> Random assignment lets you say 'caused by'.</p>"},{"h":"5. Types of bias","body":"<p><strong>Bias</strong> is a systematic error that pushes results in one direction. A bigger sample does not fix it. Learn to name it from the scenario:</p><ul><li><strong>Selection bias:</strong> the way subjects get into the study makes the group differ from the population.</li><li><strong>Sampling bias (undercoverage):</strong> the method leaves out part of the population (surveying by landline phone misses younger people).</li><li><strong>Nonresponse bias:</strong> many chosen people do not respond, and those who do differ from those who do not (a mailed survey with a 10 percent return rate).</li><li><strong>Voluntary response bias:</strong> people who self-select to respond hold stronger opinions than average.</li><li><strong>Response bias:</strong> people give inaccurate answers, for example to look good (social desirability) or because of how the question is worded.</li><li><strong>Leading question:</strong> wording that pushes toward one answer ('Don't you agree our excellent service is the best?').</li><li><strong>Survivorship bias:</strong> looking only at the winners that 'survived' and ignoring the failures that dropped out (studying only the successful startups still operating).</li><li><strong>Confirmation bias:</strong> the analyst seeks, notices and remembers evidence that supports what they already believe, and ignores the rest.</li><li><strong>Measurement bias:</strong> the instrument or process records values wrongly in a consistent direction (a scale that reads 2 pounds too high).</li><li><strong>Recall bias:</strong> people misremember past events, especially in surveys asking about the distant past.</li></ul><p>Exam tip: find <em>who</em> is missing or <em>how</em> the information was gathered, and match the keyword (volunteer, did not respond, wording, only the winners, instrument error).</p>"},{"h":"6. Data quality and cleaning","body":"<p>Real-world data is messy. Before analysis, check it against these <strong>data quality dimensions</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> values are correct and match reality.</li><li><strong>Completeness:</strong> required values are present (no blank fields).</li><li><strong>Consistency:</strong> the same thing is recorded the same way everywhere (AL, Ala. and Alabama; date formats).</li><li><strong>Validity:</strong> values follow the allowed rules and formats (age is not negative, a ZIP code has five digits).</li><li><strong>Timeliness:</strong> data is current enough for the decision.</li><li><strong>Uniqueness:</strong> each real-world item appears only once (no duplicates).</li></ul><p><strong>Common cleaning steps:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Missing values:</strong> find out why they are missing. Options: delete the row (if few are missing), fill in (<em>impute</em>) with the mean or median (median if skewed), use a flagged value like 'Unknown', or collect the data again. Never silently fill in a guess without recording it.</li><li><strong>Duplicates:</strong> identify and remove repeated records, keeping one.</li><li><strong>Outliers:</strong> extreme values. <strong>Investigate first</strong>: if it is a typo or an impossible value, correct or remove it; if it is real, keep it (and consider reporting the median).</li><li><strong>Standardize:</strong> make formats, units, spellings and capitalization the same.</li><li><strong>Fix data types and errors:</strong> numbers stored as text, misspellings, wrong units.</li></ul><p>Document every change so that the process can be repeated and audited.</p>"},{"h":"7. Data ethics and privacy basics","body":"<p>Having data does not mean you may use it however you like. Core principles:</p><ul><li><strong>Informed consent:</strong> people should know what data is collected, why, and how it will be used, and agree to it.</li><li><strong>Privacy and confidentiality:</strong> protect personal information (names, addresses, health, financial data). Share only with those who need it.</li><li><strong>Data minimization and purpose limitation:</strong> collect only what you need, and use it only for the purpose you stated.</li><li><strong>Anonymization / de-identification:</strong> remove or mask identifying details so individuals cannot be picked out. Be careful: combining several 'harmless' fields (birth date, ZIP, gender) can re-identify a person.</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> store data safely, limit access, and encrypt it.</li><li><strong>Transparency and honesty:</strong> report results fairly, including limitations, and do not cherry-pick or manipulate to push a conclusion.</li><li><strong>Fairness:</strong> watch for biased data leading to unfair decisions (for example, a hiring model trained on past biased decisions).</li></ul><p>Be aware that laws apply: HIPAA (U.S. health information), GDPR (European Union personal data), and CCPA (California consumers). You do not need legal detail, but you should recognize that selling or sharing personal data without consent is an ethical and often legal violation.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["How data is collected matters more than how fancy the analysis is, because bad data leads to bad conclusions no matter what technique you use.","In a simple random sample every member of the population has an equal chance of being chosen, which makes it the gold standard for a representative sample.","A systematic sample picks a random starting point and then takes every kth member, where k is the population size divided by the sample size.","A stratified sample divides the population into subgroups and takes a random sample from every subgroup, while a cluster sample randomly chooses whole groups and surveys everyone in the chosen groups.","Convenience, voluntary response and snowball samples are non probability methods, so their results usually cannot be generalized to the whole population.","A voluntary response sample attracts people with strong opinions, such as an online poll, and therefore tends to give misleading results.","An observational study only records what happens and can show association, while a designed experiment applies a treatment and is the only way to support a cause and effect claim.","A good experiment uses a control group, random assignment, a placebo when appropriate, and blinding so that expectations do not change the results.","A confounding or lurking variable influences both the treatment and the outcome, which makes it hard to know what really caused the effect. Random assignment is the best defense.","Common biases include selection bias, nonresponse bias, response bias from leading questions, voluntary response bias, survivorship bias, confirmation bias, measurement bias and recall bias.","Data quality is judged on accuracy, completeness, consistency, validity, timeliness and uniqueness, and cleaning involves handling missing values, removing duplicates, investigating outliers and standardizing formats.","Ethical data use requires informed consent, privacy protection, collecting only what you need, using data only for the stated purpose, and keeping it secure."],"examples":[{"q":"A warehouse has 1,200 invoices numbered 1 to 1,200 and an auditor wants a systematic sample of 60 invoices. She randomly chooses a starting number of 7. Which invoices are selected, and what is the last one?","steps":["Compute the sampling interval: k = population size / sample size = 1,200 / 60 = 20.","Start at the random starting point: invoice 7.","Then add 20 each time: 7, 27, 47, 67, 87, and so on.","The 60th invoice is 7 + (59 x 20) = 7 + 1,180 = 1,187.","Check: 1,187 is within 1 to 1,200, so the sample fits inside the list."],"answer":"Interval k = 20; invoices 7, 27, 47, ... The last (60th) invoice is number 1,187."},{"q":"A company has 5,000 employees: 2,000 in production, 1,500 in administration, 1,000 in sales, and 500 in management. An HR analyst wants a proportional stratified sample of 200 employees. How many should be randomly selected from each department?","steps":["Find the sampling fraction: 200 / 5,000 = 0.04 (4 percent).","Production: 2,000 x 0.04 = 80.","Administration: 1,500 x 0.04 = 60.","Sales: 1,000 x 0.04 = 40.","Management: 500 x 0.04 = 20.","Check: 80 + 60 + 40 + 20 = 200, matching the required sample size."],"answer":"Production 80, Administration 60, Sales 40, Management 20 (total 200)."},{"q":"A customer data set records order amounts (in dollars) for five orders as 10, 12, 14, (missing), 20. The analyst decides to fill the missing value with the mean of the known values. What value is used, and what is the mean of all five after filling?","steps":["Add the four known values: 10 + 12 + 14 + 20 = 56.","Divide by 4: 56 / 4 = 14, so the missing value is imputed as 14.","The completed data set is 10, 12, 14, 14, 20.","New mean = (10 + 12 + 14 + 14 + 20) / 5 = 70 / 5 = 14.","Notice that mean imputation does not change the mean, but it makes the data look less spread out than it really might be, so the choice should be documented."],"answer":"Fill with 14. The mean of the five values is still 14."}],"videos":[{"title":"Sampling methods: simple random, stratified, cluster, systematic","query":"sampling methods simple random stratified cluster systematic explained"},{"title":"Observational studies vs experiments, confounding variables","query":"Khan Academy observational study vs experiment confounding variable"},{"title":"Types of bias in surveys and data","query":"types of bias in statistics selection nonresponse voluntary response survivorship"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Simple random sample (SRS)","b":"Every member of the population has an equal chance of selection (names from a hat, random number generator)."},{"f":"Systematic sampling","b":"Pick a random start, then take every kth member, where k = population size / sample size."},{"f":"Stratified sampling","b":"Split the population into subgroups (strata) and randomly sample from EVERY subgroup."},{"f":"Cluster sampling","b":"Randomly pick whole natural groups (clusters), then survey everyone (or a sample) in the chosen clusters."},{"f":"Stratified vs cluster: how to tell","b":"Stratified samples from ALL groups (groups differ from each other). Cluster samples only SOME groups (each group is a mini population)."},{"f":"Convenience sampling","b":"Using whoever is easiest to reach (first people you meet). Non-probability and prone to bias."},{"f":"Voluntary response sample","b":"Subjects choose themselves to respond (online polls, call-in votes). Attracts strong opinions, so it is biased."},{"f":"Snowball sampling","b":"Participants recruit more participants from their acquaintances. Good for hard-to-reach groups, but not representative."},{"f":"Observational study vs experiment","b":"Observational: researcher only observes, shows association. Experiment: researcher assigns a treatment, can show cause and effect."},{"f":"Designed experiment with random assignment","b":"Subjects are put into treatment and control groups by chance, which balances out confounders so effects can be attributed to the treatment."},{"f":"Control group and placebo","b":"Control group gets no treatment (or the standard one) for comparison. A placebo is a fake treatment that looks real."},{"f":"Blinding (single vs double)","b":"Single-blind: subjects do not know their group. Double-blind: neither subjects nor those measuring outcomes know."},{"f":"Confounding (lurking) variable","b":"A hidden third variable tied to both the treatment and the outcome that muddles cause and effect (hot weather drives both ice cream sales and drownings)."},{"f":"Selection bias and sampling bias","b":"The way people get into the sample (or the sampling method leaving out part of the population) makes the sample unrepresentative."},{"f":"Nonresponse bias","b":"Chosen people do not respond, and those who do differ from those who do not (low mail survey return rates)."},{"f":"Response bias and leading questions","b":"Respondents give inaccurate or pressured answers, often because of wording that pushes a particular answer."},{"f":"Survivorship bias","b":"Studying only the winners that survived and ignoring the failures, leading to false conclusions about what causes success."},{"f":"Confirmation bias","b":"Favoring evidence that supports what you already believe and ignoring evidence that contradicts it."},{"f":"Measurement bias and recall bias","b":"Measurement bias: a faulty tool or process records values wrongly in a consistent direction. Recall bias: people misremember past events."},{"f":"Data quality dimensions","b":"Accuracy, completeness, consistency, validity, timeliness, and uniqueness (no duplicates)."},{"f":"Data cleaning basics","b":"Handle missing values (delete, impute, flag), remove duplicates, investigate outliers before removing, standardize formats, and document changes."},{"f":"Data ethics essentials","b":"Informed consent, privacy and confidentiality, collect only what is needed, use only for the stated purpose, secure storage, and honest reporting."}],"questions":[{"q":"A hospital system has 30 clinics. An analyst randomly selects 5 of the clinics and surveys every patient treated at those 5 clinics. Which sampling method is this?","o":["Stratified sampling","Systematic sampling","Cluster sampling","Convenience sampling"],"a":2,"e":"Whole groups (clinics) were randomly chosen and everyone inside them was surveyed, which is cluster sampling. Stratified is tempting because there are groups, but stratified sampling takes a random sample from every group, not just a few chosen groups.","d":2},{"q":"A retail manager surveys every 25th customer who enters the store, after randomly picking which of the first 25 customers to start with. Which sampling method is this?","o":["Systematic sampling","Simple random sampling","Cluster sampling","Snowball sampling"],"a":0,"e":"A random start followed by a fixed interval (every 25th customer) is the definition of systematic sampling. It is not a simple random sample because not every possible group of customers has an equal chance of being selected.","d":2},{"q":"A company's workforce is 60 percent hourly, 30 percent salaried, and 10 percent management. To survey 100 employees, an analyst randomly selects 60 hourly, 30 salaried, and 10 management employees. Which sampling method is used?","o":["Cluster sampling","Systematic sampling","Convenience sampling","Stratified sampling"],"a":3,"e":"The population was divided into non-overlapping subgroups and a random sample was taken from each in proportion to its size, which is proportional stratified sampling.","d":2},{"q":"A news website posts a poll asking readers whether they support a new tax. About 3,000 readers choose to vote, and 85 percent oppose it. What is the biggest weakness of this result?","o":["The sample is too small to be useful","Voluntary response bias, because people with strong opinions choose to participate","Survivorship bias, because only winning opinions were counted","Measurement bias from a faulty instrument"],"a":1,"e":"Readers chose themselves to respond, so the sample reflects people motivated to vote rather than the whole population. A large sample size does not fix bias. 3,000 responses is not small.","d":2},{"q":"A student researcher surveys the first 40 people she meets outside the campus library about their study habits. Which sampling method is this?","o":["Simple random sampling","Stratified sampling","Cluster sampling","Convenience sampling"],"a":3,"e":"She chose whoever was easiest to reach, which is convenience sampling. Nothing in the process was random.","d":2},{"q":"Researchers studying a rare medical condition ask each patient they find to refer other patients they know. Which sampling method is this?","o":["Snowball sampling","Systematic sampling","Stratified sampling","Simple random sampling"],"a":0,"e":"Participants recruiting further participants is snowball sampling, which is common for hard-to-reach populations but does not produce a random, representative sample.","d":2},{"q":"A firm compares the productivity of employees who chose to use standing desks with those who did not and finds the standing-desk group is 8 percent more productive. Which conclusion is justified?","o":["Standing desks cause an 8 percent increase in productivity","This is an observational study, so it shows an association but cannot prove cause and effect","This is a designed experiment because there are two groups","The result is invalid because the groups must be exactly the same size"],"a":1,"e":"Employees chose their own desks and nothing was assigned by the researcher, so it is an observational study. Motivated employees may be both more likely to choose standing desks and more productive (a confounder). Group sizes do not need to match.","d":2},{"q":"What is the main purpose of randomly assigning subjects to the treatment and control groups in an experiment?","o":["To make sure subjects know which group they belong to","To make the two groups exactly equal in size","To spread known and unknown confounding factors evenly so that differences can be attributed to the treatment","To guarantee that the treatment will work"],"a":2,"e":"Random assignment makes the groups similar at the start on every characteristic, measured or not, so a difference in outcomes is more likely caused by the treatment. It does not guarantee the treatment works and says nothing about group sizes.","d":2},{"q":"In a double-blind study of a new training program, who does NOT know which participants received the new program?","o":["Both the participants and the people who measure the outcomes","Only the participants","Only the researchers measuring outcomes","Only the lead researcher, who designed the study"],"a":0,"e":"In a double-blind study both the participants and the people evaluating outcomes are kept unaware of group assignments, which prevents expectations from affecting behavior or measurement. If only participants are unaware, it is single-blind.","d":2},{"q":"A city finds that in the summer both ice cream sales and drownings increase. Which explanation best fits the data?","o":["Eating ice cream causes people to drown","Drowning causes people to buy more ice cream","The pattern is due to random sampling error only","A lurking variable, hot weather, increases both ice cream sales and swimming"],"a":3,"e":"Hot weather is a confounding (lurking) variable that influences both measures. The two are associated but neither causes the other.","d":2},{"q":"A consultant studies the 20 most successful companies still in business and concludes that their shared habits cause success, without examining the many failed companies that had the same habits. What type of bias is this?","o":["Nonresponse bias","Survivorship bias","Recall bias","Leading question bias"],"a":1,"e":"Looking only at the successes that survived, and ignoring the failures that dropped out of view, is survivorship bias. The habits might be equally common among failed companies.","d":2},{"q":"A company mails a satisfaction survey to 2,000 randomly selected customers. Only 12 percent reply, and most of those who reply are either very happy or very angry. What is the most likely problem?","o":["Stratification error","Response bias caused by a leading question","Nonresponse bias","Confounding"],"a":2,"e":"Customers were chosen randomly, but most did not respond and the few who did are probably unlike the rest. That is nonresponse bias. There is no evidence of a leading question.","d":2},{"q":"Which survey question is most likely to produce biased answers because of leading wording?","o":["Don't you agree that our award-winning support team is the best in the industry?","How satisfied are you with our support team?","How would you rate our support team on a scale from 1 to 5?","What, if anything, could we improve about our support?"],"a":0,"e":"The first question pushes respondents toward agreeing by praising the team and asking for agreement. The other three are neutral.","d":2},{"q":"A data set records dates as 03/04/2026 in some rows and 2026-03-04 in others, and lists the state as AL, Ala., and Alabama. Which data quality dimension is the main problem?","o":["Completeness","Timeliness","Uniqueness","Consistency"],"a":3,"e":"The same information is recorded in different formats, which is a consistency problem. The values may be correct (accuracy) and nothing is missing (completeness), but they must be standardized before analysis.","d":2},{"q":"Customers gave their email addresses so they could receive electronic receipts. The company later sells the email list to third-party advertisers without telling the customers. Which principle has been violated?","o":["Data redundancy","Informed consent and use of data only for the stated purpose","Systematic sampling","Data timeliness"],"a":1,"e":"Customers agreed to share their email for receipts only. Selling it for another purpose without their knowledge violates informed consent, purpose limitation, and privacy expectations.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst is convinced a new promotion works. When reviewing results, she reports only the metrics that improved and ignores the ones that did not. This is an example of:","o":["Survivorship bias","Recall bias","Confirmation bias","Measurement bias"],"a":2,"e":"Favoring evidence that supports an existing belief and discarding contrary evidence is confirmation bias. Survivorship bias is about ignoring cases that dropped out of view, not about ignoring unfavorable metrics.","d":2},{"q":"Which sampling method gives every member of the population an equal chance of being selected?","o":["Convenience sampling","Quota sampling","Judgment sampling","Simple random sampling"],"a":3,"e":"In simple random sampling every member (and every possible sample of size n) is equally likely to be chosen. Convenience, quota, and judgment sampling are nonprobability methods in which selection depends on ease or on the researcher's choices.","d":1},{"q":"Researchers compare the absenteeism of employees who report exercising regularly with those who do not, using existing HR and survey records, without assigning anyone to a group. This is a(n):","o":["Randomized controlled experiment","Observational study","Double-blind experiment","Census"],"a":1,"e":"No treatment was assigned; the researchers only observed existing groups, so this is an observational study. It can show association but not cause and effect. A census is tempting only because all records may be used, but a census describes the sampling scope, not whether treatments were assigned.","d":1},{"q":"Why does a clinical trial give the control group a placebo?","o":["So improvement caused only by believing one is being treated is not credited to the drug","To increase the sample size","To make sure every participant is a volunteer","To remove the need for random assignment"],"a":0,"e":"A placebo lets researchers separate the drug's real effect from the placebo effect, because both groups think they may be treated. Placebos do not replace random assignment; the two work together.","d":1},{"q":"A telephone survey about internet shopping dials only landline numbers, so households that use only mobile phones have no chance of being selected. This is an example of:","o":["Non-response bias","Response bias","Undercoverage (coverage) bias","Survivorship bias"],"a":2,"e":"Mobile-only households are missing from the sampling frame, so they never had a chance to be selected: undercoverage. Non-response bias is the tempting choice, but it applies to people who were selected and did not answer.","d":1},{"q":"A company has 2,000 employees: 1,200 in production, 500 in the office, and 300 in sales. Using proportional stratified sampling with a total sample of 200, how many office employees should be selected?","o":["67","25","50","100"],"a":2,"e":"Office employees are 500 / 2,000 = 25% of the workforce, so they get 25% of the sample: 0.25 x 200 = 50. Selecting 67 would be equal allocation (200 / 3), which is not proportional, and 25 mistakes the percentage for a count.","d":2},{"q":"An auditor wants a systematic sample of 40 invoices from a batch of 1,200. What sampling interval k should be used?","o":["30","40","20","1,160"],"a":0,"e":"The interval is population size divided by sample size: 1,200 / 40 = 30, so after a random start the auditor takes every 30th invoice. Using 40 confuses the sample size with the interval.","d":2},{"q":"A retail chain finds that stores with larger advertising budgets have higher sales. Further analysis shows the large-budget stores are mostly in high-income neighborhoods. Neighborhood income is best described as a:","o":["Dependent (response) variable","Confounding variable","Control group","Sampling error"],"a":1,"e":"Income is related to both advertising budget and sales, so it can create or exaggerate an apparent link: a confounding (lurking) variable. Sales is the dependent variable, which is the tempting wrong option.","d":2},{"q":"A manager personally interviews each direct report and asks, 'Do you always follow the safety procedures?' Nearly everyone says yes, yet audits show frequent violations. Which bias most likely explains this?","o":["Undercoverage bias","Non-response bias","Selection bias in the sampling frame","Response (social desirability) bias"],"a":3,"e":"Employees gave the answer they thought the boss wanted, which is a response bias caused by the question context. Nothing suggests people were left out of the frame or refused to answer.","d":2},{"q":"In a customer database, 30 percent of records have a blank phone number field. Which data quality dimension is most directly affected?","o":["Completeness","Consistency","Timeliness","Uniqueness"],"a":0,"e":"Missing values are a completeness problem. Consistency would concern conflicting formats or values for the same item, timeliness concerns how current the data is, and uniqueness concerns duplicate records.","d":2},{"q":"An HR survey promises 'anonymous' responses, but each survey link is secretly tied to the employee's ID so managers could see who said what. Which description fits this survey?","o":["It is anonymous because names are not shown in the final report","It is anonymous as long as only managers can see the links","It is at best confidential, not anonymous, so promising anonymity is misleading","It is public data and needs no consent"],"a":2,"e":"Anonymous means responses cannot be linked to individuals by anyone. When identities can be traced, even if restricted to a few people, the data is at best confidential, and promising anonymity violates honesty and informed consent.","d":2},{"q":"A national retailer with 400 stores in 40 cities wants to cut audit travel costs by randomly choosing 6 cities and auditing every store in them. Compared with a stratified sample of the same size, what is the main statistical drawback of this cluster approach?","o":["Some stores have no chance of selection, so it is not a probability sample","The chosen clusters may not represent all cities, so estimates can be less precise","It requires a list of every store before any cities can be chosen, unlike stratified sampling","It always introduces response bias"],"a":1,"e":"Cluster sampling saves cost but, because whole clusters are chosen, similar stores are grouped together and a few unrepresentative clusters can skew results. Every store still has a chance of selection, and cluster sampling needs only a list of clusters, so the other options are false.","d":3},{"q":"An e-commerce team tests a new checkout page by showing it Monday through Wednesday and the old page Thursday through Sunday, then compares conversion rates. Why is the comparison unreliable?","o":["The sample size is too small for any experiment","A placebo page was not used","Experiments cannot be run on websites","Assignment was not random, so day of the week is confounded with page version"],"a":3,"e":"Shoppers behave differently on weekdays and weekends, so any difference could come from the days rather than the page. Randomly assigning each visitor to a page would spread those factors evenly across both versions.","d":3},{"q":"A study finds that firms spending more on employee training have higher profits, and an executive concludes that training causes profits to rise. Which alternative explanation illustrates reverse causation?","o":["Both training and profits are higher in firms with strong management","The sample of firms was too small","Profitable firms have more money available, so they spend more on training","Profits are measured in dollars, so the relationship is spurious"],"a":2,"e":"Reverse causation means the supposed effect is actually the cause: profits enable training spending. The strong-management option is the tempting one, but that describes a common cause (confounding), not reverse direction.","d":3},{"q":"Productivity at a plant rises after researchers announce they will be observing workers, regardless of which of several lighting changes they make. This is best explained by the:","o":["Hawthorne effect","Placebo effect","Survivorship bias","Simpson's paradox"],"a":0,"e":"The Hawthorne effect is a change in behavior caused by knowing one is being observed. The placebo effect is tempting, but it comes from believing a treatment works, not from being watched.","d":3},{"q":"A furnace sensor log shows readings near 450 degrees F, except for one reading of 4,500 degrees F. The process engineer says the furnace cannot reach that temperature and the value is likely a misplaced decimal. What is the best action?","o":["Keep it unchanged because removing any data introduces bias","Investigate the source, correct or exclude the value if confirmed as an error, and document the decision","Replace it with the mean without telling anyone","Discard the entire sensor's history"],"a":1,"e":"A value that is physically impossible is probably an entry or transmission error. Best practice is to verify, fix or exclude it, and record what was done. Silent replacement hides the change, and discarding all of the sensor's data is excessive.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Simple random","Every member equally likely chosen"],["Systematic","Every kth member after random start"],["Stratified","Random picks from every subgroup"],["Cluster","Randomly chosen groups, all members surveyed"],["Convenience","Easiest-to-reach people"],["Snowball","Participants recruit more participants"],["Placebo","Fake treatment given to controls"],["Confounder","Hidden variable affecting both variables"]],"traps":["Trap: A bigger sample fixes a biased sampling method. Truth: bias does not shrink with size; only better sampling design removes it.","Trap: Stratified and cluster sampling are the same. Truth: stratified samples from every group; cluster randomly selects whole groups and surveys all (or many) members.","Trap: A strong pattern in an observational study proves cause and effect. Truth: only randomized experiments support causal claims; otherwise confounders or reverse causation may explain it.","Trap: Undercoverage and non-response bias are the same. Truth: undercoverage means some people were never in the frame; non-response means selected people did not respond.","Trap: Random sampling and random assignment do the same job. Truth: random sampling lets you generalize to the population; random assignment lets you attribute cause within an experiment."],"mnemonic":"Random SAMPLING lets you GENERALIZE; random ASSIGNMENT lets you ATTRIBUTE cause. And for sampling: STRATIFIED = slice the population into groups and sample from EVERY slice; CLUSTER = pick whole chunks and sample only SOME chunks.","cram":["Random sampling supports generalizing; random assignment supports cause and effect.","Stratified: sample from every group. Cluster: sample whole randomly chosen groups.","Systematic sampling interval k = population size divided by sample size.","Voluntary response and convenience samples are nonrandom and usually biased.","Undercoverage: never in the frame. Non-response: selected but did not answer.","Double-blind: neither participants nor evaluators know who gets the treatment.","Only randomized experiments establish causation; observational studies show association.","Quality dimensions: accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, validity, uniqueness."],"ytv":[{"title":"Sampling: Simple Random, Convenience, systematic, cluster, stratified - Statistics Help","channel":"Dr Nic's Maths and Stats","id":"be9e-Q-jC-0","why":"Compares the main sampling methods side by side with clear examples."},{"title":"Types of Sampling Bias (Representation, Nonresponse, and Voluntary bias)","channel":"Screened-Instructor","id":"_GAkMyvD9Xo","why":"Explains how undercoverage, non-response, and voluntary response bias arise."},{"title":"Types of Sampling Bias-Undercoverage Bias/Self-Selection Bias/Survivorship Bias/Non Response Bias","channel":"Management Tutorials","id":"4OomQ4iUtNc","why":"Defines several sampling biases with business-style examples."}]},{"id":"u3","title":"Describing Data","day":2,"summary":"Summarize a data set with measures of center and spread, quartiles and outlier fences, percentiles, the coefficient of variation, skewness, and weighted means.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Measures of center: mean, median, mode","body":"<p>A <strong>measure of center</strong> gives a single number for the 'typical' value of a data set.</p><ul><li><strong>Mean</strong> (the average): add all the values and divide by how many there are. <code>mean = (sum of values) / n</code>. Uses every value, so it is sensitive to extremes.</li><li><strong>Median</strong>: the middle value after sorting from smallest to largest. If there is an even number of values, average the two middle ones. Half the data lies below it and half above it. It is <em>resistant</em> to outliers.</li><li><strong>Mode</strong>: the value that occurs most often. A data set can have no mode, one mode, or several (<em>bimodal</em> for two). It is the only measure that works for categorical data.</li></ul><p><strong>Quick example:</strong> daily defect counts 3, 7, 7, 9, 14. Mean = (3 + 7 + 7 + 9 + 14) / 5 = 40 / 5 = 8. Median = middle of the sorted list = 7. Mode = 7 (appears twice).</p><p><strong>Which one to use?</strong></p><ul><li>Symmetric numeric data with no outliers: <strong>mean</strong>.</li><li>Skewed data or data with outliers (salaries, home prices, wait times): <strong>median</strong>.</li><li>Categorical (nominal) data, or finding the most popular choice: <strong>mode</strong>.</li><li>Ordinal data: median or mode.</li></ul><p>Business example: if nine employees earn about 50,000 dollars and the CEO earns 2,000,000 dollars, the mean salary is about 245,000 dollars, which describes nobody. The median (50,000) is a far better 'typical' salary.</p>"},{"h":"2. Skewness and how the mean and median compare","body":"<p><strong>Skewness</strong> describes which way a distribution leans. The direction is named for the <strong>long tail</strong>, not for where the bulk of data sits.</p><ul><li><strong>Symmetric:</strong> both sides mirror each other. Mean = median (= mode when there is one peak).</li><li><strong>Right-skewed (positively skewed):</strong> long tail stretching to the right (high values). Examples: income, home prices, time to resolve a ticket. The extreme high values pull the mean up: <strong>mean &gt; median &gt; mode</strong>.</li><li><strong>Left-skewed (negatively skewed):</strong> long tail to the left (low values). Example: age at retirement, scores on an easy exam. The low values pull the mean down: <strong>mean &lt; median &lt; mode</strong>.</li></ul><p>Memory trick: <strong>the mean chases the tail.</strong> The mean is dragged toward the tail, the median stays closer to the bulk of the data.</p><p>So if a report says the mean wait time is 18 minutes and the median is 11 minutes, you can tell right away that the distribution is right-skewed: a few very long waits are pulling the mean up.</p><p><strong>Effect of an outlier:</strong> adding one extreme value changes the mean a lot, the range and standard deviation a lot, but the median only a little (or not at all). For data 10, 12, 13, 15, the mean is 12.5 and the median is 12.5. Add 100 and the mean jumps to 30 but the median is 13.</p>"},{"h":"3. Measures of spread: range, variance, standard deviation","body":"<p>Two data sets can have the same mean but very different consistency. <strong>Spread</strong> (variability) tells you how much values differ from each other.</p><ul><li><strong>Range</strong> = maximum minus minimum. Simple, but it depends only on the two extreme values.</li><li><strong>Variance</strong>: the average squared distance of the values from the mean.</li><li><strong>Standard deviation (SD)</strong>: the square root of the variance. It is in the <strong>same units as the data</strong>, so it is the most commonly used measure of spread. Roughly, it is the 'typical distance from the mean'.</li></ul><p><strong>Steps to compute (always the same):</strong></p><ol><li>Find the mean.</li><li>Subtract the mean from each value (the <em>deviation</em>). These add up to zero.</li><li>Square each deviation (so negatives do not cancel).</li><li>Add the squares (the <em>sum of squares</em>).</li><li>Divide: by <strong>N</strong> for a population, or by <strong>n minus 1</strong> for a sample.</li><li>For the standard deviation, take the square root.</li></ol><p><strong>Population vs sample:</strong></p><ul><li>Population variance: <code>sigma squared = sum of (x - mu)^2 / N</code></li><li>Sample variance: <code>s squared = sum of (x - x-bar)^2 / (n - 1)</code></li></ul><p><strong>Why n minus 1?</strong> A sample's values cluster closer to the sample's own mean than to the true population mean, so dividing by n would systematically underestimate the population's variability. Dividing by n - 1 corrects this. Use N only when your data is the <em>entire</em> population.</p><p>Units tip: variance is in <strong>squared units</strong> (square minutes), which is why people prefer SD (minutes). If all values are identical, the SD is 0. A bigger SD means more spread.</p><p><strong>Empirical rule</strong> (for roughly bell-shaped data): about <strong>68 percent</strong> of values fall within 1 SD of the mean, <strong>95 percent</strong> within 2 SDs, and <strong>99.7 percent</strong> within 3 SDs. A <strong>z-score</strong> tells how many SDs a value is from the mean: <code>z = (x - mean) / SD</code>. Values beyond z of 2 or 3 in size are unusual.</p>"},{"h":"4. Quartiles, IQR, five-number summary, and outlier fences","body":"<p>Quartiles cut sorted data into four equal parts.</p><ul><li><strong>Q1</strong> (first quartile, 25th percentile): the median of the lower half.</li><li><strong>Q2</strong> = the median (50th percentile).</li><li><strong>Q3</strong> (third quartile, 75th percentile): the median of the upper half.</li></ul><p><strong>How to find them by hand:</strong> sort the data, find the median, split into a lower half and an upper half (if n is odd, leave the median itself out of both halves), then take the median of each half. Software may use slightly different methods, so small differences are normal.</p><p><strong>Interquartile range (IQR) = Q3 minus Q1.</strong> It is the spread of the middle 50 percent of the data and is resistant to outliers.</p><p><strong>Five-number summary:</strong> Minimum, Q1, Median, Q3, Maximum. It is the information behind a box plot.</p><p><strong>Outlier fences (1.5 x IQR rule):</strong></p><ul><li>Lower fence = Q1 - 1.5 x IQR</li><li>Upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 x IQR</li><li>Any value below the lower fence or above the upper fence is flagged as a <strong>potential outlier</strong>.</li></ul><p>Flagged does not mean wrong. Investigate: is it a typo, a measurement error, or a real extreme case worth studying?</p><p><strong>Percentiles:</strong> the pth percentile is the value below which about p percent of the data fall. Percentile rank of a value = (number of values below it / total number of values) x 100. If 40 of 50 scores are below 78, then 78 is at the 80th percentile. A 90th percentile means you did better than about 90 percent of the group, <em>not</em> that you scored 90 percent.</p>"},{"h":"5. Worked example: a full standard deviation calculation","body":"<p>Five technicians' repair times (in hours) are: 5, 7, 8, 9, 11. Treat it first as a <strong>sample</strong>.</p><ol><li><strong>Mean:</strong> (5 + 7 + 8 + 9 + 11) / 5 = 40 / 5 = 8.</li><li><strong>Deviations (x minus 8):</strong> -3, -1, 0, 1, 3. (They sum to 0, a good check.)</li><li><strong>Squared deviations:</strong> 9, 1, 0, 1, 9.</li><li><strong>Sum of squares:</strong> 9 + 1 + 0 + 1 + 9 = 20.</li><li><strong>Sample variance:</strong> 20 / (5 - 1) = 20 / 4 = 5 (hours squared).</li><li><strong>Sample SD:</strong> square root of 5 = about 2.24 hours.</li></ol><p>If these five were the <em>entire population</em> instead, divide by N = 5: variance = 20 / 5 = 4 and SD = 2 hours. Notice the sample SD (2.24) is a little larger than the population SD (2.00) because of the n minus 1 correction.</p><p>Interpretation: repair times typically differ from the 8-hour average by about 2 hours.</p>"},{"h":"6. Coefficient of variation","body":"<p>The standard deviation depends on the size and units of the data. A standard deviation of 10 is huge for packages weighing 20 pounds but tiny for trucks weighing 20,000 pounds. To compare variability <strong>fairly</strong> across data sets with different means or units, use the <strong>coefficient of variation (CV)</strong>:</p><p><code>CV = (standard deviation / mean) x 100 percent</code></p><p>The CV expresses the SD as a percent of the mean. It has no units. Lower CV = more consistent relative to its size.</p><p><em>Example:</em> Machine A fills cans with a mean of 50 ounces and an SD of 5 ounces (CV = 5 / 50 = 10 percent). Machine B fills drums with a mean of 200 ounces and an SD of 10 ounces (CV = 10 / 200 = 5 percent). Machine A has the smaller SD, but Machine B is <strong>more consistent relative to its fill size</strong>.</p><p>The CV works only for ratio data with a positive mean (it breaks down if the mean is near zero).</p>"},{"h":"7. Weighted mean","body":"<p>A normal mean treats every value as equally important. A <strong>weighted mean</strong> gives some values more influence because they represent more units, more money, or a bigger share.</p><p><code>weighted mean = (sum of each value x its weight) / (sum of the weights)</code></p><p>If the weights are percentages that add to 100 percent, you just add up value x weight.</p><p><em>Example 1 (grades):</em> Homework counts 20 percent and you scored 90; quizzes count 30 percent and you scored 80; the final counts 50 percent and you scored 70. Weighted mean = 0.20(90) + 0.30(80) + 0.50(70) = 18 + 24 + 35 = 77. (The plain average of 90, 80 and 70 would be 80, which is misleading because the final matters most.)</p><p><em>Example 2 (purchasing):</em> You bought 100 units at 5 dollars, 300 units at 4 dollars and 600 units at 3 dollars. The average cost per unit is the weighted mean: (100 x 5 + 300 x 4 + 600 x 3) / 1,000 = (500 + 1,200 + 1,800) / 1,000 = 3.50 dollars. Simply averaging 5, 4 and 3 would give 4.00, which overstates the true cost.</p><p>Use a weighted mean whenever groups have different sizes or importance (average price per unit, average grade, combining branch averages of different sizes).</p>"}],"keyPoints":["The mean is the sum of the values divided by how many there are, the median is the middle value after sorting, and the mode is the most frequent value.","The mean uses every value but is pulled by outliers and skew, while the median is resistant to extreme values, so the median is the better measure of center for skewed data such as salaries or home prices.","In a right skewed distribution the long tail is on the high side and the mean is greater than the median. In a left skewed distribution the long tail is on the low side and the mean is less than the median. A good way to remember this is that the mean chases the tail.","Variance is the average squared distance from the mean and standard deviation is its square root, which puts the spread back into the original units of the data.","For a population divide the sum of squared deviations by capital N, but for a sample divide by n minus one, which corrects for the fact that samples tend to underestimate the true variability.","For bell shaped data the empirical rule says about sixty eight percent of values fall within one standard deviation of the mean, ninety five percent within two, and ninety nine point seven percent within three.","Quartiles split sorted data into four equal parts. The interquartile range is the third quartile minus the first quartile and measures the spread of the middle fifty percent of the data.","The five number summary is the minimum, first quartile, median, third quartile and maximum, and it is the information shown in a box plot.","A value is flagged as a potential outlier if it is below the first quartile minus one point five times the interquartile range, or above the third quartile plus one point five times the interquartile range.","A percentile tells you the percent of values at or below a given value, so the ninetieth percentile means doing better than about ninety percent of the group.","The coefficient of variation is the standard deviation divided by the mean, shown as a percent, and it lets you compare variability between data sets with different units or sizes.","A weighted mean gives each value a weight based on its importance or group size, and it is the right way to average things like cost per unit across purchases of different quantities."],"examples":[{"q":"Five technicians' repair times in hours are 5, 7, 8, 9 and 11. Treating this as a SAMPLE, compute the mean, sample variance, sample standard deviation, and the coefficient of variation. Then give the population standard deviation if these five were the entire population.","steps":["Mean = (5 + 7 + 8 + 9 + 11) / 5 = 40 / 5 = 8 hours.","Deviations from the mean: 5 - 8 = -3; 7 - 8 = -1; 8 - 8 = 0; 9 - 8 = 1; 11 - 8 = 3.","Squared deviations: 9, 1, 0, 1, 9. Sum of squares = 20.","Sample variance = 20 / (n - 1) = 20 / 4 = 5 hours squared.","Sample SD = square root of 5 = about 2.24 hours.","Coefficient of variation = SD / mean x 100 = 2.24 / 8 x 100 = about 28.0 percent.","If this were the whole population, divide by N = 5: variance = 20 / 5 = 4, SD = square root of 4 = 2 hours."],"answer":"Mean 8; sample variance 5; sample SD about 2.24; CV about 28 percent. Population variance would be 4 and population SD 2."},{"q":"Daily order counts for 11 days, already sorted, are 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 15, 16, 40. Find the five-number summary, the IQR, the outlier fences, and say whether any value is an outlier. Then compare the mean and median with and without the possible outlier.","steps":["n = 11, so the median is the 6th value = 10.","Lower half (excluding the median): 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, whose median is 6, so Q1 = 6.","Upper half: 12, 13, 15, 16, 40, whose median is 15, so Q3 = 15.","Five-number summary: minimum 3, Q1 6, median 10, Q3 15, maximum 40.","IQR = Q3 - Q1 = 15 - 6 = 9. Then 1.5 x IQR = 13.5.","Lower fence = 6 - 13.5 = -7.5. Upper fence = 15 + 13.5 = 28.5.","The value 40 is above 28.5, so 40 is a potential outlier. No value is below -7.5.","Mean with 40 = 137 / 11 = about 12.45; median = 10. Without 40: mean = 97 / 10 = 9.7; median = (9 + 10) / 2 = 9.5.","The mean moved by about 2.75 (12.45 to 9.7) while the median moved only 0.5 (10 to 9.5), showing that the median resists outliers."],"answer":"Five-number summary 3, 6, 10, 15, 40; IQR 9; fences -7.5 and 28.5; 40 is an outlier. The mean is much more affected than the median."},{"q":"A buyer purchased 100 units at 5 dollars each, 300 units at 4 dollars each, and 600 units at 3 dollars each. What is the average cost per unit? Why is the simple average of 5, 4 and 3 wrong?","steps":["The quantities differ, so use a weighted mean with quantities as weights.","Total cost = (100 x 5) + (300 x 4) + (600 x 3) = 500 + 1,200 + 1,800 = 3,500 dollars.","Total units = 100 + 300 + 600 = 1,000.","Weighted mean = 3,500 / 1,000 = 3.50 dollars per unit.","The simple average (5 + 4 + 3) / 3 = 4.00 treats each price equally, but most units were bought at the cheapest price, so 4.00 overstates the true average."],"answer":"3.50 dollars per unit (weighted mean). The simple average of 4.00 ignores the different quantities."}],"videos":[{"title":"Mean, median and mode, and when to use each","query":"mean median mode when to use each skewed data explained"},{"title":"Standard deviation and variance, sample vs population (n minus 1)","query":"StatQuest standard deviation and variance sample vs population"},{"title":"Quartiles, IQR, five-number summary and outliers","query":"quartiles IQR five number summary outliers 1.5 IQR rule explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Mean","b":"Sum of all values divided by the number of values. Uses every value, so it is sensitive to outliers."},{"f":"Median","b":"Middle value of sorted data (average the two middle values if n is even). Resistant to outliers."},{"f":"Mode","b":"Most frequently occurring value. Can be none, one, or several. The only center for nominal data."},{"f":"Which measure of center to use?","b":"Symmetric data: mean. Skewed or outliers: median. Categorical: mode."},{"f":"Effect of an outlier","b":"Pulls the mean, range, and standard deviation a lot, but barely changes the median and IQR."},{"f":"Right-skewed (positive skew)","b":"Long tail on the high side. Mean > median > mode. Example: incomes."},{"f":"Left-skewed (negative skew)","b":"Long tail on the low side. Mean < median < mode. The mean chases the tail."},{"f":"Range","b":"Maximum minus minimum. Quick but depends only on the two extreme values."},{"f":"Variance","b":"Average squared deviation from the mean. Expressed in squared units."},{"f":"Standard deviation","b":"Square root of the variance, in the same units as the data. Typical distance from the mean."},{"f":"Why divide by n - 1 for a sample?","b":"A sample underestimates the population's variability, so n - 1 corrects the bias when estimating population variance."},{"f":"Population vs sample variance formulas","b":"Population: sum of squared deviations / N. Sample: sum of squared deviations / (n - 1)."},{"f":"Empirical rule (68-95-99.7)","b":"For bell-shaped data about 68% of values lie within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 SD, 99.7% within 3 SD."},{"f":"z-score","b":"(value - mean) / standard deviation. Tells how many SDs a value is from the mean."},{"f":"Quartiles Q1, Q2, Q3","b":"Q1 = 25th percentile (median of lower half), Q2 = median, Q3 = 75th percentile (median of upper half)."},{"f":"Interquartile range (IQR)","b":"Q3 minus Q1. The spread of the middle 50% of the data. Resistant to outliers."},{"f":"Five-number summary","b":"Minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum. The basis of a box plot."},{"f":"Outlier fences (1.5 x IQR rule)","b":"Lower fence = Q1 - 1.5 x IQR. Upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 x IQR. Values beyond them are potential outliers."},{"f":"Percentile","b":"The value below which about p percent of the data fall. The 90th percentile beats about 90% of the group."},{"f":"Coefficient of variation (CV)","b":"(Standard deviation / mean) x 100%. Unitless; compares variability across data sets with different means or units."},{"f":"Weighted mean","b":"Sum of (value x weight) divided by sum of weights. Used when values have different importance or quantities."},{"f":"Interpreting a standard deviation of zero","b":"Every value is identical, so there is no spread. A larger SD means values are more spread out."}],"questions":[{"q":"In a city, most homes sell for 200,000 to 400,000 dollars, but a few mansions sell for over 5 million dollars. A real estate analyst wants to report the typical home price. Which measure of center is best?","o":["Mean, because it uses every value","Median, because it is not pulled by the extreme sales","Mode, because it is calculated with a formula","Range, because it shows how much prices differ"],"a":1,"e":"The mansions create a right-skewed distribution and drag the mean upward. The median is resistant to those extremes and better describes a typical home. The range is a measure of spread, not center.","d":2},{"q":"A quality inspector records the number of defects on five days: 3, 7, 7, 9, 14. What is the mean number of defects per day?","o":["7","9","7.5","8"],"a":3,"e":"The sum is 3 + 7 + 7 + 9 + 14 = 40, and 40 / 5 = 8. The value 7 is both the median and the mode, which is the tempting wrong answer.","d":2},{"q":"A sample contains the values 2, 4, and 6. What is the sample variance?","o":["4","2.67","8","2"],"a":0,"e":"The mean is 4. Squared deviations are 4, 0, and 4, which sum to 8. Sample variance divides by n - 1 = 2, giving 4. Dividing by n = 3 would give 2.67, which is the population variance, and 2 is the standard deviation.","d":2},{"q":"The average (mean) wait time at a call center is 52 seconds, the median is 45 seconds, and the mode is 40 seconds. What is the shape of the distribution?","o":["Left-skewed","Symmetric","Right-skewed","Uniform"],"a":2,"e":"Mean greater than median greater than mode indicates a right-skewed distribution with a long tail of high values pulling the mean upward. In a symmetric distribution these three would be about equal.","d":2},{"q":"For a data set, Q1 = 20 and Q3 = 32. What is the upper outlier fence using the 1.5 x IQR rule?","o":["44","50","52","38"],"a":1,"e":"IQR = 32 - 20 = 12. Then 1.5 x 12 = 18, so the upper fence = 32 + 18 = 50. Any value above 50 would be flagged as a potential outlier. (The lower fence is 20 - 18 = 2.)","d":2},{"q":"Machine A fills cans with a mean of 50 ounces and a standard deviation of 5 ounces. Machine B fills drums with a mean of 200 ounces and a standard deviation of 10 ounces. Which statement is correct about relative variability?","o":["Machine B is more consistent, because its CV is 5 percent compared with 10 percent for Machine A","Machine A is more consistent, because its standard deviation is smaller","Machine A is more consistent, because its mean is smaller","They cannot be compared because the means are different"],"a":0,"e":"The coefficient of variation is designed for exactly this comparison: A = 5 / 50 = 10 percent, B = 10 / 200 = 5 percent. Comparing raw standard deviations is misleading when the means differ.","d":2},{"q":"A course grade is based on homework (20 percent), quizzes (30 percent), and a final exam (50 percent). A student scores 90 on homework, 80 on quizzes, and 70 on the final. What is the weighted course average?","o":["80","75","78","77"],"a":3,"e":"0.20 x 90 + 0.30 x 80 + 0.50 x 70 = 18 + 24 + 35 = 77. The plain average, 80, ignores that the final exam counts the most.","d":2},{"q":"Which statistic is LEAST affected when a single extreme value is added to a data set?","o":["Mean","Range","Median","Standard deviation"],"a":2,"e":"The median depends on the middle position, not on how extreme the extreme values are. The mean, range, and standard deviation all change substantially with one extreme value.","d":2},{"q":"Why is the sample variance calculated by dividing by n - 1 instead of n?","o":["To make the variance smaller on purpose","Because a sample tends to underestimate the variability of the population, and n - 1 corrects this","Because one data point is always removed as an outlier","Because the deviations from the mean must add up to one"],"a":1,"e":"A sample's values sit closer to their own mean than to the true population mean, so dividing by n would run low on average. Using n - 1 gives an unbiased estimate of the population variance. Deviations from the mean add to zero, not one.","d":2},{"q":"Delivery times are measured in minutes and the standard deviation is 4 minutes. In what units is the variance expressed?","o":["Minutes","Percent","It has no units","Square minutes"],"a":3,"e":"Variance is the average of squared deviations, so its units are squared (minutes squared). The standard deviation, the square root of variance, returns to minutes.","d":2},{"q":"A job applicant's test score is at the 90th percentile. What does this mean?","o":["About 90 percent of test takers scored at or below this applicant","The applicant answered 90 percent of the questions correctly","The applicant scored 90 points","Ninety people scored higher than the applicant"],"a":0,"e":"A percentile shows relative standing, the percent of scores at or below the value. It does not show the percent correct or the raw score.","d":2},{"q":"The five-number summary for a set of response times is minimum 4, Q1 10, median 15, Q3 30, maximum 80. What is the interquartile range?","o":["15","76","20","50"],"a":2,"e":"IQR = Q3 - Q1 = 30 - 10 = 20. The value 76 is the range (80 - 4), and 15 is the median, so neither is the IQR.","d":2},{"q":"A survey records each customer's favorite product category (for example Electronics, Clothing, Home). Which measure of center can be used to describe this data?","o":["Mean","Median","Range","Mode"],"a":3,"e":"Categories have no numeric values and no order, so only the mode (most common category) makes sense. A mean or median needs numbers or at least an order.","d":2},{"q":"In a left-skewed distribution, how do the mean and median compare?","o":["The mean is greater than the median","The mean is less than the median","The mean equals the median","The mean always equals the mode"],"a":1,"e":"A left-skewed distribution has a long tail of low values that pulls the mean below the median. The mean chases the tail.","d":2},{"q":"A process produces parts whose lengths are bell-shaped with a mean of 100 mm and a standard deviation of 10 mm. According to the empirical rule, about 95 percent of parts have lengths between:","o":["80 mm and 120 mm","90 mm and 110 mm","70 mm and 130 mm","60 mm and 140 mm"],"a":0,"e":"About 95 percent of bell-shaped data lies within 2 standard deviations of the mean: 100 plus or minus 20 gives 80 to 120. 90 to 110 is about 68 percent, and 70 to 130 is about 99.7 percent.","d":2},{"q":"Weekly returns at a store were 6, 2, 9, 4, 14, and 5 units. What is the median?","o":["5.5","6.5","6.7","5"],"a":0,"e":"Sort first: 2, 4, 5, 6, 9, 14. With six values the median is the average of the 3rd and 4th: (5 + 6) / 2 = 5.5. Taking the middle of the unsorted list gives 6.5, and 6.7 is the mean (40 / 6).","d":1},{"q":"Daily output of a machine over five days was 22, 35, 18, 41, and 29 units. What is the range?","o":["41","29","23","59"],"a":2,"e":"Range = maximum - minimum = 41 - 18 = 23. 29 is both the mean and the median, and 59 is the sum of the extremes.","d":1},{"q":"Shirt sizes sold in a sample of 7 orders were 12, 15, 15, 18, 18, 21, and 25. Which description of the mode is correct?","o":["Unimodal, with a mode of 15","Bimodal, with modes of 15 and 18","The mode is 16.5, the midpoint of 15 and 18","There is no mode because no single value dominates"],"a":1,"e":"Both 15 and 18 occur twice, more often than any other value, so the data set is bimodal. A data set can have more than one mode, and the mode is never the midpoint of two values.","d":1},{"q":"A sales rep scores 85 on a test where the mean is 70 and the standard deviation is 5. What is the z-score?","o":["15.0","0.33","-3.0","3.0"],"a":3,"e":"z = (x - mean) / SD = (85 - 70) / 5 = 3.0, meaning the score is 3 standard deviations above the mean. 15 is only the raw difference before dividing by the SD.","d":1},{"q":"A buyer purchases 200 units at $5, 300 units at $6, and 500 units at $8. What is the weighted average price per unit?","o":["$6.33","$7.00","$6.00","$6.80"],"a":3,"e":"Total cost = 200(5) + 300(6) + 500(8) = 1,000 + 1,800 + 4,000 = 6,800 for 1,000 units, so $6.80. The simple average of the three prices ($6.33) ignores how many units were bought at each price.","d":2},{"q":"A student's score of 78 is higher than 30 of the 40 scores in the class. What is the student's percentile rank?","o":["78th","75th","30th","25th"],"a":1,"e":"Percentile rank = (number of scores below) / (total scores) = 30 / 40 = 75%. The score itself (78) is not the percentile, and 25 is the share of scores at or above it.","d":2},{"q":"For a set of order values, Q1 = 40 and Q3 = 64. Three orders have values 3, 98, and 105. Using the 1.5 x IQR rule, which are outliers?","o":["105 only","98 and 105","3 and 105","None of them"],"a":2,"e":"IQR = 64 - 40 = 24 and 1.5 x 24 = 36. Fences: lower = 40 - 36 = 4, upper = 64 + 36 = 100. So 3 is below the lower fence and 105 is above the upper fence; 98 is inside. Forgetting the lower fence gives '105 only'.","d":2},{"q":"A distribution of daily shipments has a mean of 50, a standard deviation of 5, and an unknown shape. At least what percent of days fall between 40 and 60 shipments?","o":["75%","95%","68%","89%"],"a":0,"e":"Chebyshev's theorem works for any shape. 40 and 60 are k = 2 standard deviations from the mean, so at least 1 - 1/2^2 = 75% of days fall in that range. 95% is the empirical rule, which needs a bell-shaped distribution.","d":2},{"q":"An inspector examined 50 units: 10 had 0 defects, 15 had 1, 20 had 2, and 5 had 3. What is the mean number of defects per unit?","o":["1.5","1.4","2.0","70"],"a":1,"e":"Mean = (0x10 + 1x15 + 2x20 + 3x5) / 50 = 70 / 50 = 1.4. Averaging the defect categories 0, 1, 2, 3 (1.5) ignores the frequencies, 2.0 is the mode, and 70 is the total before dividing.","d":2},{"q":"Every employee at a company receives a flat $2,000 raise. How do the mean and standard deviation of salaries change?","o":["Both increase by $2,000","The mean increases by $2,000 and the standard deviation decreases","The mean is unchanged and the standard deviation increases by $2,000","The mean increases by $2,000 and the standard deviation is unchanged"],"a":3,"e":"Adding a constant to every value shifts the center but does not change distances between values, so spread (range, variance, SD, IQR) stays the same.","d":2},{"q":"Line A fills bags with a mean of 50 g and a variance of 64 g squared. Line B has a mean of 200 g and a variance of 400 g squared. Which line has greater relative variability?","o":["Line B: its variance is larger","Line B: its standard deviation of 20 g is larger than Line A's 8 g","Line A: its CV is 16% versus 10% for Line B","It cannot be determined without the sample sizes"],"a":2,"e":"SD_A = sqrt(64) = 8, so CV_A = 8/50 = 16%. SD_B = sqrt(400) = 20, so CV_B = 20/200 = 10%. Relative variability compares SD to the mean, so A is more variable despite its smaller SD and variance.","d":3},{"q":"The data set 8, 9, 10, 11, 12 has a mean and median of 10. An extreme value of 60 is added. What are the new mean and median?","o":["Mean about 18.3 and median 10.5","Mean about 18.3 and median 11","Mean 10 and median 10 (unchanged)","Mean about 10.5 and median about 18.3"],"a":0,"e":"New mean = 110 / 6 = 18.33. The six sorted values are 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 60, so the median is (10 + 11) / 2 = 10.5. The mean is pulled strongly by the outlier while the median barely moves.","d":3},{"q":"Rep A scored 92 on an assessment with a mean of 80 and an SD of 6. Rep B scored 710 on a different assessment with a mean of 600 and an SD of 50. Relative to their peers, who performed better?","o":["Rep A, because an SD of 6 is smaller than an SD of 50","Rep A, because 92 is nearer the top of its scale","They performed equally, since both are more than 2 SDs above the mean","Rep B, with a z-score of 2.2 versus 2.0 for Rep A"],"a":3,"e":"z_A = (92 - 80) / 6 = 2.0 and z_B = (710 - 600) / 50 = 2.2. Z-scores put different scales on a common footing, and Rep B is further above the mean in SD units. 'Equal' is tempting because both exceed 2 SDs.","d":3},{"q":"Branch A has 40 employees with an average salary of $50,000. Branch B has 10 employees with an average salary of $80,000. What is the average salary across both branches?","o":["$65,000","$56,000","$58,000","$60,000"],"a":1,"e":"Weight each branch average by its headcount: (40 x 50,000 + 10 x 80,000) / 50 = 2,800,000 / 50 = $56,000. The simple average of the two branch means ($65,000) ignores that Branch A is four times larger.","d":3},{"q":"The sample 10, 12, 14, 16, 18 has a mean of 14. What is the sample standard deviation?","o":["2.83","10.0","3.16","8.0"],"a":2,"e":"Squared deviations: 16 + 4 + 0 + 4 + 16 = 40. Sample variance = 40 / (5 - 1) = 10, so SD = sqrt(10) = 3.16. 2.83 is the population SD (sqrt(40/5)), 10 is the sample variance, and 8 is the population variance.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Mean","Sum divided by the count"],["Median","Middle value when sorted"],["Mode","Most frequently occurring value"],["Range","Maximum minus minimum"],["IQR","Q3 minus Q1"],["Variance","Average squared deviation from mean"],["Standard deviation","Square root of the variance"],["CV","Standard deviation divided by mean"]],"traps":["Trap: The mean is always the best measure of center. Truth: with skew or outliers the median is more representative, since the mean is pulled toward the tail.","Trap: The median is the middle value of the list as given. Truth: sort the data first; with an even count average the two middle values.","Trap: Variance and standard deviation are in the same units. Truth: SD uses the original units; variance is in squared units.","Trap: The empirical rule (68-95-99.7) applies to any data set. Truth: it needs a bell-shaped distribution; Chebyshev (at least 75% within 2 SDs) works for any shape.","Trap: The larger standard deviation always means more variability. Truth: when means differ, compare the coefficient of variation (SD / mean)."],"mnemonic":"In a skewed distribution the MEAN chases the TAIL like a dog on a leash, while the median stays near the middle. Right tail: mean > median; left tail: mean < median. And n - 1 for a SAMPLE (it is 'fewer' because a sample is a smaller group).","cram":["Right skew: mean > median > mode. Left skew: mean < median < mode.","Outlier fences: Q1 - 1.5 x IQR and Q3 + 1.5 x IQR.","IQR = Q3 - Q1; it spans the middle 50% of the data.","Sample variance divides by n - 1; population variance divides by N.","Empirical rule: 68%, 95%, 99.7% within 1, 2, 3 SDs (bell-shaped only).","Chebyshev: at least 1 - 1/k^2 within k SDs, any shape.","CV = SD / mean x 100%; compares spread across different scales.","z = (x - mean) / SD; weighted mean divides by total weights."],"ytv":[{"title":"Skewed Distributions and Mean, Median, and Mode (Measures of Central Tendency)","channel":"Quantitative Specialists","id":"xpbYKaEbcPA","why":"Shows how skew pulls the mean away from the median and mode."},{"title":"How To Find The Interquartile Range & any Outliers - Descriptive Statistics","channel":"The Organic Chemistry Tutor","id":"STSP8gTSdT8","why":"Step-by-step IQR and 1.5 x IQR outlier fence calculations."},{"title":"Standard deviation (simply explained)","channel":"numiqo","id":"esskJJF8pCc","why":"Builds intuition for what standard deviation measures and how it is computed."}]},{"id":"u4","title":"Visualizing Data","day":2,"summary":"Choose the right chart for the data and the question, read histograms and box plots, build frequency tables, spot misleading graphs, and design useful dashboards.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Start with the question and the data type","body":"<p>A chart is a tool for answering a question. Before picking one, ask two things:</p><ol><li><strong>What am I trying to show?</strong> (compare, trend over time, distribution, relationship, parts of a whole, ranking)</li><li><strong>What type of data do I have?</strong> (categorical or numeric; one variable or two)</li></ol><p>Use this cheat sheet:</p><ul><li><strong>Compare amounts across categories</strong>: bar chart.</li><li><strong>Parts of a whole (few categories)</strong>: pie chart, or a stacked bar.</li><li><strong>Change over time</strong>: line chart.</li><li><strong>Distribution of one numeric variable</strong>: histogram (large data), dot plot or stem-and-leaf plot (small data), box plot (especially to compare groups).</li><li><strong>Relationship between two numeric variables</strong>: scatter plot.</li><li><strong>Rank causes by importance</strong>: Pareto chart.</li><li><strong>Two categories with a magnitude in each cell</strong>: heat map.</li><li><strong>Monitor many KPIs at a glance</strong>: dashboard.</li></ul><p>The best chart is the one that lets your audience see the answer in a few seconds, without having to read a paragraph to decode it.</p>"},{"h":"2. Charts for categories: bar, pie, Pareto","body":"<ul><li><strong>Bar chart (column chart):</strong> one bar per category, bar length shows the value. Bars have <strong>gaps</strong> between them because the categories are separate. Sort the bars (largest to smallest) unless the categories have a natural order. Use horizontal bars when labels are long. <strong>Always start the value axis at zero.</strong></li><li><strong>Pie chart:</strong> a circle divided into slices that show each category's share of a total. Works only when there are <strong>few slices</strong> (about five or fewer), the parts add up to <strong>100 percent</strong>, and you want to show the whole. Bad for comparing similar-sized slices or showing change over time.</li><li><strong>Pareto chart:</strong> a bar chart with the bars sorted from <strong>largest to smallest</strong> plus a <strong>line showing the cumulative percent</strong>. It is a quality-improvement tool built on the idea that a <em>vital few</em> causes create most of the problems (the 80/20 idea). Use it to decide what to fix first.</li></ul><p><em>Business example:</em> A plant tallies 40 defects: 16 scratches, 10 dents, 8 cracks, 6 discolorations. A Pareto chart shows scratches and dents together account for 65 percent of all defects, so those two should be attacked first.</p>"},{"h":"3. Charts for distributions: histogram, box plot, stem-and-leaf, dot plot","body":"<ul><li><strong>Histogram:</strong> numeric data is grouped into equal-width <strong>bins (classes)</strong>; the height of each bar shows how many (or what share of) values fall in the bin. The bars <strong>touch</strong>, because the numeric scale is continuous. Shows shape, center, spread and gaps. <em>Histogram vs bar chart:</em> a histogram is for numeric data in ranges; a bar chart is for separate categories.</li><li><strong>Box plot (box-and-whisker plot):</strong> draws the five-number summary. The <strong>box</strong> runs from Q1 to Q3 (the middle 50 percent, length = IQR); a line inside marks the <strong>median</strong>; <strong>whiskers</strong> extend to the smallest and largest values that are not outliers (within 1.5 x IQR of the box); separate <strong>dots</strong> mark potential outliers. Excellent for comparing several groups side by side.</li><li><strong>Stem-and-leaf plot:</strong> splits each number into a <em>stem</em> (leading digits) and a <em>leaf</em> (last digit), so you see the shape while keeping the actual values. Good for small data sets. Example: 23, 25, 31, 34, 34 becomes stem 2 | 3 5 and stem 3 | 1 4 4.</li><li><strong>Dot plot:</strong> one dot per value above a number line, stacked when values repeat. Good for small data sets, shows clusters, gaps and outliers.</li></ul><p>Rule of thumb: <strong>small data set</strong> use a dot plot or stem-and-leaf; <strong>larger data set</strong> use a histogram; <strong>comparing groups</strong> use box plots.</p>"},{"h":"4. Charts for time and relationships: line, scatter, heat map","body":"<ul><li><strong>Line chart:</strong> points connected by a line, with <strong>time on the x-axis</strong>. Best for showing trends, seasonality and sudden changes. Several lines can compare series (this year vs last year).</li><li><strong>Scatter plot:</strong> each point is one item, plotted by two numeric variables (x and y). Shows whether the variables are related and how: <strong>positive</strong> (both rise together), <strong>negative</strong> (one rises while the other falls), or <strong>no relationship</strong>; also shows how strong, whether it is linear, and any outliers. Put the independent variable on x and the dependent variable on y. Remember: a relationship does not prove cause and effect.</li><li><strong>Heat map:</strong> a grid where <strong>color intensity</strong> shows the size of a value. Great for spotting patterns across two categorical dimensions (call volume by weekday and hour of day) or for showing a correlation table.</li></ul>"},{"h":"5. Frequency tables and relative frequency","body":"<p>A <strong>frequency table</strong> counts how many observations fall into each category or numeric class. It is the table behind bar charts and histograms.</p><ul><li><strong>Frequency</strong> = the count.</li><li><strong>Relative frequency</strong> = frequency / total number of observations (a proportion; multiply by 100 for a percent). All relative frequencies add to 1 (or 100 percent).</li><li><strong>Cumulative frequency</strong> = running total of frequencies. <strong>Cumulative relative frequency</strong> = running total of relative frequencies, which ends at 1 (100 percent). A Pareto chart's line shows this.</li></ul><p><strong>Building a histogram:</strong></p><ol><li>Find the range: max minus min.</li><li>Choose the number of classes (usually 5 to 15).</li><li>Class width = range / number of classes, <em>rounded up</em> to a convenient number.</li><li>Make non-overlapping classes that cover all data, count the values in each, and draw touching bars.</li></ol><p>Relative frequency histograms have the same shape as frequency histograms, but the vertical axis shows proportions, which makes it easy to compare groups of different sizes.</p>"},{"h":"6. Reading histogram shapes and box plots","body":"<p><strong>Histogram shapes:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Symmetric (bell-shaped):</strong> the left and right halves mirror each other; mean is about equal to the median.</li><li><strong>Right-skewed:</strong> a peak on the left and a long tail to the right (incomes, wait times). Mean &gt; median.</li><li><strong>Left-skewed:</strong> a peak on the right and a long tail to the left (scores on an easy test). Mean &lt; median.</li><li><strong>Uniform:</strong> bars are about equal height, so all values are about equally likely.</li><li><strong>Bimodal:</strong> two distinct peaks. This often means <strong>two different groups mixed together</strong> (two machines, two shifts, two customer segments), a clue to separate the data and investigate.</li></ul><p><strong>Reading a box plot:</strong></p><ul><li>The line in the box is the median; the box shows where the middle 50 percent of the data lies.</li><li><strong>A longer box or longer whiskers</strong> mean more spread.</li><li>If the median is <strong>nearer the bottom of the box</strong> and the <strong>upper whisker is longer</strong>, the data is <strong>right-skewed</strong>. The reverse indicates left-skewed. If the median is centered and the whiskers are about equal, the data is roughly symmetric.</li><li>Dots beyond the whiskers are potential outliers.</li><li>Box plots cannot show multiple peaks, so use a histogram to check for bimodality.</li></ul>"},{"h":"7. Misleading graphs and good dashboard design","body":"<p><strong>Common ways graphs mislead:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Truncated axis:</strong> a bar chart whose value axis starts above zero makes small differences look huge. (Revenue of 92 vs 98 million looks like a three-fold gap if the axis begins at 90.)</li><li><strong>Inconsistent scale:</strong> uneven tick intervals, different scales on side-by-side charts, or a stretched or squeezed chart that exaggerates or hides change. Dual y-axes can also suggest relationships that do not exist.</li><li><strong>3D distortion:</strong> 3D bars and pie slices change the apparent size of the parts; front slices look bigger and bar tops are hard to read.</li><li><strong>Cherry-picked range:</strong> showing only the favorable time window (the three good weeks of a bad year) or leaving out context.</li><li><strong>Other tricks:</strong> pictures sized in two dimensions, pie slices that do not add to 100 percent, missing labels or units, too many colors.</li></ul><p><strong>Dashboard and KPI best practices:</strong></p><ul><li>A <strong>dashboard</strong> is a single screen that shows the most important metrics at a glance. A <strong>KPI (key performance indicator)</strong> is a measurable value tied to a business goal (on-time delivery rate, defect rate).</li><li>Know the <strong>audience and purpose</strong>; show only what supports decisions.</li><li>Put the <strong>most important KPIs top-left</strong> and make them big; use consistent colors, scales and fonts.</li><li>Give <strong>context</strong>: targets, prior period, benchmarks, and status colors (use color-blind-friendly palettes).</li><li>Pick the <strong>right chart</strong> for each question and keep clutter, decoration and 3D effects out.</li><li>Limit it to about 5 to 9 visuals so it can be understood in about five seconds; add filters or drill-down for detail.</li><li>Label titles, units and the date the data was last refreshed.</li></ul>"}],"keyPoints":["Choose a chart by asking what you want to show and what type of data you have, because the right chart lets people see the answer in seconds.","Use a bar chart to compare categories, a line chart to show change over time, a scatter plot to show the relationship between two numeric variables, and a pie chart only for parts of a whole with a few categories.","A histogram groups numeric data into equal width bins with bars that touch, while a bar chart shows separate categories with gaps between the bars.","A box plot displays the five number summary. The box spans the first to the third quartile, a line marks the median, whiskers show the typical range, and dots mark potential outliers.","In a box plot, if the median sits closer to the bottom of the box and the upper whisker is longer, the data is right skewed. The reverse means left skewed.","A Pareto chart sorts causes from largest to smallest and adds a cumulative percent line, so you can focus on the vital few causes that create most of the problems.","Stem and leaf plots and dot plots work well for small data sets because they show the shape of the data while keeping the actual values.","A heat map uses color intensity to show how large a value is across two categories, such as call volume by day and hour.","Relative frequency is the count in a category divided by the total, and all relative frequencies add up to one or one hundred percent.","Histogram shapes include symmetric, right skewed, left skewed, uniform, and bimodal. A bimodal shape with two peaks often signals two different groups mixed together.","Misleading graphs use tricks such as truncated axes, inconsistent scales, three dimensional effects, and cherry picked time ranges to exaggerate or hide differences.","A good dashboard has a clear purpose and audience, puts the most important key performance indicators first, adds context like targets and prior periods, and avoids clutter."],"examples":[{"q":"A plant records 40 defects this month: scratches 16, dents 10, cracks 8, discolorations 6. Build the frequency table with relative and cumulative relative frequencies, and explain what a Pareto chart would tell the manager.","steps":["Total defects = 16 + 10 + 8 + 6 = 40.","Relative frequency = count / 40: scratches 16/40 = 0.40; dents 10/40 = 0.25; cracks 8/40 = 0.20; discolorations 6/40 = 0.15. These add to 1.00.","The categories are already sorted from largest to smallest, as a Pareto chart requires.","Cumulative relative frequency: scratches 0.40 (40 percent); plus dents 0.65 (65 percent); plus cracks 0.85 (85 percent); plus discolorations 1.00 (100 percent).","The bars would be 16, 10, 8, 6 tall with a line rising through 40, 65, 85 and 100 percent.","Interpretation: just two defect types, scratches and dents, cause 65 percent of all defects, so fixing those first gives the biggest payoff."],"answer":"Relative frequencies 0.40, 0.25, 0.20, 0.15; cumulative 40, 65, 85, 100 percent. Scratches and dents account for 65 percent of defects, so tackle them first."},{"q":"A box plot of customer wait times (minutes) shows: minimum 10, Q1 25, median 30, Q3 50, maximum 90. (a) What is the IQR? (b) Is 90 an outlier? (c) Describe the shape.","steps":["IQR = Q3 - Q1 = 50 - 25 = 25 minutes.","1.5 x IQR = 37.5. Upper fence = 50 + 37.5 = 87.5. Lower fence = 25 - 37.5 = -12.5.","The maximum, 90, is greater than 87.5, so it is a potential outlier and would be drawn as a dot. (The upper whisker would stop at the largest value that is at or below 87.5.) Nothing falls below the lower fence.","Shape: the median (30) is only 5 above Q1 but 20 below Q3, so it sits near the bottom of the box. The upper part (box top and whisker) is stretched out, with a high outlier.","Conclusion: the distribution is right-skewed, so the mean wait would be higher than the median of 30 minutes."],"answer":"IQR = 25 minutes; 90 is a potential outlier (above the 87.5 fence); the distribution is right-skewed."},{"q":"A manager has 60 order-processing times that range from a minimum of 12 minutes to a maximum of 97 minutes and wants a histogram with 6 classes. What class width should be used, and what are the classes if the first class starts at 10?","steps":["Range = maximum - minimum = 97 - 12 = 85.","Class width = range / number of classes = 85 / 6 = 14.17.","Round UP to a convenient number: 15 minutes (so that all data is covered).","Starting at 10, the classes are 10 to 24, 25 to 39, 40 to 54, 55 to 69, 70 to 84, and 85 to 99.","Check: 6 classes x 15 = 90 minutes of coverage, from 10 up to 99, which includes the largest value of 97."],"answer":"Class width 15; classes 10-24, 25-39, 40-54, 55-69, 70-84, 85-99."}],"videos":[{"title":"Choosing the right chart: bar, line, pie, scatter, histogram","query":"how to choose the right chart type data visualization bar line pie scatter histogram"},{"title":"How to read a box plot and histogram shapes","query":"StatQuest box plots explained how to read a boxplot histogram shapes skewed"},{"title":"Misleading graphs and good dashboard design","query":"misleading graphs truncated axis 3D charts cherry picking and dashboard design best practices"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Bar chart","b":"Compares values across separate categories using bars with gaps between them. Value axis should start at zero."},{"f":"Histogram vs bar chart","b":"Histogram: numeric data grouped into continuous bins, bars touch. Bar chart: separate categories, bars have gaps."},{"f":"Pie chart","b":"Shows each category's share of a whole. Use only with a few slices that add to 100%."},{"f":"Line chart","b":"Connects points over time (time on the x-axis). Best for trends and seasonality."},{"f":"Scatter plot","b":"Plots pairs of numeric values to show the relationship (positive, negative, none), strength, and outliers."},{"f":"Box plot (parts)","b":"Box = Q1 to Q3, line = median, whiskers = non-outlier min and max, dots = potential outliers."},{"f":"Reading skew from a box plot","b":"Median near the bottom of the box and a longer upper whisker = right-skewed. The opposite = left-skewed."},{"f":"Pareto chart","b":"Bars sorted from largest to smallest plus a cumulative percent line; reveals the vital few causes (80/20 idea)."},{"f":"Stem-and-leaf plot","b":"Splits each value into stem (leading digits) and leaf (last digit); shows shape and keeps the actual values. Good for small data."},{"f":"Dot plot","b":"One dot per value above a number line; shows clusters, gaps and outliers in small data sets."},{"f":"Heat map","b":"A grid where color intensity represents magnitude, such as call volume by weekday and hour."},{"f":"Dashboard and KPI","b":"Dashboard: one screen of key visuals. KPI: a measurable value tied to a business goal (e.g., on-time delivery rate)."},{"f":"Frequency, relative frequency, cumulative","b":"Frequency = count. Relative = count / total (sums to 1). Cumulative = running total."},{"f":"Symmetric, right-skewed, left-skewed histograms","b":"Symmetric: mirror image. Right-skewed: long tail to the high side, mean > median. Left-skewed: long tail to the low side, mean < median."},{"f":"Bimodal distribution","b":"A histogram with two peaks; often two different groups (shifts, machines, segments) mixed together."},{"f":"Uniform distribution","b":"Bars of about equal height; all values are about equally likely."},{"f":"Class width for a histogram","b":"(Maximum - minimum) / number of classes, rounded up to a convenient number."},{"f":"Truncated axis","b":"Value axis that does not start at zero; makes small differences in bar charts look huge."},{"f":"3D distortion and inconsistent scales","b":"3D effects change apparent sizes; uneven or mismatched scales exaggerate or hide change."},{"f":"Cherry-picked range","b":"Showing only a favorable time window or subset while leaving out context that tells a different story."},{"f":"Matching chart to question","b":"Compare: bar. Trend: line. Distribution: histogram or box plot. Relationship: scatter. Share of whole: pie. Rank causes: Pareto."},{"f":"Good dashboard design","b":"Clear purpose and audience, key KPIs first, targets for context, consistent scales and colors, few visuals, minimal clutter."}],"questions":[{"q":"A manager wants to show how monthly sales changed over the past 24 months and identify seasonal patterns. Which chart is most appropriate?","o":["Pie chart","Scatter plot","Histogram","Line chart"],"a":3,"e":"Change over time is shown with a line chart, which has time on the x-axis. A pie chart shows shares of a whole and a histogram shows the distribution of values, neither of which shows a time trend.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst wants to examine whether stores that spend more on advertising tend to have higher sales, using data from 40 stores. Which chart should be used?","o":["Scatter plot","Pie chart","Bar chart of store names","Stem-and-leaf plot"],"a":0,"e":"Examining the relationship between two numeric variables calls for a scatter plot, with advertising on the x-axis and sales on the y-axis. A pie chart cannot show a relationship.","d":2},{"q":"A company wants to show each of its four product lines as a share of total revenue for the year. Which chart is the most direct fit?","o":["Scatter plot","Histogram","Pie chart","Box plot"],"a":2,"e":"Parts of a whole with only a few categories is the classic use of a pie chart. (A sorted bar chart of the percentages would also work if you want to compare similar-sized shares.) A histogram and box plot show distributions of numeric data, and a scatter plot shows relationships.","d":2},{"q":"What is the main purpose of a Pareto chart in quality improvement?","o":["To show how a process changes over time","To rank causes by frequency and show the cumulative percent, identifying the vital few to fix first","To show the relationship between two numeric variables","To display the five-number summary of a data set"],"a":1,"e":"A Pareto chart sorts causes from most to least frequent and adds a cumulative percent line to highlight the few causes responsible for most problems. Showing change over time is the job of a line chart or run chart.","d":2},{"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a histogram from a bar chart?","o":["A histogram groups numeric values into continuous bins, and its bars touch","A histogram displays categories with gaps between the bars","A histogram can only show percentages that sum to 100","A histogram shows the relationship between two variables"],"a":0,"e":"Histograms are for numeric data grouped into ranges, so the bars touch to show the continuous scale. Bar charts show separate categories and have gaps. A scatter plot is for relationships.","d":2},{"q":"A bar chart compares quarterly revenue of 92 million dollars and 98 million dollars, but the vertical axis starts at 90 million. The second bar looks about four times taller. Which type of misleading graph is this?","o":["Cherry-picked time range","Three-dimensional distortion","Inconsistent scale across charts","Truncated axis"],"a":3,"e":"The axis does not begin at zero, so a small difference is visually exaggerated. Bar lengths should be proportional to the values, which requires a zero baseline.","d":2},{"q":"A box plot shows the median line close to the bottom of the box, a short lower whisker, and a much longer upper whisker. What is the most likely shape of the distribution?","o":["Left-skewed","Right-skewed","Symmetric","Uniform"],"a":1,"e":"The long upper whisker means the high side stretches out, and the median near the lower part of the box confirms that the data bunches up at lower values: a right-skewed distribution. Left-skewed would show the reverse.","d":2},{"q":"A histogram of the weights of items coming off a production line shows two distinct peaks, one near 48 grams and another near 52 grams. What is the best interpretation?","o":["The data is uniform","The data is right-skewed","The data is bimodal, possibly because two machines or shifts are mixed together","The data is symmetric with a single center"],"a":2,"e":"Two separate peaks describe a bimodal distribution. This often indicates two different underlying groups (such as two machines), so the next step would be to separate the data by machine, shift, or supplier.","d":2},{"q":"In a box plot, what does a dot drawn beyond the end of a whisker represent?","o":["The mean of the data","The third quartile","A value that occurs many times","A potential outlier beyond 1.5 x IQR from the box"],"a":3,"e":"Whiskers extend to the most extreme values within 1.5 x IQR of the box. Points beyond that are plotted individually as potential outliers. The mean is not displayed in a standard box plot.","d":2},{"q":"Of 60 customers surveyed, 18 said they prefer the mobile app. What is the relative frequency for the mobile app category?","o":["0.30","0.18","3.33","0.60"],"a":0,"e":"Relative frequency = count / total = 18 / 60 = 0.30, or 30 percent. 3.33 is the inverted ratio (60 / 18).","d":2},{"q":"A company's stock fell over the year, but the company's presentation shows only the three best-performing weeks of the year as an upward line. What kind of misleading practice is this?","o":["Truncated axis","Three-dimensional distortion","Cherry-picked time range","Dual-axis confusion"],"a":2,"e":"Showing only a favorable window and omitting the rest of the period misrepresents the overall trend. That is cherry-picking.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is a best practice for designing an executive dashboard?","o":["Use many colors and chart types to keep viewers interested","Highlight the most important KPIs prominently and give context such as targets or prior-period comparisons","Include every available metric so nothing is missed","Use 3D charts to make the dashboard more visually engaging"],"a":1,"e":"A dashboard should be understood at a glance. Prominent KPIs with context (targets, trends) support decisions, while clutter, too many metrics, and 3D effects hurt readability.","d":2},{"q":"A logistics manager wants to compare the typical delivery time and the variability of delivery times across four carriers. Which display is best?","o":["Pie chart","Line chart","Scatter plot","Side-by-side box plots"],"a":3,"e":"Box plots show the median, spread (IQR), and outliers for each group, and placing them side by side makes the carriers easy to compare. A pie chart shows shares of a whole, not distributions.","d":2},{"q":"A call center wants to see when call volume peaks across days of the week and hours of the day. Which visualization is a good fit?","o":["A heat map with weekday and hour as the axes and color showing call volume","A pie chart with one slice per hour","A box plot of the total number of calls","A stem-and-leaf plot of the weekday names"],"a":0,"e":"A heat map uses color intensity across two categorical dimensions (day and hour) to reveal hot spots at a glance. A pie chart with 24 slices would be unreadable.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is the POOREST choice of chart for the stated purpose?","o":["A bar chart of sales by region","A line chart of monthly sales over two years","A pie chart of 12 monthly sales values to show the trend over the year","A histogram of the sizes of customer orders"],"a":2,"e":"A pie chart shows parts of a whole and is poor with 12 slices; it cannot show a trend over time. A line chart is the right tool for trends. The other three choices fit their purposes.","d":2},{"q":"A regional manager wants to compare last quarter's sales totals across five sales regions. Which chart is the best fit?","o":["Line chart","Bar (column) chart","Scatter plot","Histogram"],"a":1,"e":"Bar charts compare values across distinct categories. A line chart is for trends over time, a scatter plot for relationships between two numeric variables, and a histogram for the distribution of one numeric variable.","d":1},{"q":"A pie chart is most appropriate when:","o":["Showing a trend over 36 months","Comparing 15 categories with very similar values","Showing the relationship between two numeric variables","Showing the parts of a whole for a small number of categories that add to 100 percent"],"a":3,"e":"Pies show composition of a single whole and work only with a few slices. With many similar slices, angles are hard to compare and a bar chart is better.","d":1},{"q":"A frequency table for order sizes has class frequencies of 12, 18, and 30. What is the cumulative relative frequency after the second class?","o":["30%","20%","50%","100%"],"a":2,"e":"Total = 60. Cumulative frequency after class 2 is 12 + 18 = 30, and 30 / 60 = 50%. 30% is class 2 alone (18/60) and 20% is class 1 alone (12/60).","d":1},{"q":"In a scatter plot of advertising spend (x) versus sales (y), the points trend upward from the lower left to the upper right. This pattern suggests:","o":["A positive association between the variables","A negative association between the variables","No relationship between the variables","That advertising spend causes sales to increase"],"a":0,"e":"An upward trend means that as x rises, y tends to rise: a positive association. Causation is the tempting conclusion, but a scatter plot of observational data shows association only.","d":1},{"q":"Order values range from $12 to $92. An analyst wants a histogram with 8 equal-width classes covering this range. What class width is appropriate?","o":["$10","$8","$11.50","$80"],"a":0,"e":"Class width = (maximum - minimum) / number of classes = (92 - 12) / 8 = 10. Using 8 mistakes the number of classes for the width, 11.5 is 92/8, and 80 is the range.","d":2},{"q":"A histogram of 40 delivery times shows these counts: 0 to under 10 min: 4; 10 to under 20: 9; 20 to under 30: 15; 30 to under 40: 8; 40 to under 50: 4. What percent of deliveries took 20 to under 30 minutes?","o":["15%","62.5%","37.5%","25%"],"a":2,"e":"Relative frequency = 15 / 40 = 0.375 = 37.5%. Reporting 15% treats the count as a percent, and 62.5% is the share outside this class.","d":2},{"q":"A pictograph shows sales doubling from 1 to 2 by drawing a coin icon twice as tall AND twice as wide. Why is this misleading?","o":["Icons can never be used in charts","Doubling only the height would make the increase look even larger","It uses too many colors","The area becomes four times as large, so the increase looks bigger than it really is"],"a":3,"e":"Scaling both dimensions of an icon by 2 multiplies its area by 4, so viewers perceive a fourfold change for a twofold increase. Scale only one dimension, or use repeated icons of equal size.","d":2},{"q":"Box plots for delivery times show Carrier A's box spanning 30 to 50 minutes and Carrier B's box spanning 38 to 42 minutes. Both medians are 40. What can be concluded?","o":["Carrier A is faster because its box extends lower","Carrier B is more consistent; its IQR of 4 minutes is far smaller than Carrier A's 20","Carrier B is slower because its box is smaller","Both carriers have equal variability because the medians match"],"a":1,"e":"The box spans Q1 to Q3, so its length is the IQR: 20 minutes for A and 4 for B. Equal medians show the same typical time, but a narrower box means less variability.","d":2},{"q":"A Pareto chart of defects shows scratches 45, dents 25, cracks 15, and other 15. What cumulative percent do the top two categories account for?","o":["45%","25%","70%","85%"],"a":2,"e":"Total defects = 100. The top two are 45 + 25 = 70, so 70%. 85% is the cumulative value after the third category.","d":2},{"q":"A dashboard uses only red and green to show below-target and above-target KPIs. Why is this a design problem?","o":["Many viewers with color-vision deficiency cannot tell red from green, so symbols or labels should be added","Red and green cannot be displayed on screens","Only blue may be used for KPIs","Dashboards should never use color"],"a":0,"e":"About 8% of men have red-green color blindness. Redundant cues such as arrows, icons, or text labels, or color-blind-safe palettes, keep the message clear for everyone.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following can a histogram reveal that a standard box plot cannot?","o":["The median","The interquartile range","Whether any values are outliers","Whether the distribution has two distinct peaks (is bimodal)"],"a":3,"e":"A box plot summarizes the five-number summary and outliers, so it hides the shape within the box, including multiple peaks. A histogram shows the full shape of the distribution.","d":3},{"q":"A stacked bar chart shows monthly revenue split into four product lines. Which comparison is hardest for viewers to make accurately?","o":["The total revenue of each month","Comparing the size of a middle segment across months, because it has no common baseline","The size of the bottom segment across months","Which month had the highest total"],"a":1,"e":"Only the bottom segment and the total bar share a common baseline. Middle and top segments start at different heights, so their lengths are hard to compare by eye.","d":3},{"q":"In a data set, Q1 = 20, median = 26, and Q3 = 34. The two largest values are 51 and 58, and no other value exceeds 51. How should the upper end of the box plot be drawn under the 1.5 x IQR rule?","o":["The whisker ends at 51 and 58 is plotted as a separate outlier point","The whisker extends to 58 and there are no outliers","The whisker ends at 55 and 58 is an outlier","The whisker ends at 34 and both 51 and 58 are outliers"],"a":0,"e":"IQR = 14, so the upper fence = 34 + 1.5(14) = 55. The whisker extends to the largest value not beyond the fence (51), not to the fence itself. 58 > 55 is an outlier shown as a dot.","d":3},{"q":"A CFO wants to show how each product line contributed to total revenue across 12 months and also how the total changed over the year. Which display is best?","o":["Twelve separate pie charts","A single scatter plot of revenue versus product","A stacked column or stacked area chart with months on the x-axis","A histogram of monthly revenue"],"a":2,"e":"A stacked chart shows both the component contributions and the overall total over time. Twelve pies make trends and totals hard to compare, and a histogram ignores time order.","d":3},{"q":"A bar chart's vertical axis starts at 40 instead of 0. The bars represent 50 and 60 units. How tall does the 60 bar look compared with the 50 bar, and how does that compare with the true ratio?","o":["1.2 times as tall, because truncating the axis does not change bar heights","1.5 times as tall, but the true ratio is 1.2","2.0 times as tall, which matches the true ratio","2.0 times as tall, but the true ratio is only 1.2"],"a":3,"e":"Visible heights are 60 - 40 = 20 and 50 - 40 = 10, so the bar looks twice as tall, while the true ratio is 60 / 50 = 1.2. A truncated axis exaggerates differences in a bar chart.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Bar chart","Compares values across categories"],["Line chart","Shows change over time"],["Histogram","Distribution of numeric data in bins"],["Scatter plot","Relationship between two numeric variables"],["Box plot","Five-number summary with outliers"],["Pareto chart","Ranked causes with cumulative percent"],["Heat map","Color intensity shows magnitude"],["KPI","Metric tracking progress toward a goal"]],"traps":["Trap: A histogram is just a bar chart. Truth: histogram bars touch and show ordered numeric classes of one continuous variable; bar charts compare separate categories.","Trap: The box in a box plot covers the whole data range. Truth: the box spans Q1 to Q3 (middle 50%); whiskers reach the fences and dots show outliers.","Trap: Pie charts work for many categories or for trends over time. Truth: use them for a few parts of one whole; use bars or lines otherwise.","Trap: A truncated y-axis is fine on a bar chart. Truth: bar length encodes value, so bars must start at zero or differences are exaggerated.","Trap: A strong upward trend in a scatter plot shows causation. Truth: it shows association only."],"mnemonic":"Match the question to the chart: Compare = Bar, Trend = Line, Share = Pie, Relate = Scatter, Distribute = Histogram, Summarize spread = Box. Rule for the hardest idea (box plots): the box is the MIDDLE 50%, whiskers reach the fences, dots are outliers. Bars always start at zero.","cram":["Bar compares categories; line shows trends over time.","Scatter plot shows the relationship between two numeric variables.","Histogram: touching bars show the distribution of one continuous variable.","Box plot: box is Q1 to Q3, line is median, dots are outliers.","Pie charts: a few parts of one whole totaling 100 percent.","Bar charts must start at zero; truncated axes exaggerate differences.","Pareto chart: bars sorted descending plus a cumulative percent line.","Class width = range / classes; relative frequency = count / total."],"ytv":[{"title":"Skewness - Right, Left & Symmetric Distribution - Mean, Median, & Mode With Boxplots - Statistics","channel":"The Organic Chemistry Tutor","id":"U0NZu6f5TMI","why":"Reading shape and skew from box plots and distributions."},{"title":"Misleading Graphs: Real Life Examples","channel":"Stephanie Glen","id":"GPGk8ANB-MI","why":"Real examples of truncated axes, distorted scales, and other misleading graph tricks."},{"title":"How to choose the best chart for your data","channel":"Metabase","id":"QSXN28qL1D4","why":"Practical guide to matching chart type to the question being asked."}]},{"id":"u5","title":"Probability","day":3,"summary":"How to measure chance with numbers from 0 to 1, combine probabilities with the addition and multiplication rules, read two-way tables, compute expected value, and count outcomes.","lesson":[{"h":"1. What probability is (the 0 to 1 scale)","body":"<p><strong>Probability</strong> is a number that tells you how likely something is to happen. It always lives on a scale from <strong>0 to 1</strong>.</p><ul><li><strong>0</strong> means the event is impossible (a part that is both defective and perfect at the same time).</li><li><strong>1</strong> means the event is certain (the sun will rise tomorrow).</li><li><strong>0.5</strong> means a coin-flip chance. You can also say 50%. Percent is just probability times 100.</li></ul><p>A probability can <em>never</em> be negative and can <em>never</em> be bigger than 1. If you calculate 1.2 or -0.3, you made a mistake.</p><p><strong>Three ways to get a probability:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Classical</strong> (theoretical): every outcome is equally likely, so you count. A fair six-sided die gives P(rolling a 4) = 1/6. No data needed, just logic.</li><li><strong>Empirical</strong> (relative frequency): you use past data. If 28 of the last 400 shipments were late, P(late) = 28/400 = 0.07. This is the most common type in business.</li><li><strong>Subjective</strong>: it is a judgment call or educated guess with no formal data. A sales director says, 'I think there is a 70% chance this client renews.'</li></ol><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> if a question says 'based on past records' it is empirical; 'all outcomes equally likely' is classical; 'in my opinion' or 'gut feeling' is subjective.</p>"},{"h":"2. Sample space, events, and the complement rule","body":"<p>The <strong>sample space</strong> is the list of <em>every</em> possible outcome. For flipping two coins it is HH, HT, TH, TT (four outcomes). An <strong>event</strong> is any piece of the sample space you care about, such as 'at least one head' = {HH, HT, TH}. With four equally likely outcomes, P(at least one head) = 3/4 = 0.75.</p><p>All the outcomes in a sample space add up to a probability of 1. That leads to the <strong>complement rule</strong>:</p><p><code>P(not A) = 1 - P(A)</code></p><p>The complement of an event is 'everything else' (A does not happen). If 92% of parts pass inspection, then P(a part fails) = 1 - 0.92 = 0.08.</p><p><strong>The 'at least one' shortcut.</strong> Whenever a question says 'at least one', work out the opposite ('none') and subtract from 1. Example: each of 3 independent parts has a 10% chance of being defective. P(none defective) = 0.9 x 0.9 x 0.9 = 0.729. So P(at least one defective) = 1 - 0.729 = <strong>0.271</strong>. This is much faster than listing every way to get one, two, or three defects.</p>"},{"h":"3. The addition rule ('A or B')","body":"<p>Use the <strong>addition rule</strong> when the question asks for the probability that <em>either</em> A <em>or</em> B (or both) happens.</p><p><strong>Case 1: Mutually exclusive events.</strong> Two events are <em>mutually exclusive</em> when they cannot happen at the same time (an order can be small or medium, never both). Then you simply add:</p><p><code>P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)</code></p><p>Example: P(small order) = 0.25 and P(medium order) = 0.45. P(small or medium) = 0.25 + 0.45 = 0.70.</p><p><strong>Case 2: Not mutually exclusive.</strong> If both can happen together (a customer can buy online <em>and</em> in store), adding double-counts the overlap, so you subtract it once:</p><p><code>P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)</code></p><p>Example: P(online) = 0.60, P(in store) = 0.45, P(both) = 0.20. P(online or store) = 0.60 + 0.45 - 0.20 = <strong>0.85</strong>.</p><p>The general formula (Case 2) always works. In Case 1, P(A and B) is 0, so the subtraction disappears.</p>"},{"h":"4. The multiplication rule, independence, and conditional probability ('A and B')","body":"<p>Use the <strong>multiplication rule</strong> for the probability that A <em>and</em> B both happen.</p><p><strong>Independent events:</strong> knowing that one happened does not change the chance of the other. Example: two separate machines each work 95% of the time and do not affect each other.</p><p><code>P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B)</code></p><p>P(both machines work) = 0.95 x 0.95 = 0.9025.</p><p><strong>Dependent events:</strong> one event changes the odds of the other. The classic business case is sampling <em>without replacement</em>: a box has 10 parts and 3 are defective. P(first is defective) = 3/10. If the first was defective, only 2 defective parts remain among 9, so P(second defective | first defective) = 2/9. Then</p><p><code>P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B given A)</code></p><p>P(both defective) = 3/10 x 2/9 = 6/90 = 0.0667.</p><p><strong>Conditional probability</strong> is the probability of A <em>given that</em> B already happened. It is written P(A | B) and read 'probability of A given B':</p><p><code>P(A | B) = P(A and B) / P(B)</code></p><p><strong>Test for independence:</strong> A and B are independent if P(A | B) = P(A), or equivalently if P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B). If P(A) = 0.30, P(B) = 0.50, and P(A and B) = 0.15, then 0.30 x 0.50 = 0.15, so they are independent.</p><p><strong>Warning:</strong> 'mutually exclusive' and 'independent' are NOT the same. Mutually exclusive events are actually highly dependent (if one happens, the other cannot).</p>"},{"h":"5. Reading a contingency (two-way) table","body":"<p>A <strong>contingency table</strong> counts items by two categories at once. It is the easiest place to calculate probabilities, because you just divide counts. Suppose a factory records 1,000 units:</p><ul><li>Day shift made 600 units: 24 defective, 576 good.</li><li>Night shift made 400 units: 36 defective, 364 good.</li><li>Column totals: 60 defective, 940 good, 1,000 overall.</li></ul><p>Three kinds of probabilities come from a table:</p><ol><li><strong>Marginal</strong> (one category only, use a total): P(defective) = 60/1,000 = 0.06.</li><li><strong>Joint</strong> ('and', use one cell): P(night and defective) = 36/1,000 = 0.036.</li><li><strong>Conditional</strong> ('given', use the row or column total as the denominator): P(defective | night) = 36/400 = 0.09. This restricts attention to just the night shift.</li></ol><p><strong>The key trick:</strong> the word 'given' tells you the denominator. 'Given night shift' means divide by the night shift total (400), not by the grand total. The reverse is a different question: P(night | defective) = 36/60 = 0.60. So 60% of defects come from the night shift even though night makes only 40% of units. That suggests the shifts are <em>not</em> independent of defect rate.</p>"},{"h":"6. Expected value (the long-run average)","body":"<p><strong>Expected value (EV)</strong> is the probability-weighted average of all possible outcomes. It is what you would earn on average if you could repeat the decision many, many times. Multiply each outcome by its probability, then add:</p><p><code>EV = sum of (outcome x probability)</code></p><p><strong>Business example:</strong> A company considers a project with these outcomes:</p><ul><li>30% chance of a $200,000 profit: 0.30 x 200,000 = 60,000</li><li>50% chance of a $50,000 profit: 0.50 x 50,000 = 25,000</li><li>20% chance of a $100,000 loss: 0.20 x (-100,000) = -20,000</li></ul><p>EV = 60,000 + 25,000 - 20,000 = <strong>$65,000</strong>. The project will never actually pay exactly $65,000; that is the average over many similar projects.</p><p><strong>How it is used:</strong> compare options (choose the larger EV), price warranties and insurance, decide whether a marketing campaign is worth its cost. Remember to subtract any cost to get <em>net</em> expected value. A $10,000 campaign with an expected revenue of $15,000 has a net EV of $5,000.</p><p>Probabilities in an EV calculation must add up to 1 (or 100%).</p>"},{"h":"7. Counting basics: permutations vs combinations","body":"<p>Sometimes you need to count outcomes before you can find a probability. Two tools handle most cases. The symbol <strong>n!</strong> ('n factorial') means n x (n-1) x ... x 1, and 0! = 1.</p><p><strong>Permutation: order matters.</strong> Use it when you are ranking or assigning distinct roles (gold, silver, bronze; president, treasurer).</p><p><code>P(n, r) = n! / (n - r)!</code></p><p>Example: 5 candidates, award 1st, 2nd and 3rd place: P(5, 3) = 5 x 4 x 3 = <strong>60</strong>.</p><p><strong>Combination: order does not matter.</strong> Use it when you are just choosing a group (a 3-person committee, a sample of 10 items).</p><p><code>C(n, r) = n! / (r! x (n - r)!)</code></p><p>Example: choose a 3-person committee from 8 employees: C(8, 3) = (8 x 7 x 6) / (3 x 2 x 1) = 336 / 6 = <strong>56</strong>.</p><p><strong>How to decide:</strong> ask, 'If I swap two chosen items, do I get a different result?' If yes, it is a permutation. If no, it is a combination. Because a group of 3 can be arranged in 6 ways, a combination is always the permutation divided by r! (here 336 / 6 = 56). Permutations are always at least as large as combinations.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Probability is a number between zero and one. Zero means impossible, one means certain, and a probability can never be negative or larger than one.","Classical probability counts equally likely outcomes, empirical probability uses past data, and subjective probability is an educated guess or judgment.","The sample space is the list of all possible outcomes, and an event is any group of those outcomes that you care about.","The complement rule says the probability that an event does not happen equals one minus the probability that it does happen.","For the phrase at least one, find the probability of none and subtract it from one.","The addition rule finds the probability of A or B. For mutually exclusive events you add the probabilities. If the events can overlap, add them and then subtract the probability of both.","The multiplication rule finds the probability of A and B. For independent events you multiply the two probabilities. For dependent events you multiply the first probability by the conditional probability of the second.","Events are independent when knowing one happened does not change the chance of the other. Mutually exclusive events are not independent, because if one happens the other cannot.","Conditional probability is the chance of A given that B has already happened. It equals the probability of A and B divided by the probability of B.","In a two-way table, the word given tells you which row or column total to use as the denominator.","Expected value is the probability-weighted average of all outcomes. Multiply each outcome by its probability and add the results. Businesses use it to compare risky options.","Use a permutation when order matters and a combination when order does not matter. For the same numbers, the permutation count is always at least as large as the combination count."],"examples":[{"q":"A retailer finds that P(customer buys online) = 0.60, P(customer buys in store) = 0.45, and P(customer buys both online and in store) = 0.20. What is the probability a customer buys online or in store (or both), and what is the probability the customer buys through neither channel?","steps":["These events can happen together (0.20 overlap), so they are NOT mutually exclusive. Use the general addition rule.","P(online or store) = P(online) + P(store) - P(both)","P(online or store) = 0.60 + 0.45 - 0.20 = 0.85","'Neither channel' is the complement of 'online or store'.","P(neither) = 1 - 0.85 = 0.15"],"answer":"P(online or store) = 0.85; P(neither) = 0.15."},{"q":"A factory made 1,000 units. Day shift: 600 units with 24 defective. Night shift: 400 units with 36 defective. (a) What is P(defective)? (b) What is P(defective given night shift)? (c) If a unit is defective, what is the probability it came from night shift? (d) Is defect status independent of shift?","steps":["Total defective = 24 + 36 = 60. Total units = 1,000.","(a) P(defective) = 60/1,000 = 0.06","(b) 'Given night' means the denominator is the night total of 400. P(defective | night) = 36/400 = 0.09","(c) 'Given defective' means the denominator is the defective total of 60. P(night | defective) = 36/60 = 0.60","(d) For independence P(defective | night) would equal P(defective). 0.09 is not equal to 0.06, so they are not independent."],"answer":"(a) 0.06; (b) 0.09; (c) 0.60; (d) Not independent, since night shift has a higher defect rate (9% vs 6% overall)."},{"q":"A company is deciding on a project. There is a 30% chance of a $200,000 profit, a 50% chance of a $50,000 profit, and a 20% chance of a $100,000 loss. (a) What is the expected value? (b) The project costs nothing extra to run, but an alternative safe investment is guaranteed to return $60,000. Which should the company choose based on expected value?","steps":["Check that probabilities add to 1: 0.30 + 0.50 + 0.20 = 1.00. Good.","Multiply each outcome by its probability: 0.30 x 200,000 = 60,000; 0.50 x 50,000 = 25,000; 0.20 x (-100,000) = -20,000.","Add: 60,000 + 25,000 - 20,000 = 65,000.","Compare: 65,000 (project) vs 60,000 (safe investment).","Project has the higher expected value, so choose the project if the company is willing to accept the risk of a loss."],"answer":"EV = $65,000, which is higher than the $60,000 safe option, so the project is favored on expected value alone."}],"videos":[{"title":"Intro to probability: the basics, rules, and the 0 to 1 scale","query":"Khan Academy probability introduction basic rules"},{"title":"Conditional probability and independence explained with two-way tables","query":"conditional probability two-way table explained statistics"},{"title":"Permutations vs combinations in plain English","query":"permutations vs combinations explained simply"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"What range of values can a probability take?","b":"0 to 1 inclusive. 0 = impossible, 1 = certain. Never negative, never above 1."},{"f":"Classical probability","b":"Theoretical probability based on counting equally likely outcomes (fair die: 1/6)."},{"f":"Empirical probability","b":"Probability based on observed data: (times event happened) / (total observations)."},{"f":"Subjective probability","b":"Probability based on judgment, opinion, or experience rather than data or counting."},{"f":"Sample space","b":"The set of all possible outcomes of a random process."},{"f":"Event","b":"A specific outcome or group of outcomes from the sample space that you care about."},{"f":"Complement rule","b":"P(not A) = 1 - P(A)."},{"f":"How do you handle 'at least one' problems?","b":"Compute P(none), then subtract from 1."},{"f":"Mutually exclusive events","b":"Events that cannot both occur at the same time, so P(A and B) = 0."},{"f":"Addition rule when events are mutually exclusive","b":"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)."},{"f":"General addition rule (not mutually exclusive)","b":"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)."},{"f":"Independent events","b":"One event occurring does not change the probability of the other: P(A | B) = P(A)."},{"f":"Multiplication rule for independent events","b":"P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B)."},{"f":"Multiplication rule for dependent events","b":"P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B | A)."},{"f":"Conditional probability formula","b":"P(A | B) = P(A and B) / P(B). The probability of A given B has happened."},{"f":"In a two-way table, what does the word 'given' tell you?","b":"Use that row or column total as the denominator, not the grand total."},{"f":"Are mutually exclusive events independent?","b":"No. If one happens the other cannot, so they are dependent (unless one has probability 0)."},{"f":"Expected value formula","b":"EV = sum of (each outcome x its probability)."},{"f":"Business use of expected value","b":"Compare risky options, price warranties and insurance, and decide if a campaign or project is worth its cost."},{"f":"Permutation vs combination","b":"Permutation: order matters (rankings, roles). Combination: order does not matter (committees, samples)."},{"f":"Formula for C(n, r)","b":"n! / (r! x (n - r)!). Example: C(8,3) = 56."},{"f":"Formula for P(n, r)","b":"n! / (n - r)!. Example: P(5,3) = 60."}],"questions":[{"q":"A data analyst reports several calculated probabilities. Which of the following values could NOT be a valid probability?","o":["0.35","0","1.2","1"],"a":2,"e":"A probability must be between 0 and 1 inclusive. 1.2 is greater than 1, so it is impossible. 0 (impossible event) and 1 (certain event) are valid boundaries, which is the tempting trap in options B and D.","d":2},{"q":"A sales director says, 'Based on my experience with this client, I believe there is a 70% chance they will renew.' What type of probability is this?","o":["Classical","Empirical","Complement","Subjective"],"a":3,"e":"A probability based on personal judgment or experience rather than formal data or equally likely outcomes is subjective. It is not empirical because no relative frequency from recorded data was used, and not classical because there are no equally likely outcomes to count.","d":2},{"q":"Last quarter a warehouse shipped 400 orders and 28 arrived late. Using this record to estimate the chance that the next order is late gives which empirical probability?","o":["0.28","0.07","0.93","0.72"],"a":1,"e":"Empirical probability = 28/400 = 0.07. The value 0.93 is the complement (the probability an order is on time), which is a tempting wrong answer.","d":2},{"q":"Each of 3 independent parts has a 10% chance of being defective. What is the probability that at least one of the three is defective?","o":["0.300","0.271","0.729","0.100"],"a":1,"e":"P(none defective) = 0.9 x 0.9 x 0.9 = 0.729, so P(at least one defective) = 1 - 0.729 = 0.271. 0.729 is the probability of NO defects. 0.300 simply adds 0.1 three times, which ignores overlap.","d":2},{"q":"In a retail study, P(buys online) = 0.60, P(buys in store) = 0.45, and P(buys both) = 0.20. What is the probability a customer buys online or in store?","o":["1.05","0.65","0.25","0.85"],"a":3,"e":"The events overlap, so use P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B) = 0.60 + 0.45 - 0.20 = 0.85. Simply adding gives 1.05, which is impossible and shows the overlap was double-counted.","d":2},{"q":"An order is either small (probability 0.25), medium (0.45), or large. Orders cannot be two sizes at once. What is the probability an order is small or medium?","o":["0.20","0.55","0.70","0.1125"],"a":2,"e":"Small and medium are mutually exclusive, so P(small or medium) = 0.25 + 0.45 = 0.70. 0.1125 would be the product, which is the rule for 'and' with independent events, not for 'or'.","d":2},{"q":"A production line has 3 machines that operate independently. Each is working 95% of the time. What is the probability all three are working at the same time?","o":["0.857","0.950","0.143","0.995"],"a":0,"e":"For independent events, multiply: 0.95 x 0.95 x 0.95 = 0.857. 0.143 is the complement (probability that at least one is down). 0.950 ignores the other machines.","d":2},{"q":"A survey of 200 customers is summarized: East region (100 customers): 90 satisfied, 10 not satisfied. West region (100 customers): 60 satisfied, 40 not satisfied. What is the probability a customer is satisfied given that the customer is in the West region?","o":["0.30","0.40","0.75","0.60"],"a":3,"e":"'Given West' means the denominator is the West total of 100. P(satisfied | West) = 60/100 = 0.60. 0.30 is the joint probability (60/200), and 0.75 is the overall satisfaction rate (150/200).","d":2},{"q":"For two events, P(A) = 0.30, P(B) = 0.50, and P(A and B) = 0.15. What conclusion is correct?","o":["The events are mutually exclusive","The events are independent","The events are dependent","The events are complements"],"a":1,"e":"P(A) x P(B) = 0.30 x 0.50 = 0.15, which equals P(A and B), so the events are independent. They are not mutually exclusive because P(A and B) is not 0.","d":2},{"q":"A bin holds 10 parts, 3 of which are defective. Two parts are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are defective?","o":["0.0900","0.3000","0.0667","0.0556"],"a":2,"e":"Drawing without replacement makes the draws dependent: 3/10 x 2/9 = 6/90 = 0.0667. 0.09 (3/10 x 3/10) wrongly treats the draws as independent.","d":2},{"q":"A marketing campaign costs $10,000. There is a 40% chance it brings in $30,000 of revenue and a 60% chance it brings in $5,000 of revenue. What is the expected net gain from the campaign?","o":["$5,000","$15,000","$17,500","$10,000"],"a":0,"e":"Expected revenue = 0.40 x 30,000 + 0.60 x 5,000 = 12,000 + 3,000 = 15,000. Net = 15,000 - 10,000 = $5,000. $15,000 forgets to subtract cost, and $17,500 wrongly averages the two outcomes without using the probabilities.","d":2},{"q":"A manager must pick a 3-person committee from 8 employees. All members have equal roles. How many different committees are possible?","o":["336","24","56","512"],"a":2,"e":"Order does not matter for a committee with equal roles, so use a combination: C(8,3) = 8x7x6 / (3x2x1) = 56. 336 is the permutation P(8,3), which would count the same group in different orders as different.","d":2},{"q":"Five finalists compete for an award. First, second and third place are each given to a different finalist. How many different ways can the three places be awarded?","o":["10","60","125","15"],"a":1,"e":"Rank matters, so use a permutation: P(5,3) = 5 x 4 x 3 = 60. 10 is the combination C(5,3), which would ignore who got which place.","d":2},{"q":"Using the same survey (East: 90 satisfied, 10 not; West: 60 satisfied, 40 not), a customer is randomly chosen from those who were NOT satisfied. What is the probability the customer is from the West?","o":["0.20","0.40","0.25","0.80"],"a":3,"e":"'Given not satisfied' limits the group to 10 + 40 = 50 customers. P(West | not satisfied) = 40/50 = 0.80. 0.20 is the East share of this group (10/50), and 0.40 is the not-satisfied rate within West (40/100), which answers the reverse question.","d":2},{"q":"A fair coin is flipped twice. What is the probability of getting at least one head?","o":["0.25","0.50","0.75","1.00"],"a":2,"e":"The sample space is HH, HT, TH, TT. Only TT has no head, so P(at least one head) = 1 - 1/4 = 0.75. 0.25 is the probability of two tails.","d":2},{"q":"A hospital's records show the probability that an emergency-room patient waits more than 30 minutes is 0.18. What is the probability that a randomly selected patient waits 30 minutes or less?","o":["0.18","0.09","0.82","1.18"],"a":2,"e":"Complement rule: P(not A) = 1 - P(A) = 1 - 0.18 = 0.82. The tempting 0.18 is the probability of the event itself (waiting more than 30 minutes), not its complement; 1.18 is impossible because probabilities cannot exceed 1.","d":1},{"q":"A raffle prize will be awarded to one of 50 employees chosen at random. Twelve of the employees work in Sales. What is the probability that the winner works in Sales?","o":["0.24","0.12","0.76","0.02"],"a":0,"e":"Classical probability = favorable outcomes / total equally likely outcomes = 12/50 = 0.24. The tempting 0.76 is 38/50, the chance the winner is NOT in Sales, and 0.02 is the chance any one specific employee wins.","d":1},{"q":"A company has 500 employees. 200 of them work in Sales, and 60 of those Sales employees work remotely. What is the probability that a randomly chosen employee works in Sales AND works remotely?","o":["0.30","0.40","0.88","0.12"],"a":3,"e":"A joint ('and') probability from a table uses the whole group as the denominator: 60/500 = 0.12. The tempting 0.30 = 60/200 is the conditional probability P(remote | Sales); it reuses the same 60 but divides by the Sales group only.","d":1},{"q":"The number of machine breakdowns per day at a plant has this distribution: 0 breakdowns (probability 0.50), 1 (0.30), 2 (0.15), 3 (0.05). What is the expected number of breakdowns per day?","o":["1.50","0.75","0.50","1.00"],"a":1,"e":"E(X) = sum of x times P(x) = 0(0.50) + 1(0.30) + 2(0.15) + 3(0.05) = 0.30 + 0.30 + 0.15 = 0.75. The tempting 1.50 is the plain average of 0, 1, 2 and 3, which ignores that low values are far more likely; 0.50 is just the most likely value, not the mean.","d":1},{"q":"A plant inspected 600 units. Day shift made 400 units, of which 20 were defective. Night shift made 200 units, of which 20 were defective. One unit is chosen at random from the 40 defective units. What is the probability it came from the night shift?","o":["0.10","0.50","0.033","0.333"],"a":1,"e":"Condition on 'defective': 20 of the 40 defective units came from night shift, so P(night | defective) = 20/40 = 0.50. The tempting 0.10 = 20/200 is the reversed conditional P(defective | night). 0.033 is the joint 20/600 and 0.333 is P(night) = 200/600.","d":2},{"q":"Among 120 vaccinated employees, 12 caught the flu. Among 80 unvaccinated employees, 16 caught the flu. Which statement is correct?","o":["Flu and vaccination status are independent, because only 28 of the 200 employees caught the flu.","Flu and vaccination status are independent, because the flu counts (12 and 16) are close to each other.","Flu and vaccination status are not independent, because there are more vaccinated than unvaccinated employees.","Flu and vaccination status are not independent, because P(flu | vaccinated) = 0.10 but P(flu | unvaccinated) = 0.20."],"a":3,"e":"Independence requires P(flu | group) to equal P(flu) = 28/200 = 0.14 for every group. Here the rates are 12/120 = 0.10 and 16/80 = 0.20, so they differ. The tempting 'counts are close' answer compares raw counts from groups of different sizes; always compare rates.","d":2},{"q":"At an electronics store, P(customer buys a laptop) = 0.40 and P(customer buys a laptop AND a warranty) = 0.12. What is the probability a customer buys a warranty, given that the customer bought a laptop?","o":["0.30","0.12","0.28","0.048"],"a":0,"e":"P(warranty | laptop) = P(laptop and warranty) / P(laptop) = 0.12 / 0.40 = 0.30. The tempting 0.048 = 0.40 x 0.12 multiplies the two numbers (which would only make sense for independent events and answers a different question); 0.12 is the joint probability, not the conditional.","d":2},{"q":"A retailer sells a $60 extended warranty. On 8% of warranties sold, the customer makes a claim that costs the retailer $400; on the rest there is no cost. What is the retailer's expected profit per warranty sold?","o":["$32","$60","$28","$340"],"a":2,"e":"Expected profit = revenue - expected claim cost = 60 - (0.08)(400) = 60 - 32 = $28. The tempting $32 is only the expected claim cost, and $60 ignores claims entirely. $340 wrongly subtracts a full $400 claim from the $60 fee.","d":2},{"q":"A warehouse labels bins with a code made of two letters (A-Z) followed by three digits (0-9), such as 'KT482'. Letters and digits may repeat. How many different codes are possible?","o":["468,000","67,600","82","676,000"],"a":3,"e":"Multiplication principle: 26 x 26 x 10 x 10 x 10 = 676,000. The tempting 468,000 = 26 x 25 x 10 x 9 x 8 assumes no repeated characters, which the problem allows. 82 comes from adding (26 + 26 + 10 + 10 + 10) instead of multiplying.","d":2},{"q":"A manager will interview two candidates chosen at random, one after the other without replacement, from a pool of 5 candidates: 2 internal and 3 external. What is the probability that the first interviewee is internal AND the second is external?","o":["0.30","0.24","0.40","0.75"],"a":0,"e":"The draws are dependent: P(internal first) = 2/5, then 4 candidates remain, 3 of them external, so P(external second | internal first) = 3/4. Product = 6/20 = 0.30. The tempting 0.24 = (2/5)(3/5) treats the draws as independent, as if the first candidate were put back.","d":2},{"q":"A compliance audit consists of 4 independent checks. Each check passes with probability 0.85. What is the probability that at least one check fails?","o":["0.522","0.478","0.600","0.150"],"a":1,"e":"Use the complement of 'none fail': P(all pass) = 0.85^4 = 0.522, so P(at least one fails) = 1 - 0.522 = 0.478. The tempting 0.522 is the probability that all four pass. 0.600 = 4 x 0.15 wrongly adds the failure probabilities, double counting audits where several checks fail.","d":3},{"q":"Events A and B are mutually exclusive, with P(A) = 0.30 and P(B) = 0.40. Which statement is correct?","o":["A and B are independent, because mutually exclusive events never influence each other.","A and B are independent, because P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B) = 0.12.","A and B are not independent, because P(A and B) = 0 but P(A) x P(B) = 0.12.","P(A or B) = 0.58, because the overlap must be subtracted."],"a":2,"e":"Independence requires P(A and B) = P(A)P(B). Mutually exclusive events with positive probabilities have P(A and B) = 0, which is not 0.12, so they are dependent: if A happens, B cannot. The tempting 'never influence each other' idea is exactly backwards. Also P(A or B) = 0.30 + 0.40 = 0.70 because there is no overlap to subtract.","d":3},{"q":"A plant buys parts from two suppliers. Supplier A provides 60% of the parts and 2% of its parts are defective. Supplier B provides 40% of the parts and 5% of its parts are defective. A randomly selected part is found to be defective. What is the probability it came from Supplier B?","o":["0.625","0.400","0.050","0.375"],"a":0,"e":"P(defective) = 0.60(0.02) + 0.40(0.05) = 0.012 + 0.020 = 0.032. P(B | defective) = 0.020 / 0.032 = 0.625. The tempting 0.400 is B's share of all parts before we know the part is defective; the defect evidence raises it. 0.375 is the probability the defective part came from Supplier A.","d":3},{"q":"A team of 3 people is chosen at random from 6 nurses and 4 doctors (10 people in total). What is the probability that all 3 team members are nurses?","o":["0.216","0.600","0.250","1/6 (about 0.167)"],"a":3,"e":"Order does not matter, so count combinations: C(6,3) / C(10,3) = 20 / 120 = 1/6, about 0.167. The tempting 0.216 = 0.6^3 treats each pick as independent with a 60% chance of a nurse, ignoring that people are not replaced. 0.600 is only the share of nurses in the pool.","d":3},{"q":"Two independent machines feed a line. Machine 1 is working 90% of the time and Machine 2 is working 80% of the time. What is the probability that exactly one of the two machines is working?","o":["0.72","0.26","0.98","0.10"],"a":1,"e":"Exactly one = (M1 works and M2 fails) + (M1 fails and M2 works) = (0.9)(0.2) + (0.1)(0.8) = 0.18 + 0.08 = 0.26. The tempting 0.98 is 'at least one works' (1 - 0.1 x 0.2), which also includes the case where both work; 0.72 is the probability both work.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Sample space","List of all possible outcomes"],["Complement rule","P(not A) = 1 minus P(A)"],["Mutually exclusive","Events that cannot occur together"],["Independent events","One does not change the other's chance"],["Conditional probability","Chance of A given B occurred"],["Expected value","Probability-weighted average outcome"],["Permutation","Arrangement where order matters"],["Combination","Selection where order is ignored"]],"traps":["Trap: Mutually exclusive events are also independent. Truth: exclusive events with positive probabilities are dependent, because one happening rules out the other; independent means P(A and B) = P(A) x P(B).","Trap: P(A given B) equals P(B given A). Truth: they share a numerator but have different denominators (the given group), so they usually differ.","Trap: For 'A or B', just add the two probabilities. Truth: subtract the overlap, P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B), unless the events are mutually exclusive.","Trap: Draws without replacement can be multiplied as if they were independent. Truth: each draw changes the pool, so the second probability must be recomputed from what is left.","Trap: 'At least one' means add up the individual chances. Truth: compute 1 - P(none); adding double counts outcomes where several events occur."],"mnemonic":"After 'given', shrink the denominator: P(A | B) = (A and B) / B. 'And' multiplies, 'or' adds minus overlap, 'at least one' is 1 minus none.","cram":["Probabilities range from 0 to 1; complement rule: P(not A) = 1 - P(A).","P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B).","P(A | B) = P(A and B) / P(B); given group is denominator","Independent means P(A | B) = P(A), or P(A and B) = P(A)P(B).","Mutually exclusive events (positive probability) are always dependent, never independent.","Expected value = sum of each outcome times its probability.","Order matters: permutation nPr. Order ignored: combination nCr = nPr / r!.","At least one = 1 - P(none); without replacement, the denominators shrink."],"ytv":[{"title":"Probability Part 1: Rules and Patterns: Crash Course Statistics #13","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"OyddY7DlV58","why":"Core probability rules: addition, multiplication, complements and independence."},{"title":"Calculating conditional probability","channel":"Khan Academy","id":"6xPkG2pA-TU","why":"Worked examples of P(A | B) with the given group as the denominator."},{"title":"Compound probability of independent events","channel":"Khan Academy","id":"xSc4oLA9e8o","why":"How to multiply probabilities of independent events and check independence."}]},{"id":"u6","title":"The Normal Distribution & Z-Scores","day":3,"summary":"How probability distributions describe data, with a focus on binomial basics, the bell-shaped normal curve, the empirical rule, and using z-scores to find percentiles, compare values, and flag unusual results.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Distributions: discrete vs continuous","body":"<p>A <strong>probability distribution</strong> shows all the values a variable can take and how likely each value (or range of values) is. Think of it as a 'map of where the data tends to land.'</p><p>The first question to ask is what kind of variable you have:</p><ul><li><strong>Discrete variable:</strong> you can <em>count</em> the possible values, and there are gaps between them. Examples: number of defects on a circuit board (0, 1, 2, ...), number of customers in line, number of units sold today. You can find the probability of an exact value, such as P(exactly 2 defects).</li><li><strong>Continuous variable:</strong> it can take <em>any</em> value in a range, including decimals. Examples: time to assemble a unit (7.43 minutes), weight of a package, temperature, revenue in dollars. For a continuous variable the probability of one exact value is essentially 0, so we ask about <em>ranges</em>: P(time is between 7 and 8 minutes). Probability is the <strong>area under the curve</strong> over that range, and the total area under the curve is 1.</li></ul><p><strong>Quick test:</strong> if you got it by counting, it is discrete. If you got it by measuring, it is continuous.</p>"},{"h":"2. Binomial basics","body":"<p>The <strong>binomial distribution</strong> is the most important discrete distribution. It counts the number of 'successes' in a fixed number of tries. A situation is binomial when all four conditions hold:</p><ol><li>There is a <strong>fixed number of trials</strong>, n (for example, 10 parts inspected).</li><li>Each trial has <strong>only two outcomes</strong>: success or failure (defective or not). 'Success' just means the outcome you are counting.</li><li>The <strong>probability of success, p, is the same</strong> on every trial.</li><li>The trials are <strong>independent</strong> (one result does not change another).</li></ol><p><strong>Business example:</strong> a supplier's parts are defective 10% of the time (p = 0.10). You inspect n = 10 parts. The number of defective parts follows a binomial distribution.</p><ul><li>Mean (expected number of successes) = <code>n x p</code> = 10 x 0.10 = 1.0 defective part.</li><li>Standard deviation = <code>square root of (n x p x (1 - p))</code> = square root of (10 x 0.10 x 0.90) = square root of 0.9, which is about 0.95.</li><li>P(no defects) = 0.9^10 = about 0.349. P(exactly 1 defect) = 10 x 0.1 x 0.9^9 = about 0.387.</li></ul><p>You will rarely have to compute individual binomial probabilities on this exam. Be ready to (a) recognize a binomial situation and (b) find its mean with n x p. Counting the time a task takes is NOT binomial; it is not a count of successes in fixed trials.</p>"},{"h":"3. The normal curve and the empirical rule","body":"<p>The <strong>normal distribution</strong> is the famous bell curve. It describes many continuous measurements in business and nature: package weights, call-handling times, test scores, and measurement errors. Its properties:</p><ul><li>It is <strong>symmetric</strong> and bell-shaped.</li><li>The <strong>mean, median, and mode are all equal</strong> and sit at the center peak.</li><li>It is fully described by two numbers: the <strong>mean</strong> (center) and the <strong>standard deviation</strong> (spread).</li><li>The tails get closer and closer to the horizontal axis but never quite touch it.</li><li>The total area under the curve is 1.</li></ul><p>The <strong>empirical rule (68-95-99.7 rule)</strong> tells you what share of data falls within 1, 2, and 3 standard deviations of the mean:</p><ul><li>About <strong>68%</strong> within 1 standard deviation (mean +/- 1 sd)</li><li>About <strong>95%</strong> within 2 standard deviations</li><li>About <strong>99.7%</strong> within 3 standard deviations</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> a call center's handle time is normal with mean 8 minutes and standard deviation 2 minutes.</p><ul><li>68% of calls last between 6 and 10 minutes.</li><li>95% last between 4 and 12 minutes.</li><li>99.7% last between 2 and 14 minutes.</li></ul><p>Because 5% of calls fall outside 4 to 12 minutes and the curve is symmetric, half of that 5% is in each tail. So about <strong>2.5%</strong> of calls last longer than 12 minutes. And since 68% is centered, 16% is in each tail beyond 1 sd: about 16% of calls last longer than 10 minutes.</p>"},{"h":"4. The z-score","body":"<p>A <strong>z-score</strong> tells you how many standard deviations a value is from the mean. It converts any value into a common, unit-free scale.</p><p><code>z = (x - mean) / standard deviation</code></p><p><strong>How to read it:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>z = 0</strong>: the value equals the mean.</li><li><strong>Positive z</strong>: above the mean. <strong>Negative z</strong>: below the mean. The sign gives direction, the size gives distance.</li><li><strong>z = 1.5</strong> means 1.5 standard deviations above the mean. <strong>z = -2</strong> means 2 standard deviations below.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> exam scores have mean 70 and standard deviation 10. A score of 85 has z = (85 - 70) / 10 = <strong>1.5</strong>, so it is 1.5 standard deviations above average.</p><p>You can also go backward: <code>x = mean + z x standard deviation</code>. A value with z = -2 on a scale with mean 500 and standard deviation 30 is 500 + (-2)(30) = 440.</p><p>Z-scores keep the shape of the distribution. If the data are approximately normal, the z-scores follow the <strong>standard normal distribution</strong>, which has mean 0 and standard deviation 1.</p>"},{"h":"5. Using z to find percent above or below","body":"<p>For a normal distribution, a z-score tells you the <strong>percentile</strong>: the percent of values below that point. A z-table (or calculator or Excel's NORM.S.DIST) gives these. Memorize the common ones:</p><ul><li><strong>z = 0</strong>: 50th percentile (50% below)</li><li><strong>z = 1</strong>: about 84% below (0.8413) and 16% above</li><li><strong>z = 2</strong>: about 97.7% below (0.9772) and 2.3% above</li><li><strong>z = 3</strong>: about 99.87% below</li><li><strong>z = -1</strong>: about 16% below (0.1587)</li><li><strong>z = -2</strong>: about 2.3% below (0.0228)</li><li><strong>z = 1.5</strong>: about 93.3% below; <strong>z = -1.5</strong>: about 6.7% below</li><li><strong>z = 1.645</strong>: about 95% below; <strong>z = 1.96</strong>: about 97.5% below</li></ul><p><strong>Symmetry shortcut:</strong> the curve is symmetric, so the area below -z equals the area above +z. Also 'percent above' = 100% minus 'percent below.'</p><p><strong>Steps for any problem:</strong></p><ol><li>Compute z.</li><li>Look up (or recall) the percent below that z.</li><li>If the question asks for 'above,' subtract from 100%. If it asks for 'between,' subtract the two percentiles.</li></ol><p><strong>Example:</strong> exam mean 70, sd 10. A score of 85 has z = 1.5, which is the 93.3rd percentile. So 93.3% scored lower and 6.7% scored higher. The empirical rule is the quick estimate; the z-table is the precise version for in-between values like 1.5.</p>"},{"h":"6. Comparing different distributions and spotting unusual values","body":"<p>You cannot directly compare a $95,000 sales total to a 52-unit sales count; the scales differ. But you <em>can</em> compare their z-scores, because z measures 'how far above or below typical, in standard deviations.' The higher z indicates the relatively stronger result.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Store X sold $52,000 in a market with mean $40,000 and standard deviation $6,000, so z = (52,000 - 40,000) / 6,000 = <strong>2.0</strong>. Store Y sold $95,000 in a market with mean $80,000 and standard deviation $10,000, so z = (95,000 - 80,000) / 10,000 = <strong>1.5</strong>. Store Y has larger dollar sales, but Store X performed better <em>relative to its own market</em>.</p><p><strong>Unusual values (outliers).</strong> A common rule of thumb:</p><ul><li><strong>|z| greater than 2</strong> (more than 2 standard deviations from the mean) is considered <strong>unusual</strong>. Only about 5% of normal data is this far out.</li><li><strong>|z| greater than 3</strong> is considered <strong>very unusual</strong> (an extreme outlier). Only about 0.3% of normal data is this far out.</li></ul><p>The vertical bars mean absolute value: ignore the sign. A part weighing 490 g when the mean is 500 g and the standard deviation is 4 g has z = (490 - 500) / 4 = <strong>-2.5</strong>. That is unusual: it is worth checking whether the machine drifted.</p>"},{"h":"7. Uniform and skewed distributions","body":"<p>Not all data are bell-shaped. Know these two other shapes:</p><p><strong>Uniform distribution.</strong> Every value in a range is equally likely, so the graph is a flat rectangle. Example: a customer's wait for a shuttle that arrives at a random time within a 10-minute window is uniform from 0 to 10 minutes. Probabilities are just proportions of the range: P(wait under 2.5 minutes) = 2.5 / 10 = 0.25. The mean is the midpoint (5 minutes).</p><p><strong>Skewed distribution.</strong> The data are lopsided with a long tail on one side.</p><ul><li><strong>Right-skewed (positively skewed):</strong> a long tail to the right. Typical of incomes, home prices, and customer order sizes (a few very large values). The <strong>mean is greater than the median</strong> because the big values pull the mean up.</li><li><strong>Left-skewed (negatively skewed):</strong> a long tail to the left. Typical of exam scores on an easy test or age at retirement. The <strong>mean is less than the median</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Memory trick:</strong> the mean is dragged toward the tail. Because of that, for skewed data the median is often a better 'typical value.'</p><p><strong>Important:</strong> the empirical rule (68-95-99.7) and normal z-table percentiles only apply when the data are approximately normal. For a skewed distribution, don't use them for individual values. (A later unit shows that <em>averages</em> of samples become approximately normal even when the raw data are skewed.)</p>"}],"keyPoints":["A discrete variable is something you count, such as the number of defects. A continuous variable is something you measure, such as time or weight, and it can take any value in a range.","For a continuous variable, probability is the area under the curve over a range of values, and the total area under the curve equals one.","A binomial situation has a fixed number of trials, only two outcomes on each trial, the same probability of success every time, and independent trials, and its mean equals the number of trials times the probability of success.","The normal distribution is a symmetric bell shape where the mean, median, and mode are all equal and sit at the center.","The empirical rule says about 68 percent of values fall within one standard deviation of the mean, about 95 percent within two, and about 99 point 7 percent within three.","A z-score equals the value minus the mean, divided by the standard deviation. It tells you how many standard deviations a value is above or below the mean.","A positive z-score is above the mean, a negative z-score is below the mean, and a z-score of zero is exactly at the mean.","A z-score of one is about the 84th percentile, a z-score of two is about the 97 point 7th percentile, and a z-score of negative one is about the 16th percentile.","To find the percent above a value, find the percent below and subtract it from 100 percent. Because the curve is symmetric, the area below negative z equals the area above positive z.","Z-scores let you compare values from different distributions. The larger z-score shows the stronger result relative to its own group.","A value is considered unusual if its z-score is more than 2 away from zero in either direction, and very unusual if it is more than 3 away.","In a uniform distribution every value in the range is equally likely. In a right-skewed distribution the mean is greater than the median, and in a left-skewed distribution the mean is less than the median."],"examples":[{"q":"A call center's handle time is approximately normal with a mean of 8 minutes and a standard deviation of 2 minutes. Using the empirical rule, (a) what range contains the middle 95% of calls? (b) What percent of calls take longer than 12 minutes? (c) What percent take between 6 and 12 minutes?","steps":["(a) 95% is within 2 standard deviations. Mean +/- 2(sd) = 8 +/- 4, so the range is 4 to 12 minutes.","(b) 12 minutes is 2 standard deviations above the mean. 95% is in the middle, so 5% is in the two tails combined. By symmetry each tail holds 2.5%. So 2.5% of calls take longer than 12 minutes.","(c) 6 minutes is 1 sd below the mean and 12 minutes is 2 sd above. From the mean to 1 sd is 34%, and from the mean to 2 sd is 47.5% (half of 95%).","Add the two pieces: 34% + 47.5% = 81.5%."],"answer":"(a) 4 to 12 minutes; (b) about 2.5%; (c) about 81.5%."},{"q":"Exam scores for a training program have mean 70 and standard deviation 10. (a) Find the z-score and approximate percentile for a score of 85. (b) A different measure, part weight, has mean 500 g and standard deviation 4 g. A part weighs 490 g. Find its z-score and decide whether it is unusual.","steps":["(a) z = (85 - 70) / 10 = 15 / 10 = 1.5.","A z of 1.5 corresponds to about 0.9332 below, so the 93rd percentile. About 6.7% of scores are higher.","(b) z = (490 - 500) / 4 = -10 / 4 = -2.5.","The absolute value 2.5 is greater than 2, so the part is unusual (but not greater than 3, so not extreme). About 0.6% of parts would weigh this little or less if the process is normal. This suggests checking the machine."],"answer":"(a) z = 1.5, about the 93rd percentile; (b) z = -2.5, which is unusual (|z| > 2)."},{"q":"Sales Rep A sold 120 units in a region where the mean is 100 and the standard deviation is 10. Sales Rep B sold 450 units in a region where the mean is 400 and the standard deviation is 50. Who performed better relative to their own region, and what percent of reps in A's region sold fewer than A?","steps":["Compute each z-score so the comparison is on the same scale.","Rep A: z = (120 - 100) / 10 = 2.0.","Rep B: z = (450 - 400) / 50 = 1.0.","A's z (2.0) is higher than B's z (1.0), so A performed better relative to their region even though B sold more units in total.","A z of 2.0 is about the 97.7th percentile, so about 97.7% of reps in A's region sold fewer units."],"answer":"Rep A (z = 2.0 vs z = 1.0); about 97.7% of reps in A's region sold fewer."}],"videos":[{"title":"The normal distribution and the empirical rule (68-95-99.7)","query":"Khan Academy empirical rule normal distribution"},{"title":"Z-scores explained: what they mean and how to calculate them","query":"StatQuest z-scores explained"},{"title":"Binomial distribution basics for beginners","query":"binomial distribution explained simply n p success"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Discrete variable","b":"A variable whose values are counted (gaps between values), e.g., number of defects."},{"f":"Continuous variable","b":"A variable that is measured and can take any value in a range, e.g., time, weight, dollars."},{"f":"For a continuous distribution, what represents probability?","b":"Area under the curve over a range of values. Total area = 1."},{"f":"Four conditions for a binomial distribution","b":"Fixed number of trials n; two outcomes per trial; constant probability p; independent trials."},{"f":"Mean of a binomial distribution","b":"n x p (trials times probability of success)."},{"f":"Standard deviation of a binomial distribution","b":"Square root of (n x p x (1 - p))."},{"f":"Properties of the normal curve","b":"Symmetric bell shape; mean = median = mode at center; defined by mean and standard deviation; total area = 1."},{"f":"Empirical rule","b":"About 68% within 1 sd, 95% within 2 sd, 99.7% within 3 sd of the mean (normal data)."},{"f":"Z-score formula","b":"z = (x - mean) / standard deviation."},{"f":"What does a z-score tell you?","b":"How many standard deviations a value is above (+) or below (-) the mean."},{"f":"Formula to get x from z","b":"x = mean + z x standard deviation."},{"f":"Standard normal distribution","b":"Normal distribution with mean 0 and standard deviation 1 (z-scores follow it)."},{"f":"Percentile for z = 1","b":"About 84th percentile (0.8413 below, 16% above)."},{"f":"Percentile for z = 2","b":"About 97.7th percentile (0.9772 below, 2.3% above)."},{"f":"Percentile for z = -1","b":"About 16th percentile (0.1587 below)."},{"f":"Percentile for z = 0","b":"50th percentile (the mean = the median in a normal distribution)."},{"f":"How do you find the percent ABOVE a z-score?","b":"Find the percent below and subtract from 100%. By symmetry, area above +z = area below -z."},{"f":"When is a value 'unusual'?","b":"When |z| > 2 (more than 2 sd from the mean). 'Very unusual' when |z| > 3."},{"f":"Why use z-scores to compare values?","b":"They put values from different distributions on the same unit-free scale; the higher z is relatively stronger."},{"f":"Uniform distribution","b":"All values in a range are equally likely; flat rectangle shape; probability = proportion of the range."},{"f":"Right-skewed distribution: mean vs median","b":"Long tail to the right; the mean is greater than the median (e.g., income)."},{"f":"Left-skewed distribution: mean vs median","b":"Long tail to the left; the mean is less than the median."}],"questions":[{"q":"A quality analyst tracks several variables on a production line. Which of the following is a CONTINUOUS variable?","o":["Number of defects on a circuit board","Number of customers in line","Time in minutes to assemble a unit","Number of units sold per day"],"a":2,"e":"Assembly time is measured and can take any value in a range (7.43 minutes), so it is continuous. The other three are counts, which are discrete.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following situations is best modeled by a binomial distribution?","o":["The time in minutes to resolve a support ticket","The number of defective units among 20 inspected, where each unit independently has a 4% chance of being defective","The daily revenue of a store","The average weight of 20 randomly chosen units"],"a":1,"e":"Binomial requires a fixed number of trials (20), two outcomes (defective or not), constant probability (4%), and independence. The other options are measured values, not counts of successes in fixed trials.","d":2},{"q":"A company sends a marketing email to 200 customers. Each customer independently has a 5% chance of clicking the link. What is the expected (mean) number of clicks?","o":["10","5","20","190"],"a":0,"e":"The binomial mean is n x p = 200 x 0.05 = 10. 190 is n x (1 - p), the expected number of non-clicks.","d":2},{"q":"Which statement about the normal distribution is TRUE?","o":["The mean is always larger than the median","The mean, median, and mode are equal and located at the center","It is skewed to the right","Every value in the range is equally likely"],"a":1,"e":"The normal curve is symmetric, so mean, median, and mode coincide at the center. Equal likelihood describes the uniform distribution, and a mean larger than the median describes right skew.","d":2},{"q":"A machine fills bags with a mean weight of 50 oz and a standard deviation of 5 oz, and weights are approximately normal. Between which two values should about 95% of bag weights fall?","o":["40 oz and 60 oz","45 oz and 55 oz","35 oz and 65 oz","30 oz and 70 oz"],"a":0,"e":"95% falls within 2 standard deviations: 50 +/- 2(5) = 40 to 60. 45 to 55 is only 1 standard deviation (about 68%). 35 to 65 is 3 standard deviations (about 99.7%).","d":2},{"q":"Delivery times are approximately normal with a mean of 30 minutes and a standard deviation of 5 minutes. About what percent of deliveries take longer than 40 minutes?","o":["16%","5%","2.5%","0.15%"],"a":2,"e":"40 minutes is 2 standard deviations above the mean. 95% lie within 2 sd, leaving 5% in the two tails, or 2.5% in the upper tail. 16% would be the share beyond 1 sd, and 5% is the combined two-tail share.","d":2},{"q":"A student scores 82 on an exam with a mean of 70 and a standard deviation of 6. What is the z-score?","o":["12","0.5","1.5","2.0"],"a":3,"e":"z = (82 - 70) / 6 = 12 / 6 = 2.0. The raw difference of 12 is not the z-score until it is divided by the standard deviation.","d":2},{"q":"A shipping time has a z-score of -1.5. What does this mean?","o":["It is 1.5 standard deviations below the mean","It is 1.5 standard deviations above the mean","It is 1.5 units below the mean","It is impossible because z cannot be negative"],"a":0,"e":"A negative z means below the mean, and the size is measured in standard deviations (not original units). Negative z-scores are perfectly valid.","d":2},{"q":"An employee's performance score has a z-score of +1.0 in a normal distribution. Approximately what percent of employees scored lower?","o":["16%","34%","68%","84%"],"a":3,"e":"z = 1 corresponds to about the 84th percentile (0.8413). 34% is the area between the mean and z = 1, and 68% is the area between -1 and +1. 16% is the share ABOVE z = 1.","d":2},{"q":"Using a standard normal table, a product's measurement has a z-score of 2.0. Approximately what percent of products measure HIGHER than this?","o":["97.7%","95%","2.3%","5%"],"a":2,"e":"z = 2 is about the 97.7th percentile, so 97.7% are below and 100% - 97.7% = 2.3% are above. 97.7% is the share below, a common mix-up.","d":2},{"q":"Store X had weekly sales of $52,000 in a market with mean $40,000 and standard deviation $6,000. Store Y had weekly sales of $95,000 in a market with mean $80,000 and standard deviation $10,000. Which store performed better relative to its own market?","o":["Store Y, because its dollar sales are larger","Neither, because the dollar amounts cannot be compared","They performed equally well","Store X, because its z-score of 2.0 is higher than 1.5"],"a":3,"e":"Store X: z = (52,000 - 40,000) / 6,000 = 2.0. Store Y: z = (95,000 - 80,000) / 10,000 = 1.5. Z-scores allow comparison across different distributions, and the higher z is relatively stronger.","d":2},{"q":"A quality engineer computes the z-scores of four parts: 1.2, -0.4, -2.6, and 1.9. Using the |z| greater than 2 rule, which part is unusual?","o":["-2.6","1.2","-0.4","1.9"],"a":0,"e":"|-2.6| = 2.6 is greater than 2, so it is unusual. The sign does not matter for this rule. 1.9 is close but does not exceed 2.","d":2},{"q":"Prices of a component are approximately normal with a mean of $500 and a standard deviation of $30. What price corresponds to a z-score of -2?","o":["$530","$440","$560","$470"],"a":1,"e":"x = mean + z(sd) = 500 + (-2)(30) = 440. $560 is the price at z = +2, the mirror image.","d":2},{"q":"Annual household incomes in a city are strongly right-skewed. Which relationship between the mean and median is most likely?","o":["Mean is greater than the median","Mean is less than the median","Mean equals the median","The mean and median cannot be compared"],"a":0,"e":"In a right-skewed distribution a few very large values pull the mean up toward the long right tail, so the mean exceeds the median. Left-skew gives the opposite.","d":2},{"q":"A customer's wait for a shuttle is equally likely to be any time between 0 and 10 minutes (uniform distribution). What is the probability the wait is less than 2.5 minutes?","o":["0.25","0.10","0.50","0.75"],"a":0,"e":"For a uniform distribution, probability equals the proportion of the range: 2.5 / 10 = 0.25. It is not 0.50, which would be the probability of less than 5 minutes.","d":2},{"q":"Employee commute times are approximately normal with a mean of 25 minutes and a standard deviation of 4 minutes. About what percent of employees commute between 21 and 29 minutes?","o":["95%","68%","34%","99.7%"],"a":1,"e":"21 and 29 are exactly 1 standard deviation below and above the mean (25 +/- 4), and the empirical rule says about 68% of values fall within 1 SD. The tempting 34% is only one side (mean to +1 SD); 95% would be within 2 SD (17 to 33).","d":1},{"q":"A supplier's lead time has a mean of 200 hours and a standard deviation of 25 hours. One shipment took 160 hours. What is its z-score?","o":["1.6","-1.6","6.4","-40"],"a":1,"e":"z = (x - mean) / SD = (160 - 200) / 25 = -1.6. The tempting 1.6 drops the sign, but 160 is below the mean so z must be negative. 6.4 results from forgetting to subtract the mean, and -40 forgets to divide by the SD.","d":1},{"q":"Machine cycle times are approximately normal with a mean of 120 seconds and a standard deviation of 8 seconds. What cycle time has a z-score of +1.5?","o":["128 seconds","12 seconds","108 seconds","132 seconds"],"a":3,"e":"x = mean + z(SD) = 120 + 1.5(8) = 132. The tempting 128 uses z = 1 (one SD above). 12 is just z times the SD and forgets to add the mean; 108 subtracts instead of adds.","d":1},{"q":"Which feature would prevent a situation from being modeled as a binomial experiment?","o":["The probability of success changes depending on the results of earlier trials.","There is a fixed number of trials.","Each trial has only two possible outcomes, success or failure.","The trials are independent of each other."],"a":0,"e":"A binomial experiment needs a fixed number of trials, two outcomes per trial, independent trials and a constant probability of success. A success probability that shifts with earlier results breaks independence and constancy, so the model no longer applies. The other three options are required features, not violations.","d":1},{"q":"Customer satisfaction scores are approximately normal with a mean of 70 and a standard deviation of 10. About what percent of customers scored below 85? (Use z = 1.5, which has an area of 0.9332 to its left.)","o":["6.7%","85%","93.3%","84.1%"],"a":2,"e":"z = (85 - 70) / 10 = 1.5, and the standard normal table gives the area to the LEFT: 0.9332, or about 93.3%. The tempting 6.7% is the area above 85. 85% confuses the score with a percentage, and 84.1% is the area below z = 1.0.","d":2},{"q":"Monthly output for a line is approximately normal with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Using the empirical rule, about what percent of months fall between 85 and 130?","o":["68%","95%","97.5%","81.5%"],"a":3,"e":"85 is 1 SD below the mean and 130 is 2 SD above. The empirical rule gives 34% (mean to -1 SD) plus 34% + 13.5% = 47.5% (mean to +2 SD), for 81.5%. The tempting 68% only covers -1 to +1 SD and 95% only covers -2 to +2 SD.","d":2},{"q":"Entrance exam scores are approximately normal with a mean of 500 and a standard deviation of 100. Which score is closest to the 90th percentile? (The 90th percentile has z of about 1.28.)","o":["628","590","528","680"],"a":0,"e":"x = mean + z(SD) = 500 + 1.28(100) = 628. The tempting 590 is 500 + 0.9(100), which treats the percentile 0.90 as if it were a z-score. 528 uses z = 0.28 and 680 corresponds to z = 1.8.","d":2},{"q":"A sales team makes 100 independent calls. Each call has a 20% chance of ending in a sale. What are the mean and standard deviation of the number of sales?","o":["Mean 20, SD 16","Mean 20, SD 4","Mean 20, SD 4.47","Mean 80, SD 4"],"a":1,"e":"For a binomial, mean = np = 100(0.20) = 20 and SD = sqrt(np(1 - p)) = sqrt(100 x 0.2 x 0.8) = sqrt(16) = 4. The tempting SD of 16 is the variance (the value under the square root). SD 4.47 = sqrt(np) forgets the (1 - p) factor, and a mean of 80 counts failures.","d":2},{"q":"A supplier's parts pass inspection independently with probability 0.90. What is the probability that all of the next 3 parts pass?","o":["0.900","0.270","0.729","0.001"],"a":2,"e":"For independent trials, multiply: 0.9 x 0.9 x 0.9 = 0.729 (this is the binomial P(X = 3) with n = 3, p = 0.9). The tempting 0.900 is the chance for a single part. 0.270 = 3 x 0.9 adds instead of multiplying, and 0.001 is the chance all three FAIL.","d":2},{"q":"In a normal process, a measurement is 3.4 standard deviations above the mean. Which description is best?","o":["Extremely unusual: only about 0.3% of values fall more than 3 SD from the mean, both tails combined.","Fairly common: about 5% of values are at least this far from the mean.","Typical: it falls inside the middle 68% of values.","Impossible: no normal value can exceed 3 SD."],"a":0,"e":"The empirical rule says 99.7% of values lie within 3 SD, so only about 0.3% lie beyond (both tails), and 3.4 SD is even more extreme. The tempting 5% figure is the cutoff for 2 SD, and a normal curve never makes a value truly impossible, only very rare.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst converts skewed monthly revenue data into z-scores and then applies the empirical rule (68-95-99.7) to the z-scores. What is the main problem?","o":["Nothing is wrong, because z-scores make any dataset approximately normal.","The empirical rule can never be applied to z-scores, only to raw scores.","Z-scores can only be computed for data that are already normal.","Converting to z-scores only rescales the data; a skewed distribution stays skewed, so the empirical rule percentages are not reliable."],"a":3,"e":"A z-score is a linear transformation (subtract the mean, divide by the SD), so it changes the center and scale but not the shape. The tempting 'z-scores normalize data' belief is a classic misconception. Z-scores can be computed for any data; it is the empirical rule that needs a roughly normal shape.","d":3},{"q":"A company gives every employee a flat $2,000 raise (the same dollar amount added to every salary). What happens to the mean salary, the standard deviation, and each employee's z-score?","o":["The mean and SD both rise by $2,000; z-scores stay the same.","The mean rises by $2,000; the SD and every z-score stay the same.","The mean rises by $2,000, the SD rises, and every z-score rises.","The mean stays the same and the SD rises by $2,000."],"a":1,"e":"Adding a constant shifts every value, so the mean shifts by that constant, but the spread (SD) is unchanged. Each employee's distance from the mean in SD units is also unchanged, so every z-score is the same. The tempting 'SD rises too' confuses a shift with a stretch (multiplying would change SD).","d":3},{"q":"A bottling line fills bottles with a normal distribution and an SD of 2 oz. The label says 16 oz, and management wants only 2.5% of bottles to be underfilled (below 16 oz). Using z = -1.96 for the lowest 2.5%, what mean fill setting should be used?","o":["12.08 oz","16.00 oz","19.92 oz","17.96 oz"],"a":2,"e":"Solve 16 = mean - 1.96(2), so mean = 16 + 3.92 = 19.92 oz. The tempting 12.08 subtracts instead of adds, moving the mean below the label. 17.96 adds 1.96 but forgets to multiply by the SD, and 16.00 would leave half of all bottles underfilled.","d":3},{"q":"Daily demand for a product is approximately normal with a mean of 200 units and a standard deviation of 20 units. What is the probability demand falls between 170 and 230 units? (P(Z < 1.5) = 0.9332.)","o":["86.6%","93.3%","13.4%","68.0%"],"a":0,"e":"z for 170 is (170 - 200)/20 = -1.5 and z for 230 is +1.5. Probability = 0.9332 - 0.0668 = 0.8664, about 86.6%. The tempting 93.3% is the area to the left of z = 1.5 only, and 13.4% is the area outside the interval (the two tails).","d":3},{"q":"Each of 4 independent sales calls has a 30% chance of ending in a sale. What is the probability of exactly 2 sales?","o":["0.044","0.180","0.600","0.265"],"a":3,"e":"Binomial: P(X = 2) = C(4,2)(0.3)^2(0.7)^2 = 6 x 0.09 x 0.49 = 0.2646. The tempting 0.044 = (0.3)^2(0.7)^2 forgets the C(4,2) = 6 ways to choose which two calls succeed. 0.180 and 0.600 come from multiplying 2 by the probabilities, which is not how the formula works.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Discrete variable","Counted values with gaps between them"],["Continuous variable","Measured; any value within a range"],["Binomial","Fixed trials, two outcomes, constant p"],["Normal curve","Symmetric bell shape; mean equals median"],["Empirical rule","68-95-99.7 percent within 1-2-3 sds"],["z-score","Number of sds from the mean"],["Standard normal","Normal with mean 0, sd 1"],["Uniform distribution","All values equally likely"]],"traps":["Trap: Converting data to z-scores makes it normal. Truth: z-scores only rescale; shape stays the same, so skewed data stays skewed.","Trap: The empirical rule works for any dataset. Truth: 68-95-99.7 only applies to approximately normal (bell-shaped, symmetric) data.","Trap: The z-table gives the area above z. Truth: the standard table gives the area to the LEFT; for 'above', compute 1 minus the table value.","Trap: A negative z-score means a bad or invalid value. Truth: it only means the value is below the mean; the sign shows direction, not quality.","Trap: Binomial SD is np(1 - p). Truth: the SD is the square root, sqrt(np(1 - p)); np(1 - p) is the variance."],"mnemonic":"Table = LEFT: 'below' reads straight off the z-table, 'above' is 1 minus it. And 68-95-99.7 is 1-2-3 SDs, normal data only.","cram":["z = (x - mean) / SD; it counts standard deviations from the mean.","Empirical rule: 68%, 95%, 99.7% within 1, 2, 3 SD (normal data only).","Z-table gives area to the left; above z equals 1 minus table value.","Convert back with x = mean + z times SD.","Rule of thumb: |z| greater than 2 is unusual; greater than 3 is extreme.","Binomial: mean = np, SD = sqrt(np(1 - p)).","Z-scores allow fair comparison across different scales and distributions.","Adding a constant shifts the mean only; SD and z-scores do not change."],"ytv":[{"title":"ck12.org normal distribution problems: z-score","channel":"Khan Academy","id":"Wp2nVIzBsE8","why":"Walks through computing and interpreting z-scores in normal distribution problems."},{"title":"ck12.org normal distribution problems: Empirical rule","channel":"Khan Academy","id":"OhRr26AfFBU","why":"Applies the 68-95-99.7 rule to normal distribution problems."},{"title":"The Binomial Distribution: Crash Course Statistics #15","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"WR0nMTr6uOo","why":"Explains binomial setups, probabilities, mean and spread."}]},{"id":"u7","title":"Sampling Distributions & Confidence Intervals","day":4,"summary":"How sample averages behave (sampling distribution, Central Limit Theorem, standard error) and how to turn a sample into a confidence interval estimate of a population mean or proportion.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Populations, samples, and the sampling distribution","body":"<p>In business you almost never get to measure <em>everyone</em>. You measure a <strong>sample</strong> to learn about the whole <strong>population</strong>.</p><ul><li>A <strong>parameter</strong> is a number describing the population (the true average order value, mu). It is fixed but usually unknown.</li><li>A <strong>statistic</strong> is a number computed from a sample (the sample mean, x-bar). It is known, but it changes from sample to sample.</li></ul><p>Imagine a retailer with 1 million orders. If you pull a random sample of 50 orders, the average might be $62. A different random sample of 50 might average $58, another $65. Each sample gives a slightly different mean because of random chance.</p><p>The <strong>sampling distribution of the mean</strong> is the distribution you would get if you took <em>every possible sample</em> of the same size n and plotted all of those sample means. It is a distribution of <em>sample averages</em>, not of individual orders. Three key facts about it:</p><ol><li>Its <strong>center</strong> equals the population mean (sample means are right on target on average).</li><li>Its <strong>spread</strong> is the standard error (explained next), which is smaller than the spread of individual values.</li><li>Its <strong>shape</strong> becomes approximately normal as n grows (the Central Limit Theorem).</li></ol>"},{"h":"2. Central Limit Theorem and standard error","body":"<p>The <strong>Central Limit Theorem (CLT)</strong> is one of the most important ideas in statistics. It says: if you take random samples of size n, the sampling distribution of the sample mean is <strong>approximately normal</strong> when n is large enough, <em>no matter what shape the population has</em>. The common rule of thumb is <strong>n of 30 or more</strong>. (If the population is already normal, any sample size works.)</p><p>Why it matters: even if individual order values are heavily right-skewed, the <em>averages</em> of samples of 40 orders will form a nice bell curve. That lets us use normal-curve tools (z-scores, critical values) for inferences about means.</p><p>The spread of the sampling distribution is the <strong>standard error (SE)</strong>:</p><p><code>SE = standard deviation / square root of n</code></p><p>If individual delivery times have a standard deviation of 20 minutes and you average n = 100 deliveries, then SE = 20 / 10 = <strong>2 minutes</strong>. Averages are much less variable than single values.</p><p><strong>Key relationships:</strong></p><ul><li>Larger n gives a smaller SE: more data gives a more precise average.</li><li>Because of the square root, to <em>cut the SE in half</em> you must <strong>quadruple</strong> the sample size (100 to 400).</li><li>SE is not the same as the standard deviation. The standard deviation describes spread of <em>individual values</em>; the SE describes spread of <em>sample means</em>.</li></ul><p>In practice, you usually don't know the population standard deviation, so you substitute the sample standard deviation (s) to estimate SE.</p>"},{"h":"3. Law of large numbers","body":"<p>The <strong>law of large numbers</strong> says that as you collect more and more observations, the sample mean gets closer and closer to the true population mean.</p><p>Example: after the first 5 orders of the day, the average order size may be far from the long-run average. After 5,000 orders, the running average will be very close to the true mean. A casino loses on individual spins, but with millions of spins, the casino's average result settles right on the true odds.</p><p><strong>Don't confuse the two big ideas:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Law of large numbers:</strong> a bigger sample gives a sample mean <em>closer to</em> the population mean (about accuracy).</li><li><strong>Central Limit Theorem:</strong> a bigger sample makes the <em>shape</em> of the distribution of sample means <em>approximately normal</em> (about shape).</li></ul><p>The law of large numbers does NOT mean a short streak 'has to even out.' A coin that has landed heads five times in a row is still 50/50 on the next flip.</p>"},{"h":"4. Point estimates, interval estimates, and the confidence interval formula","body":"<p>A <strong>point estimate</strong> is a single number used to estimate a parameter. The sample mean of $62 is a point estimate of the true average order value. It is simple but ignores uncertainty: nobody expects it to be exactly right.</p><p>An <strong>interval estimate</strong> gives a range of plausible values. A <strong>confidence interval (CI)</strong> is the standard interval estimate:</p><p><code>CI = point estimate +/- margin of error</code></p><p>and for a mean,</p><p><code>margin of error = critical value x standard error</code></p><p>so <code>CI for a mean = x-bar +/- (critical value) x (s / square root of n)</code>.</p><p><strong>Critical values</strong> from the normal (z) distribution depend on the confidence level you choose:</p><ul><li><strong>90% confidence:</strong> 1.645</li><li><strong>95% confidence:</strong> 1.96</li><li><strong>99% confidence:</strong> 2.576</li></ul><p><strong>Steps:</strong></p><ol><li>Compute the sample mean.</li><li>Compute SE = s / square root of n.</li><li>Multiply the critical value by SE to get the margin of error.</li><li>Subtract and add the margin of error to the sample mean.</li></ol><p>Example: x-bar = 120, s = 16, n = 64. SE = 16 / 8 = 2. Margin of error (95%) = 1.96 x 2 = 3.92. CI = 120 +/- 3.92 = <strong>(116.08, 123.92)</strong>. The CI is always centered on the point estimate.</p>"},{"h":"5. What changes the width of a confidence interval","body":"<p>The width of the interval is 2 x the margin of error. Since margin of error = critical value x (s / square root of n), three things control the width:</p><ul><li><strong>Confidence level:</strong> higher confidence uses a bigger critical value, so the interval gets <strong>wider</strong>. A 99% interval is wider than a 95% interval, which is wider than a 90% interval. You pay for more confidence with less precision.</li><li><strong>Sample size n:</strong> larger n shrinks the SE, so the interval gets <strong>narrower</strong>. To halve the margin of error, quadruple n.</li><li><strong>Variability (s):</strong> more variable data gives a bigger SE and a <strong>wider</strong> interval.</li></ul><p>Example with s = 30:</p><ul><li>n = 100: SE = 3, 95% margin of error = 1.96 x 3 = 5.88.</li><li>n = 400: SE = 1.5, 95% margin of error = 1.96 x 1.5 = 2.94 (exactly half).</li><li>n = 100 at 99%: margin of error = 2.576 x 3 = 7.73, wider than the 5.88 at 95%.</li></ul><p><strong>Business trade-off:</strong> a narrower interval needs more data, which costs more money and time. A manager picks the confidence level and sample size to balance precision against cost.</p>"},{"h":"6. Interpreting a 95% confidence interval correctly","body":"<p>Suppose a 95% CI for the true mean customer wait time is (45, 55) seconds.</p><p><strong>Correct interpretation:</strong> 'We are 95% confident that the true population mean wait time is between 45 and 55 seconds.' Even more precisely, the 95% describes the <em>method</em>: if we repeated the sampling process many times and built an interval each time, about 95% of those intervals would contain the true mean.</p><p><strong>Common wrong interpretations (watch for these on the exam):</strong></p><ul><li>WRONG: '95% of all customers wait between 45 and 55 seconds.' The CI is about the <em>mean</em>, not individual values. Individual waits vary much more.</li><li>WRONG: 'There is a 95% chance the sample mean is between 45 and 55.' The sample mean is already known (it is the center of the interval).</li><li>WRONG: '95% of future samples will have means between 45 and 55.'</li><li>WRONG: 'The true mean changes and is in the interval 95% of the time.' The true mean is a fixed number. It is the interval that varies from sample to sample.</li></ul><p><strong>Also remember:</strong> a higher confidence level does not make the interval 'more accurate'; it makes it wider. And you can read the sample mean directly from a CI: it is the midpoint. For (45, 55) the mean is 50 and the margin of error is 5.</p>"},{"h":"7. CI for a proportion, and t versus z","body":"<p><strong>Confidence interval for a proportion.</strong> When the data are 'yes or no' (satisfied or not, clicked or not), the sample proportion p-hat = (number of successes) / n is the point estimate. The standard error is</p><p><code>SE = square root of ( p-hat x (1 - p-hat) / n )</code></p><p>and the CI is <code>p-hat +/- critical value x SE</code>.</p><p>Example: 400 customers surveyed, 240 satisfied. p-hat = 240 / 400 = 0.60. SE = square root (0.60 x 0.40 / 400) = square root (0.0006) = 0.0245. 95% margin of error = 1.96 x 0.0245 = 0.048. CI = <strong>(0.552, 0.648)</strong>, or 55.2% to 64.8%. (Use this when n x p-hat and n x (1 - p-hat) are both at least about 10.)</p><p><strong>t versus z.</strong> The z critical values (1.645, 1.96, 2.576) assume you know the population standard deviation. In real life you usually don't. When you have to use the <em>sample</em> standard deviation (s) to estimate the SE, especially with a small sample, you use the <strong>t distribution</strong> instead.</p><ul><li>The t distribution looks like the normal curve but has <strong>heavier tails</strong>, so t critical values are larger. This builds in extra uncertainty from estimating the standard deviation.</li><li>Its shape depends on <strong>degrees of freedom</strong> = n - 1.</li><li>As n gets large (about 30 or more), t becomes almost identical to z. Example: 95% critical value is 2.145 for df = 14 (n = 15), 2.045 for df = 29, and approaches 1.96.</li></ul><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> population standard deviation known (rare) uses z. Population standard deviation unknown (typical) uses t, and with n of 30 or more the two give nearly the same answer.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["A parameter describes a population and is usually unknown. A statistic is computed from a sample and changes from one sample to the next.","The sampling distribution of the mean is the distribution of sample means from all possible samples of the same size. Its center equals the population mean.","The Central Limit Theorem says that for a large enough sample size, the sampling distribution of the mean is approximately normal, regardless of the shape of the population. A common rule of thumb is a sample size of at least 30.","The standard error equals the standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size. It measures how much sample means vary from sample to sample To cut the standard error in half, you must quadruple the sample size.","The law of large numbers says that as the sample size grows, the sample mean gets closer to the true population mean.","A point estimate is a single number that estimates a parameter. An interval estimate gives a range of plausible values.","A confidence interval equals the point estimate plus or minus the margin of error, and the margin of error equals the critical value times the standard error.","The common critical values are 1.645 for 90 percent confidence, 1.96 for 95 percent confidence, and 2.576 for 99 percent confidence.","A higher confidence level makes the interval wider. A larger sample size makes the interval narrower. More variability in the data makes the interval wider.","A 95 percent confidence interval means we are 95 percent confident the true population value is inside the interval. It does not mean that 95 percent of individual values fall inside it.","For a proportion, the standard error equals the square root of the sample proportion times one minus the sample proportion, divided by the sample size.","Use the t distribution when the population standard deviation is unknown, especially for small samples. It has heavier tails than the normal curve and gets closer to the normal curve as the sample size increases."],"examples":[{"q":"A company samples n = 64 customer service calls. The sample mean handle time is 120 seconds and the sample standard deviation is 16 seconds. Construct a 95% confidence interval for the true mean handle time and interpret it.","steps":["Standard error: SE = s / square root of n = 16 / square root of 64 = 16 / 8 = 2 seconds.","Critical value for 95% confidence: 1.96.","Margin of error = 1.96 x 2 = 3.92 seconds.","CI = 120 - 3.92 to 120 + 3.92 = 116.08 to 123.92.","Interpretation: we are 95% confident the true mean handle time is between 116.08 and 123.92 seconds."],"answer":"(116.08, 123.92) seconds; we are 95% confident the true mean handle time is in this range."},{"q":"In a survey of 400 customers, 240 say they are satisfied. Construct a 95% confidence interval for the true proportion of satisfied customers.","steps":["Sample proportion: p-hat = 240 / 400 = 0.60.","Standard error: square root of (0.60 x 0.40 / 400) = square root of (0.24 / 400) = square root of 0.0006 = about 0.0245.","Margin of error: 1.96 x 0.0245 = about 0.048.","CI = 0.60 - 0.048 to 0.60 + 0.048 = 0.552 to 0.648."],"answer":"(0.552, 0.648), meaning about 55.2% to 64.8% of all customers are satisfied."},{"q":"The standard deviation of order processing time is 30 minutes. Compare the 95% margin of error for sample sizes n = 100 and n = 400, and the margin of error for n = 100 at 99% confidence. What do you conclude?","steps":["n = 100: SE = 30 / 10 = 3. 95% margin of error = 1.96 x 3 = 5.88.","n = 400: SE = 30 / 20 = 1.5. 95% margin of error = 1.96 x 1.5 = 2.94.","Quadrupling n cut the margin of error exactly in half (5.88 to 2.94).","n = 100 at 99%: margin of error = 2.576 x 3 = 7.728, which is wider than the 5.88 at 95%.","Conclusion: more data narrows the interval; more confidence widens it."],"answer":"95% margins of error: 5.88 (n = 100) and 2.94 (n = 400); at 99% with n = 100 it is 7.73. Larger n narrows the interval and higher confidence widens it."}],"videos":[{"title":"The Central Limit Theorem and sampling distributions explained","query":"StatQuest Central Limit Theorem"},{"title":"Confidence intervals explained: how to build and interpret them","query":"Khan Academy confidence intervals introduction"},{"title":"Standard error and margin of error in plain English","query":"standard error vs standard deviation explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Parameter vs statistic","b":"Parameter describes a population (fixed, usually unknown). Statistic describes a sample (known, varies by sample)."},{"f":"Sampling distribution of the mean","b":"The distribution of sample means from all possible samples of size n. Centered at the population mean."},{"f":"Central Limit Theorem","b":"For large n (rule of thumb n >= 30), the distribution of sample means is approximately normal, regardless of population shape."},{"f":"Standard error of the mean","b":"SE = standard deviation / square root of n. Measures the spread of sample means."},{"f":"Standard error vs standard deviation","b":"SD = spread of individual values. SE = spread of sample means (smaller, shrinks as n grows)."},{"f":"How do you cut the standard error in half?","b":"Quadruple the sample size (because of the square root)."},{"f":"Law of large numbers","b":"As sample size increases, the sample mean gets closer to the true population mean."},{"f":"CLT vs law of large numbers","b":"CLT: shape of sample means becomes normal. LLN: sample mean gets closer to the population mean."},{"f":"Point estimate","b":"A single number used to estimate a parameter (e.g., sample mean)."},{"f":"Interval estimate","b":"A range of plausible values for a parameter, such as a confidence interval."},{"f":"Confidence interval formula (mean)","b":"x-bar +/- critical value x (s / square root of n)."},{"f":"Margin of error","b":"Critical value x standard error. Half the width of the CI."},{"f":"Critical value for 90% confidence","b":"1.645"},{"f":"Critical value for 95% confidence","b":"1.96"},{"f":"Critical value for 99% confidence","b":"2.576"},{"f":"Effect of higher confidence level on CI width","b":"Wider interval (bigger critical value)."},{"f":"Effect of larger sample size on CI width","b":"Narrower interval (smaller standard error)."},{"f":"Correct interpretation of a 95% CI","b":"We are 95% confident the true population value lies in the interval; 95% of intervals built this way capture the truth."},{"f":"Common wrong interpretation of a 95% CI","b":"'95% of individual values fall in the interval.' The CI is about the mean, not individuals."},{"f":"How do you find the sample mean from a CI?","b":"It is the midpoint: (lower + upper) / 2. Margin of error = (upper - lower) / 2."},{"f":"Standard error of a sample proportion","b":"Square root of (p-hat x (1 - p-hat) / n)."},{"f":"When use t instead of z?","b":"When the population standard deviation is unknown (use s) especially for small n. Degrees of freedom = n - 1; heavier tails; approaches z as n grows."}],"questions":[{"q":"A retailer repeatedly takes random samples of 50 orders and records the average order value from each sample. The distribution of all those sample averages is called:","o":["The population distribution","The distribution of values within a single sample","The sampling distribution of the mean","The distribution of sample standard deviations"],"a":2,"e":"The sampling distribution of the mean is the distribution of sample means across many samples of the same size. A single sample's values or the population values describe individual orders, not averages.","d":2},{"q":"A population of customer order values is strongly right-skewed. An analyst takes many random samples of 40 orders and computes each sample mean. What shape will the distribution of sample means have?","o":["Approximately normal, by the Central Limit Theorem","Right-skewed, like the population","Uniform","Left-skewed"],"a":0,"e":"By the Central Limit Theorem, with n = 40 (at least 30) the sampling distribution of the mean is approximately normal regardless of the population's shape.","d":2},{"q":"The standard deviation of daily sales is 25 units. What is the standard error of the mean for a sample of 100 days?","o":["25","0.25","5","2.5"],"a":3,"e":"SE = 25 / square root of 100 = 25 / 10 = 2.5. Using 25 (the standard deviation) confuses spread of individual values with spread of sample means.","d":2},{"q":"A manager increases a survey's sample size from 100 to 400 (the standard deviation stays the same). What happens to the standard error of the mean?","o":["It doubles","It is cut in half","It stays the same","It is cut to one quarter"],"a":1,"e":"SE = s / square root of n. The square root of n goes from 10 to 20, so the SE is halved. It is not cut to one quarter because of the square root.","d":2},{"q":"A company tracks the running average of order sizes. After 5,000 orders the running average is very close to the true long-run average order size, though after only 5 orders it was far off. Which principle does this illustrate?","o":["The law of large numbers","The complement rule","A Type I error","The empirical rule"],"a":0,"e":"The law of large numbers says the sample mean converges toward the population mean as the number of observations grows.","d":2},{"q":"A report states that the sample mean customer age is 42 and uses this number as the estimate of the population mean age. This single-number estimate is called a:","o":["Confidence interval","Parameter","Point estimate","Margin of error"],"a":2,"e":"A single number computed from a sample to estimate a population value is a point estimate. A confidence interval is a range (interval estimate), and a parameter is the true population value.","d":2},{"q":"Which critical value (z) is used to build a 99% confidence interval?","o":["1.645","1.96","1.28","2.576"],"a":3,"e":"The standard z critical values are 1.645 (90%), 1.96 (95%), and 2.576 (99%).","d":2},{"q":"A sample of 36 invoices has a mean processing time of 80 minutes and a standard deviation of 12 minutes. Which is the 95% confidence interval for the true mean processing time?","o":["(68.00, 92.00)","(76.08, 83.92)","(78.04, 81.96)","(56.48, 103.52)"],"a":1,"e":"SE = 12 / 6 = 2, margin of error = 1.96 x 2 = 3.92, so the CI is 80 +/- 3.92 = (76.08, 83.92). (56.48, 103.52) wrongly used the standard deviation (not the SE) and (78.04, 81.96) used a margin of just 1.96, forgetting to multiply the critical value by the standard error of 2.","d":2},{"q":"Which change would make a confidence interval for a mean WIDER?","o":["Raising the confidence level from 95% to 99%","Increasing the sample size from 100 to 400","Lowering the confidence level from 95% to 90%","Collecting data that has less variability"],"a":0,"e":"A higher confidence level uses a larger critical value (2.576 vs 1.96), which widens the interval. The other three changes all narrow it.","d":2},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for the mean weekly hours worked by employees is (38, 42). Which interpretation is correct?","o":["95% of all employees work between 38 and 42 hours per week","There is a 95% chance that the sample mean is between 38 and 42","We are 95% confident the true mean weekly hours is between 38 and 42","95% of future samples will have a mean between 38 and 42"],"a":2,"e":"A confidence interval is a statement about the population mean. It is not a statement about individual employees, the sample mean (already known to be 40), or future sample means.","d":2},{"q":"A manager reads that a 95% confidence interval for the mean order value is ($40, $50) and concludes, '95% of our customers spend between $40 and $50.' What is wrong with this conclusion?","o":["Nothing; the interpretation is correct","The confidence level should be 99%","The interval must be centered on zero","The interval estimates the population mean, not the spending of individual customers"],"a":3,"e":"A confidence interval estimates a parameter (the mean). Individual customer orders vary much more than the mean does, so far fewer than 95% of individual customers would necessarily fall within the interval.","d":2},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean is (45, 55). What are the sample mean and the margin of error?","o":["Mean = 45, margin of error = 10","Mean = 50, margin of error = 5","Mean = 55, margin of error = 10","Mean = 50, margin of error = 10"],"a":1,"e":"The sample mean is the midpoint (45 + 55) / 2 = 50, and the margin of error is half the width: (55 - 45) / 2 = 5.","d":2},{"q":"In a survey of 400 customers, 240 are satisfied. Which is the approximate 95% confidence interval for the true proportion of satisfied customers?","o":["0.552 to 0.648","0.560 to 0.640","0.502 to 0.698","0.576 to 0.624"],"a":0,"e":"p-hat = 0.60, SE = square root(0.60 x 0.40 / 400) = 0.0245, margin of error = 1.96 x 0.0245 = 0.048, so the CI is 0.552 to 0.648. The interval 0.576 to 0.624 forgot to multiply the SE by the critical value.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst has only 15 delivery-time observations from a roughly normal process and does not know the population standard deviation. Which approach is most appropriate for a 95% confidence interval for the mean?","o":["Use z with a critical value of 1.96","Use z with a critical value of 2.576","Use the t distribution with 14 degrees of freedom","Use the t distribution with 15 degrees of freedom"],"a":2,"e":"When the population standard deviation is unknown and the sample is small, use the t distribution with n - 1 = 14 degrees of freedom. Using z would make the interval too narrow.","d":2},{"q":"A research team wants to cut the margin of error of its estimate in half while keeping the same confidence level and the same variability. What must it do to the sample size?","o":["Double it","Quadruple it","Increase it by 50%","Cut it in half"],"a":1,"e":"Margin of error is proportional to 1 / square root of n. To divide it by 2, the square root of n must double, so n must be multiplied by 4. Doubling n only reduces the margin of error by about 29%.","d":2},{"q":"The mean employee tenure for a company's whole workforce is 6.4 years. If an analyst takes many random samples of size 30 and averages the sample means, what value should that average be close to?","o":["6.4 years","1.17 years","30 years","0 years"],"a":0,"e":"The sampling distribution of the sample mean is centered at the population mean, so the average of many sample means is about 6.4 years (the sample mean is an unbiased estimator). The tempting 1.17 is not a center at all; it is a standard-error-style number (6.4 / sqrt(30)) that mixes up center and spread.","d":1},{"q":"Delivery times have a population standard deviation of 18 minutes. For a random sample of 81 deliveries, what is the standard error of the sample mean?","o":["18 minutes","0.22 minutes","2 minutes","3 minutes"],"a":2,"e":"SE = sigma / sqrt(n) = 18 / sqrt(81) = 18 / 9 = 2 minutes. The tempting 18 is the SD of individual deliveries, not of the sample mean. 0.22 = 18 / 81 divides by n instead of the square root of n.","d":1},{"q":"Which critical z-value is used to build a 90% confidence interval?","o":["1.96","2.576","1.28","1.645"],"a":3,"e":"A 90% interval leaves 5% in each tail, so the critical value is z = 1.645. The tempting 1.96 belongs to 95% and 2.576 to 99%. 1.28 cuts off 10% in one tail (an 80% two-sided interval).","d":1},{"q":"A hospital surveys 120 of its 9,000 patients and finds an average satisfaction rating of 4.2 out of 5. The average rating of all 9,000 patients is a ____, and the 4.2 is a ____.","o":["parameter; statistic","statistic; parameter","parameter; parameter","statistic; statistic"],"a":0,"e":"A parameter describes the entire population (all 9,000 patients) and is usually unknown; a statistic describes a sample (the 120 surveyed) and is used to estimate it. The tempting reverse answer swaps them: the sample result 4.2 is the statistic.","d":1},{"q":"A random sample of 49 orders has a mean of $120 and a standard deviation of $14. Which is the 95% confidence interval for the mean order value?","o":["$106.00 to $134.00","$116.08 to $123.92","$118.00 to $122.00","$92.56 to $147.44"],"a":1,"e":"SE = 14 / sqrt(49) = 2; margin of error = 1.96 x 2 = 3.92; interval = 120 +/- 3.92 = (116.08, 123.92). The tempting $106 to $134 uses the SD (14) as the margin, which describes spread of individual orders, and $118 to $122 uses only 1 SE, which is about a 68% interval.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst wants a 95% confidence interval for the mean processing time with a margin of error of no more than 3 minutes. The population standard deviation is about 15 minutes. What minimum sample size is needed?","o":["96","25","97","10"],"a":2,"e":"n = (z x sigma / E)^2 = (1.96 x 15 / 3)^2 = (9.8)^2 = 96.04, and sample sizes are always rounded UP, so n = 97. The tempting 96 rounds down, which would give a margin slightly above 3. 25 leaves out the z-value, and 10 forgets to square.","d":2},{"q":"A sample of 25 invoices from a roughly normal process has a mean of 50 minutes and a standard deviation of 10 minutes (population SD unknown). With df = 24, the 95% t critical value is 2.064. Which is the 95% confidence interval for the mean?","o":["45.87 to 54.13","46.08 to 53.92","29.36 to 70.64","47.94 to 52.06"],"a":0,"e":"SE = 10 / sqrt(25) = 2; margin = 2.064 x 2 = 4.128; interval = 50 +/- 4.128 = (45.87, 54.13). The tempting 46.08 to 53.92 uses z = 1.96, which is too narrow for a small sample with unknown sigma. 29.36 to 70.64 multiplies t by the SD instead of the SE.","d":2},{"q":"In a random sample of 200 customers, 50 say they prefer the new mobile app. Which is the approximate 95% confidence interval for the true proportion who prefer it?","o":["0.200 to 0.300","0.219 to 0.281","0.150 to 0.350","0.190 to 0.310"],"a":3,"e":"p-hat = 50/200 = 0.25; SE = sqrt(0.25 x 0.75 / 200) = 0.0306; margin = 1.96 x 0.0306 = 0.060; interval = (0.190, 0.310). The tempting 0.219 to 0.281 uses only 1 SE as the margin (about 68% confidence), and 0.200 to 0.300 is a rounded guess that ignores the formula.","d":2},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for the mean order-to-delivery time (days) is 62 to 68. A manager claims the true mean is 70 days. What does the interval suggest?","o":["70 is only 2 days from the interval, so it is plausible.","70 is outside the interval, so a mean of 70 is not plausible at 95% confidence.","The interval only describes the sample mean, so it says nothing about the population mean.","There is a 5% chance the true mean is 70."],"a":1,"e":"A confidence interval gives the plausible values of the population mean at the stated confidence level. 70 lies outside (62, 68), so it is not supported, which matches rejecting H0: mean = 70 in a two-sided test at alpha = 0.05. The tempting '5% chance the mean is 70' treats the confidence level as a probability about the parameter.","d":2},{"q":"Fill volumes for a bottling line follow a normal distribution. An analyst takes random samples of only n = 9 bottles. What is the shape of the sampling distribution of the sample mean?","o":["Not normal, because n is less than 30.","Skewed, because small samples are always skewed.","Normal, because the population itself is normal.","Uniform, because each sample mean is equally likely."],"a":2,"e":"If the population is normal, the sampling distribution of the mean is normal for ANY sample size. The 'n of at least 30' guideline from the Central Limit Theorem is only needed when the population is non-normal or skewed. The tempting 'n < 30 means not normal' mistakes a sufficient guideline for a requirement.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst builds a 95% confidence interval, then switches to 99% confidence AND quadruples the sample size (same variability). What happens to the margin of error?","o":["It falls by about 34%.","It falls by 50%.","It rises by about 31%.","It stays about the same."],"a":0,"e":"Margin = z x sigma / sqrt(n). Moving from 1.96 to 2.576 multiplies it by 1.314, while quadrupling n multiplies it by 1/2. Net = 1.314 x 0.5 = 0.657, so the margin falls about 34%. The tempting 50% ignores the penalty for higher confidence, and 'rises 31%' ignores the benefit of the larger sample.","d":3},{"q":"Individual customer order values have a mean of $100 and a standard deviation of $20. Using the Central Limit Theorem for a random sample of 100 orders, what is the approximate probability the sample mean exceeds $104?","o":["42.1%","15.9%","97.7%","2.3%"],"a":3,"e":"SE = 20 / sqrt(100) = 2, so z = (104 - 100) / 2 = 2.0 and P(Z > 2) = about 0.023. The tempting 42.1% uses the SD of individual orders (z = 0.2), which answers a question about one order, not the sample mean. 97.7% is the area below z = 2.","d":3},{"q":"A company builds a 95% confidence interval for mean delivery time from one random sample and gets (50, 60). A second random sample from the same population gives (52, 64). A colleague says one of the intervals must be wrong. What is the best response?","o":["At least one is wrong, because both intervals should be identical.","Neither is necessarily wrong; different samples give different intervals, and about 95% of such intervals capture the true mean.","The second interval is better, because it is wider.","The intervals must overlap completely, or the data is invalid."],"a":1,"e":"Each random sample produces its own sample mean and interval, so the intervals vary. The 95% describes the long-run success rate of the METHOD. The tempting 'both should be identical' ignores sampling variability, which is the whole reason confidence intervals exist.","d":3},{"q":"A poll of 1,000 voters has a 95% margin of error of about 3 percentage points. Roughly how many voters are needed for a margin of about 1 percentage point?","o":["3,000","9,000","4,000","30,000"],"a":1,"e":"Margin is proportional to 1 / sqrt(n). To divide the margin by 3, multiply n by 3^2 = 9, so 1,000 x 9 = 9,000. The tempting 3,000 assumes the relationship is linear. 4,000 would only cut the margin in half.","d":3},{"q":"A company collects 50,000 voluntary online reviews and reports a very narrow 95% confidence interval for mean customer satisfaction. Only unhappy customers tend to volunteer reviews. Which statement is correct?","o":["The huge sample guarantees the interval contains the true mean.","The Central Limit Theorem corrects for the voluntary-response bias.","The narrow interval proves satisfaction is accurately measured.","The narrow interval reflects low random error but the estimate may still be badly biased, because a large sample does not fix a non-random sample."],"a":3,"e":"Larger n shrinks the standard error and margin of error (random error), but it does nothing about bias from how the sample was collected. Confidence interval formulas assume a random sample. The tempting 'huge sample guarantees accuracy' confuses precision (narrowness) with accuracy (being centered on the truth).","d":3}],"pairs":[["Sampling distribution","Distribution of a statistic's values"],["Central Limit Theorem","Sample means become approximately normal"],["Standard error","Standard deviation divided by root n"],["Point estimate","Single-number guess for a parameter"],["Margin of error","Critical value times standard error"],["Confidence level","Long-run capture rate of method"],["Law of large numbers","Bigger samples approach true mean"],["t distribution","Used when sigma is unknown"]],"traps":["Trap: A 95% confidence interval contains 95% of the individual data values. Truth: it estimates the population MEAN; individual values spread far wider than the interval.","Trap: There is a 95% probability that this particular computed interval contains the true mean. Truth: 95% describes the long-run success rate of the method; any one interval either captures the mean or not.","Trap: Use the standard deviation as the margin in a CI for the mean. Truth: use the standard error, SD / sqrt(n), times the critical value.","Trap: The Central Limit Theorem makes the population normal as n grows. Truth: the population is unchanged; it is the sampling distribution of the sample mean that becomes approximately normal.","Trap: A bigger sample always fixes a bad estimate. Truth: it reduces random error only (SE falls by sqrt(n)); n must quadruple to halve the error, and bias remains."],"mnemonic":"Quadruple n to halve the wobble: SE = sigma / sqrt(n). And the 95% belongs to the METHOD, not to the one interval you computed.","cram":["Sample mean is a statistic; population mean is a parameter.","Standard error of the mean = sigma / sqrt(n).","Sampling distribution of x-bar centers at mu; roughly normal when n is 30+.","CI = estimate +/- critical value x standard error.","Critical z: 90% is 1.645, 95% is 1.96, 99% is 2.576.","Use t when sigma is unknown, especially small samples; df = n-1.","Halving the margin of error requires quadrupling the sample size.","Higher confidence means a wider interval; larger n means a narrower interval."],"ytv":[{"title":"The Central Limit Theorem, Clearly Explained!!!","channel":"StatQuest with Josh Starmer","id":"YAlJCEDH2uY","why":"Builds intuition for sampling distributions of the mean and why they become normal."},{"title":"Confidence interval example","channel":"Khan Academy","id":"SeQeYVJZ2gE","why":"Step-by-step construction of a confidence interval from sample data."},{"title":"Interpreting confidence level example","channel":"Khan Academy","id":"XZAGtQt-lQo","why":"Shows the correct way to interpret a confidence level and avoid the usual wording mistakes."}]},{"id":"u8","title":"Hypothesis Testing","day":4,"summary":"How to turn a business claim into null and alternative hypotheses, use a test statistic, p-value and significance level to make a decision, understand Type I and II errors and power, and choose the right statistical test.","lesson":[{"h":"1. The logic of hypothesis testing: null and alternative","body":"<p>A <strong>hypothesis test</strong> is a formal way to use sample data to decide whether a claim about a population is believable. It works like a courtroom: the claim of 'nothing special is happening' is presumed true (innocent) until the evidence is strong enough to reject it.</p><ul><li>The <strong>null hypothesis (H0)</strong> is the status quo or 'no effect, no difference, no change' claim. It always contains an equality (=, or sometimes at least or at most).</li><li>The <strong>alternative hypothesis (Ha)</strong> is what you are trying to find evidence for: there <em>is</em> an effect, difference, or change. It uses not equal, greater than, or less than.</li></ul><p><strong>How to write them from a business claim:</strong></p><ol><li>Identify the parameter (mean, proportion).</li><li>Put the 'nothing changed' version in H0 using the benchmark number.</li><li>Put what you want to detect in Ha.</li></ol><p><strong>Examples:</strong></p><ul><li>'The new process reduces mean cycle time from 40 minutes.' H0: mean = 40. Ha: mean &lt; 40.</li><li>'Is the bottle fill volume different from the 500 ml spec?' H0: mean = 500. Ha: mean is not equal to 500.</li><li>'The new website converts more than the current 10%.' H0: p = 0.10. Ha: p &gt; 0.10.</li></ul><p>The data are used only to <em>challenge H0</em>. You never prove the null; you either find enough evidence against it or you don't.</p>"},{"h":"2. One-tailed vs two-tailed tests and the significance level","body":"<p>The form of the alternative hypothesis sets the type of test:</p><ul><li><strong>Two-tailed test:</strong> Ha says 'not equal to.' You care about a difference in <em>either</em> direction (fill volume too high or too low). The rejection region is split into both tails.</li><li><strong>One-tailed test:</strong> Ha says 'greater than' (right-tailed) or 'less than' (left-tailed). You only care about one direction (did the cycle time go <em>down</em>?). The whole rejection region is in one tail.</li></ul><p>Look for the words: 'differs,' 'changed,' 'different from' mean two-tailed. 'Increases,' 'exceeds,' 'more than' mean right-tailed. 'Reduces,' 'less than,' 'decreases' mean left-tailed.</p><p>The <strong>significance level, alpha</strong>, is the threshold of evidence you demand <em>before</em> looking at the data. It is the maximum probability of a Type I error (rejecting a true null) you are willing to accept. Common choices: <strong>0.05</strong> (most common), 0.01 (stricter), 0.10 (looser). Alpha = 0.05 means you accept a 5% risk of a false alarm.</p><p>Alpha is chosen by the decision maker based on the cost of making a mistake. It is not computed from the data.</p>"},{"h":"3. Test statistic, p-value, and the decision rule","body":"<p>A <strong>test statistic</strong> measures how far your sample result is from what H0 predicts, in standard-error units. For a mean:</p><p><code>test statistic = (sample mean - hypothesized mean) / (s / square root of n)</code></p><p>It is the same idea as a z-score: how many standard errors away is the sample result from the null value? The larger its absolute value, the stronger the evidence against H0.</p><p>The <strong>p-value</strong> is the probability of getting a result <em>at least as extreme as the one observed, assuming H0 is true</em>. A small p-value means 'this data would be very surprising if H0 were true.'</p><p><strong>Decision rule:</strong></p><ul><li>If <strong>p-value is less than or equal to alpha</strong>: <strong>reject H0</strong>. There is statistically significant evidence for Ha.</li><li>If <strong>p-value is greater than alpha</strong>: <strong>fail to reject H0</strong>. Not enough evidence for Ha.</li></ul><p><strong>Wording matters!</strong> Never say 'accept H0' or 'prove H0.' Failing to reject is like a 'not guilty' verdict: it means the evidence wasn't strong enough, not that the defendant is proven innocent. Say 'we fail to reject the null hypothesis; there is insufficient evidence that...'.</p><p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> the p-value is NOT the probability that H0 is true, and it is NOT the probability that you made a mistake. It is the probability of data this extreme if H0 were true.</p><p><strong>Worked example.</strong> Claim: mean delivery time is 30 minutes. A sample of n = 36 has mean 32 and s = 6. Test statistic = (32 - 30) / (6 / 6) = 2.0. The two-tailed p-value is about 0.0455. At alpha = 0.05, p is less than alpha, so reject H0. (Equivalent critical-value approach: reject when the absolute value of the test statistic exceeds 1.96. Here 2.0 &gt; 1.96.)</p>"},{"h":"4. Type I error, Type II error, and power","body":"<p>Because we decide using sample data, we can be wrong in two ways:</p><ul><li><strong>Type I error (false positive):</strong> rejecting H0 when it is actually true. Probability = <strong>alpha</strong>. Business example: concluding a production process has drifted and shutting down the line for costly adjustments when it was fine all along.</li><li><strong>Type II error (false negative):</strong> failing to reject H0 when it is actually false. Probability = <strong>beta</strong>. Business example: missing a real increase in defect rate, so defective product ships to customers.</li></ul><p>Which error is worse depends on the situation. For a drug safety test, missing a harmful effect (Type II) may be worse. For a costly equipment replacement, a false alarm (Type I) may be worse.</p><p><strong>Power</strong> = 1 - beta = the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis (detecting a real effect). Higher power is better. Ways to increase power:</p><ul><li><strong>Increase the sample size</strong> (the most common method).</li><li>Use a larger alpha (but this raises Type I risk).</li><li>Study a larger true effect or less variable data.</li></ul><p><strong>Trade-off:</strong> with a fixed sample size, lowering alpha (say from 0.05 to 0.01) makes Type I errors less likely but Type II errors <em>more</em> likely (power drops). Only more data reduces both.</p>"},{"h":"5. Statistical significance vs practical significance","body":"<p><strong>Statistically significant</strong> means the result is unlikely to be due to chance alone (p-value less than or equal to alpha). It says nothing about whether the difference is <em>big enough to matter</em>.</p><p><strong>Practical significance</strong> asks, 'Is the size of the effect large enough to matter to the business?'</p><p>With a very large sample, even a trivial difference becomes statistically significant. Example: a company tests 1,000,000 page loads and finds the new page loads 0.01 seconds faster with a p-value below 0.001. That is statistically significant but practically meaningless: no customer will notice, and the redesign may cost $200,000.</p><p>The reverse also happens: a small pilot study (n = 10) may show a big, valuable improvement that is not statistically significant simply because the sample was too small (low power).</p><p><strong>Best practice:</strong> report the p-value <em>and</em> the size of the effect (and a confidence interval). Then judge the effect against costs and goals. Decision makers should ask both questions: 'Is it real?' and 'Does it matter?'</p>"},{"h":"6. Choosing the right test","body":"<p>Pick the test by asking: <strong>What kind of data do I have</strong> (numbers or categories) and <strong>how many groups</strong> are being compared?</p><p><strong>Tests about means (numerical data):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>z-test:</strong> compares a sample mean (or proportion) to a benchmark when the population standard deviation is known or the sample is large. Also used for comparing two proportions (e.g., two conversion rates).</li><li><strong>One-sample t-test:</strong> one group's mean vs a known standard or target, population standard deviation unknown (e.g., 'is average delivery time different from the 30-minute promise?' with n = 20).</li><li><strong>Two-sample t-test (independent samples):</strong> compare the means of <em>two separate groups</em> (e.g., Plant A vs Plant B output).</li><li><strong>Paired t-test:</strong> compare two measurements on the <em>same</em> subjects (before and after training for the same employees; two machines tested on the same parts). Analyze the differences.</li><li><strong>ANOVA:</strong> compare the means of <strong>three or more groups</strong> (sales in four regions). A significant result says at least one mean differs.</li></ul><p><strong>Tests about categories (counts):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Chi-square goodness-of-fit:</strong> does the distribution of <em>one</em> categorical variable match an expected pattern? (Do customer complaints spread evenly across the 5 weekdays?)</li><li><strong>Chi-square test of independence:</strong> are <em>two</em> categorical variables related? (Is product preference related to age group?) Uses a two-way table.</li></ul><p><strong>Scenario shortcuts:</strong> 'before and after, same people' = paired t. 'Two different groups' = two-sample t. 'Three or more groups' = ANOVA. 'Categories in a table' = chi-square. 'Compare rates or percentages between two groups' = two-proportion z.</p>"},{"h":"7. A/B testing and putting it all together","body":"<p><strong>A/B testing</strong> is hypothesis testing for business experiments. Customers are <em>randomly assigned</em> to version A (control, the current version) or version B (the change). You compare a metric between the groups: conversion rate (use a two-proportion z-test or chi-square) or average order value (use a two-sample t-test).</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> Version A: 1,000 visitors, 100 purchases (10%). Version B: 1,000 visitors, 130 purchases (13%).</p><ul><li>H0: the two conversion rates are equal. Ha: they differ (two-tailed).</li><li>Pooled proportion = 230 / 2,000 = 0.115. Standard error = square root (0.115 x 0.885 x (1/1000 + 1/1000)) = about 0.0143.</li><li>Test statistic z = (0.13 - 0.10) / 0.0143 = about 2.10. Two-tailed p-value is about 0.036.</li><li>At alpha = 0.05: p is less than alpha, so reject H0. Version B's higher conversion is statistically significant.</li></ul><p><strong>A/B testing best practices:</strong> randomize assignment; decide the sample size and alpha <em>before</em> starting; don't stop the test early the moment p dips below 0.05 ('peeking' inflates false positives); and check practical significance (is a 3-point lift worth the cost?).</p><p><strong>The full process in 5 steps:</strong> (1) State H0 and Ha. (2) Choose alpha. (3) Choose the test and compute the test statistic and p-value. (4) Decide: reject H0 if p is less than or equal to alpha, otherwise fail to reject. (5) State the conclusion in plain business language, including practical significance.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["The null hypothesis is the status quo claim of no effect or no difference, and it always includes an equality. The alternative hypothesis is what you are trying to find evidence for Put the nothing changed version in the null and the change you want to detect in the alternative.","A two-tailed test is used when the alternative says not equal to. A one-tailed test is used when the alternative says greater than or less than.","The significance level, called alpha, is the maximum risk of a Type I error that you are willing to accept. The most common choice is 0.05.","The test statistic measures how many standard errors the sample result is from the value claimed in the null hypothesis.","The p value is the probability of getting a result at least as extreme as the one observed, assuming the null hypothesis is true.","If the p value is less than or equal to alpha, reject the null hypothesis. If the p value is greater than alpha, fail to reject the null hypothesis.","Never say you accept the null hypothesis. Say you fail to reject it, which means there is not enough evidence for the alternative.","A Type I error is rejecting a true null hypothesis, a false alarm. A Type II error is failing to reject a false null hypothesis, a missed detection.","Power is the probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis. Increasing the sample size is the most common way to increase power.","A result can be statistically significant but not practically significant. With a very large sample, even a tiny and unimportant difference can produce a small p value.","Use a one-sample t test for one mean against a target, a two-sample t test for two independent groups, a paired t test for before and after on the same subjects, and ANOVA for three or more group means. Use a chi-square goodness of fit test for one categorical variable against an expected pattern, and a chi-square test of independence to see whether two categorical variables are related.","An A/B test randomly assigns customers to two versions and compares a metric, such as conversion rate, using a two-proportion z test or a two-sample t test."],"examples":[{"q":"A delivery company promises a mean delivery time of 30 minutes. A sample of n = 36 deliveries has a mean of 32 minutes and a sample standard deviation of 6 minutes. At alpha = 0.05, test whether the true mean differs from 30 minutes.","steps":["Hypotheses: H0: mean = 30. Ha: mean is not equal to 30 (two-tailed, because the question asks whether it differs).","Standard error = s / square root of n = 6 / 6 = 1.","Test statistic = (32 - 30) / 1 = 2.0.","Two-tailed p-value for a test statistic of 2.0 is about 2 x 0.0228 = 0.0455 (the critical value approach agrees: 2.0 is greater than 1.96).","Decision: p = 0.0455 is less than alpha = 0.05, so reject H0.","Conclusion: there is statistically significant evidence that the mean delivery time differs from 30 minutes (it appears longer). Whether 2 extra minutes matters is a practical-significance judgment."],"answer":"Test statistic = 2.0, p about 0.0455, which is below 0.05. Reject H0: the mean delivery time differs from 30 minutes."},{"q":"Write H0 and Ha and state whether each test is one-tailed or two-tailed: (a) A manager claims a new process reduces the current mean cycle time of 40 minutes. (b) QA wants to check whether mean bottle fill differs from the 500 ml spec. (c) Marketing believes a new ad lifts the conversion rate above the current 10%.","steps":["(a) The parameter is mean cycle time. H0: mean = 40. Ha: mean < 40. 'Reduces' means one direction, so a left-tailed (one-tailed) test.","(b) The parameter is mean fill volume. H0: mean = 500. Ha: mean is not equal to 500. 'Differs' in either direction means a two-tailed test.","(c) The parameter is a proportion. H0: p = 0.10. Ha: p > 0.10. 'Lifts above' means a right-tailed (one-tailed) test."],"answer":"(a) H0: mean = 40, Ha: mean < 40, one-tailed (left). (b) H0: mean = 500, Ha: mean is not equal to 500, two-tailed. (c) H0: p = 0.10, Ha: p > 0.10, one-tailed (right)."},{"q":"An A/B test shows Version A converts 100 of 1,000 visitors (10%) and Version B converts 130 of 1,000 visitors (13%). At alpha = 0.05, is the difference statistically significant? Which test applies?","steps":["Two independent groups and a yes/no outcome (converted or not), so use a two-proportion z-test.","H0: the two conversion rates are equal. Ha: the rates differ (two-tailed).","Pooled proportion = (100 + 130) / 2,000 = 0.115.","Standard error = square root (0.115 x 0.885 x (1/1000 + 1/1000)) = square root (0.0002036) = about 0.0143.","z = (0.13 - 0.10) / 0.0143 = about 2.10. Two-tailed p-value is about 0.036.","Decision: 0.036 is less than 0.05, so reject H0. Version B's conversion rate is significantly higher. Still ask whether a 3-point lift justifies the cost of the change."],"answer":"Two-proportion z-test: z is about 2.10, p about 0.036, so reject H0. The difference is statistically significant at the 0.05 level."}],"videos":[{"title":"Hypothesis testing explained: null, alternative, p-values","query":"StatQuest hypothesis testing and p-values"},{"title":"Type I and Type II errors and statistical power","query":"Type I and Type II errors power explained simply"},{"title":"How to choose the right statistical test (t-test, chi-square, ANOVA)","query":"which statistical test to use t-test chi-square ANOVA"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Null hypothesis (H0)","b":"The status quo claim of no effect or no difference; always contains an equality. You assume it is true until evidence says otherwise."},{"f":"Alternative hypothesis (Ha)","b":"The claim you seek evidence for: a difference, increase, or decrease. Uses not equal, greater than, or less than."},{"f":"Two-tailed test","b":"Ha uses 'not equal to'; looks for a difference in either direction."},{"f":"One-tailed test","b":"Ha uses greater than (right-tailed) or less than (left-tailed); looks for a difference in one direction."},{"f":"Significance level (alpha)","b":"The maximum Type I error risk you will accept; chosen before the test. Common values: 0.10, 0.05, 0.01."},{"f":"Test statistic","b":"How many standard errors the sample result is from the value in H0, e.g. (x-bar - mu0) / (s / square root of n)."},{"f":"p-value","b":"Probability of getting a result at least as extreme as observed, assuming H0 is true."},{"f":"Decision rule using p-value","b":"If p <= alpha, reject H0. If p > alpha, fail to reject H0."},{"f":"Correct wording when p > alpha","b":"'Fail to reject H0; insufficient evidence for Ha.' Never 'accept H0' or 'prove H0'."},{"f":"Type I error","b":"Rejecting a true H0 (false positive). Probability = alpha."},{"f":"Type II error","b":"Failing to reject a false H0 (false negative). Probability = beta."},{"f":"Business example of a Type I error","b":"Shutting down a production line for repairs when the process was actually fine."},{"f":"Business example of a Type II error","b":"Missing a real rise in defect rate so bad product ships."},{"f":"Power","b":"1 - beta: the probability of correctly rejecting a false H0 (detecting a real effect)."},{"f":"How to increase power","b":"Increase the sample size (best), use a larger alpha, or reduce variability / test larger effects."},{"f":"Effect of lowering alpha on Type II risk (n fixed)","b":"Type I risk falls, but Type II risk rises (power falls)."},{"f":"Statistical vs practical significance","b":"Statistical: unlikely due to chance (small p). Practical: the effect is large enough to matter for the business."},{"f":"One-sample t-test","b":"Compare one group's mean to a known target when the population standard deviation is unknown."},{"f":"Two-sample t-test vs paired t-test","b":"Two-sample: two independent groups. Paired: two measurements on the same subjects (before/after)."},{"f":"ANOVA","b":"Compares the means of three or more groups; a significant result means at least one mean differs."},{"f":"Chi-square goodness-of-fit vs independence","b":"Goodness-of-fit: one categorical variable vs an expected pattern. Independence: are two categorical variables related (two-way table)."},{"f":"A/B testing","b":"Randomly assign users to version A or B and compare a metric (e.g., conversion rate) with a test such as a two-proportion z-test."}],"questions":[{"q":"A manager claims that a new process reduces the mean cycle time below the current 40 minutes. Which is the correct alternative hypothesis for a test of this claim?","o":["Mean cycle time = 40 minutes","Mean cycle time > 40 minutes","Mean cycle time < 40 minutes","Mean cycle time is not equal to 40 minutes"],"a":2,"e":"The claim is that the process reduces (lowers) cycle time, so Ha is mean < 40 (a left-tailed test). Mean = 40 is the null hypothesis. 'Not equal' would be used only if either direction mattered.","d":2},{"q":"Which scenario calls for a TWO-tailed test?","o":["Checking whether the mean fill volume differs from the 500 ml specification in either direction","Checking whether a training program increases mean productivity","Checking whether a new supplier decreases the defect rate","Checking whether the proportion of late shipments exceeds 5%"],"a":0,"e":"'Differs from' in either direction is two-tailed. The other three each specify one direction (increase, decrease, exceed), which makes them one-tailed.","d":2},{"q":"Which statement best describes the null hypothesis?","o":["It is the claim the researcher hopes to prove","It states no effect or no difference (the status quo) and contains an equality","It states that the p-value is less than alpha","It is always rejected when the sample is large"],"a":1,"e":"The null hypothesis is the status quo claim of no effect or difference and contains an equality. The researcher usually hopes to find evidence for the alternative, not the null.","d":2},{"q":"What does the significance level, alpha, represent?","o":["The probability that the null hypothesis is true","The probability that the alternative hypothesis is true","The probability of failing to reject a false null hypothesis","The maximum probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis that the researcher will tolerate"],"a":3,"e":"Alpha is the accepted risk of a Type I error: rejecting H0 when H0 is true. Failing to reject a false null is a Type II error (beta).","d":2},{"q":"A hypothesis test of H0: mean = 100 produces a p-value of 0.03. The analyst uses alpha = 0.05. What is the correct decision?","o":["Reject H0; the result is statistically significant","Fail to reject H0","Accept H0 as true","Change alpha to 0.01 so the result is not significant"],"a":0,"e":"Because p = 0.03 is less than or equal to alpha = 0.05, reject H0. Alpha must be chosen before seeing the data and should not be changed to get a desired result.","d":2},{"q":"A test of whether a new training program changes productivity gives a p-value of 0.12 at alpha = 0.05. Which conclusion is worded correctly?","o":["We have proven the training has no effect","We accept that the null hypothesis is true","We fail to reject the null hypothesis; there is insufficient evidence that the training changes productivity","We reject the null hypothesis"],"a":2,"e":"Since p = 0.12 is greater than 0.05, we fail to reject H0. 'Fail to reject' means the evidence was not strong enough; it does not prove the null hypothesis is true.","d":2},{"q":"A manufacturer tests H0: 'the process is in control.' The sample leads them to conclude the process is out of control and shut the line down for adjustment, but the process was actually fine. What type of error occurred?","o":["Type II error","Type I error","No error; a correct decision","Sampling frame error"],"a":1,"e":"Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error (a false alarm). A Type II error would be failing to detect a process that really is out of control.","d":2},{"q":"A hospital tests H0: 'a supplier's syringes meet the specification.' The test fails to reject H0, but the syringes actually do NOT meet the specification. What type of error is this?","o":["Type I error","A correct decision","Power","Type II error"],"a":3,"e":"Failing to reject a false null hypothesis is a Type II error (a missed detection). Power is the probability of avoiding this error, not an error itself.","d":2},{"q":"What does the statistical power of a test represent?","o":["The probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis","The probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis","The probability that the null hypothesis is true","The probability of failing to reject a false null hypothesis"],"a":0,"e":"Power = 1 - beta = the probability of detecting a real effect (rejecting H0 when it is false). Rejecting a true null is alpha, and failing to reject a false null is beta.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst lowers alpha from 0.05 to 0.01 without changing the sample size. What is the most likely consequence?","o":["Power increases and Type II errors become less likely","Type I errors become more likely","The risk of a Type II error increases (power decreases)","Nothing changes"],"a":2,"e":"A stricter alpha makes it harder to reject H0, which cuts Type I risk but raises Type II risk and lowers power when the sample size is fixed.","d":2},{"q":"A company tests 1,000,000 page loads and finds the new design is 0.01 seconds faster with a p-value below 0.001. The redesign costs $200,000. Which conclusion is best?","o":["The result is not statistically significant","The result is statistically significant but probably not practically significant","The result is practically significant because p is so small","The null hypothesis must be true"],"a":1,"e":"With a huge sample, even a trivially small difference gives a tiny p-value. A 0.01 second speedup is unlikely to matter to customers, so it is statistically but not practically significant.","d":2},{"q":"A sales director wants to compare the mean monthly sales of four regional offices to see whether at least one differs from the others. Which test is appropriate?","o":["Paired t-test","Two-sample t-test","Chi-square goodness-of-fit test","ANOVA"],"a":3,"e":"Comparing the means of three or more groups calls for ANOVA. A two-sample t-test handles only two groups, and a paired t-test handles before/after measurements on the same subjects.","d":2},{"q":"A company measures the productivity of the same 25 employees before and after a training program and wants to know whether mean productivity changed. Which test is most appropriate?","o":["Paired t-test","Two-sample t-test for independent groups","ANOVA","Chi-square test of independence"],"a":0,"e":"The same subjects are measured twice, so the data are paired and the paired t-test (analyzing the differences) applies. A two-sample t-test would be wrong because the two sets of measurements are not independent.","d":2},{"q":"An HR analyst has a table counting employees by department (Sales, Operations, Support) and by job satisfaction level (low, medium, high). The goal is to determine whether satisfaction level is related to department. Which test should be used?","o":["One-sample t-test","ANOVA","Chi-square test of independence","Paired t-test"],"a":2,"e":"Two categorical variables summarized in a two-way table call for the chi-square test of independence. t-tests and ANOVA require numerical outcomes.","d":2},{"q":"An e-commerce team randomly shows 5,000 visitors Page A and 5,000 visitors Page B and wants to know whether the purchase rates (percent who buy) differ. Which test is most appropriate?","o":["Paired t-test","Two-proportion z-test","One-sample t-test","ANOVA"],"a":1,"e":"Two independent groups with a yes/no outcome (bought or not) are compared with a two-proportion z-test (a chi-square test gives an equivalent result). The paired t-test requires the same subjects measured twice.","d":2},{"q":"A quality analyst tests H0: mean = 50. A sample of n = 100 gives a mean of 52 and a standard deviation of 10. What is the test statistic?","o":["2.0","0.2","20","1.0"],"a":0,"e":"Standard error = 10 / square root of 100 = 1. Test statistic = (52 - 50) / 1 = 2.0. Dividing by the standard deviation (10) instead of the standard error would wrongly give 0.2.","d":2},{"q":"A test of whether a new packaging design changes the return rate gives a p-value of 0.002. Which interpretation is correct?","o":["If the design had no effect, results at least this extreme would occur only about 0.2% of the time.","There is a 0.2% chance that the null hypothesis is true.","There is a 99.8% chance that the new design works.","The new design reduces returns by 0.2%."],"a":0,"e":"The p-value is the probability of seeing data at least as extreme as ours IF H0 were true. It is not the probability that H0 is true, which is the tempting misreading in the first wrong option. It also says nothing about the size of the effect.","d":1},{"q":"A retailer's current average basket is $52. The marketing team wants to test whether a new promotion INCREASES the average basket. Which hypotheses are correct?","o":["H0: mu > 52; Ha: mu = 52","H0: mu = 52; Ha: mu > 52","H0: mu = 52; Ha: mu < 52","H0: mu = 52; Ha: mu not equal to 52"],"a":1,"e":"The alternative hypothesis states what the researcher wants to show, an increase, so Ha: mu > 52, with the null holding the 'no change' value. The tempting two-tailed pair would detect any change, up or down, so it does not match the one-directional claim. The first option puts the claim in the null, which is backwards.","d":1},{"q":"A test of H0: p = 0.30 produces a p-value of 0.07. The analyst has chosen a significance level of alpha = 0.10 before collecting data. What is the correct decision?","o":["Fail to reject H0, because 0.07 is greater than 0.05.","Fail to reject H0, because the p-value is not exactly zero.","Reject H0, because 0.07 is less than 0.10.","Accept H0, because 0.07 is small."],"a":2,"e":"The decision rule compares the p-value to the alpha chosen in advance: p = 0.07 <= 0.10, so reject H0. The tempting 'greater than 0.05' applies a default alpha the analyst did not choose. A p-value never has to be zero to reject.","d":1},{"q":"A factory claims its boxes average 500 g. An inspector weighs a random sample of 16 boxes, does not know the population standard deviation, and wants to compare the sample mean to 500 g. Which test is most appropriate?","o":["One-sample t-test","Chi-square test of independence","One-way ANOVA","Paired t-test"],"a":0,"e":"One sample compared to a known value with sigma unknown and a small n calls for a one-sample t-test. The tempting paired t-test is for two measurements on the same units; ANOVA compares 3 or more groups; chi-square handles categorical counts, not means.","d":1},{"q":"H0: p = 0.40 versus Ha: p > 0.40. In a random sample of 100 customers, 50 respond positively. What is the test statistic z? (Use the hypothesized p in the standard error.)","o":["2.00","0.10","10.0","2.04"],"a":3,"e":"p-hat = 0.50; SE under H0 = sqrt(0.40 x 0.60 / 100) = 0.0490; z = (0.50 - 0.40) / 0.0490 = 2.04. The tempting 2.00 uses the sample proportion in the SE (sqrt(0.5 x 0.5 / 100) = 0.05), but a hypothesis test assumes H0 is true, so it uses p0. 0.10 is just the raw difference.","d":2},{"q":"A company wants to know whether the mean time-to-hire differs between two independent groups of recruiters, one of 30 recruiters at Company A and one of 30 at Company B. Which test is most appropriate?","o":["Paired t-test","Two-sample (independent) t-test","Chi-square goodness-of-fit test","One-sample t-test"],"a":1,"e":"Two separate, unrelated groups compared on a numerical mean call for a two-sample independent t-test. The tempting paired t-test is only for before/after or matched measurements on the same units, which is not the case with different recruiters.","d":2},{"q":"A retailer expects customer traffic to be spread equally across Monday to Thursday. It records observed counts for each day and wants to test whether the traffic pattern fits the expected equal distribution. Which test should be used?","o":["One-way ANOVA","Paired t-test","Chi-square goodness-of-fit test","Two-sample t-test"],"a":2,"e":"Observed counts in categories compared to an expected distribution is the chi-square goodness-of-fit test. The tempting ANOVA compares MEANS of numerical data across groups, but here the data are counts in categories.","d":2},{"q":"Which change would increase the power of a hypothesis test, with everything else held constant?","o":["Increasing the sample size","Decreasing alpha from 0.05 to 0.01","Reducing the size of the true effect","Increasing the variability of the data"],"a":0,"e":"More data shrinks the standard error, making a real effect easier to detect, so power rises. The tempting option, lowering alpha, makes rejection harder and lowers power (raising the Type II error rate). A smaller effect and more variability both lower power.","d":2},{"q":"A two-tailed test gives a p-value of 0.08, and the sample result is in the direction the manager predicted. If the manager had run a right-tailed test in the correct direction on the same data, what would the p-value be, and what is the decision at alpha = 0.05?","o":["p = 0.16; fail to reject H0","p = 0.08; fail to reject H0","p = 0.04; fail to reject H0","p = 0.04; reject H0"],"a":3,"e":"The two-tailed p-value counts both tails, so the one-tailed p-value (correct direction) is half: 0.08 / 2 = 0.04. Since 0.04 < 0.05, reject H0. The tempting 0.16 doubles instead of halving. (In practice the tail must be chosen before seeing the data.)","d":2},{"q":"For a right-tailed z test at alpha = 0.05, the critical value is 1.645. A two-tailed test at alpha = 0.05 has critical values +/-1.96. The calculated test statistic is z = 1.80. Which statement is correct?","o":["Reject H0 for both tests.","Reject H0 for the right-tailed test, but fail to reject for the two-tailed test.","Fail to reject H0 for both tests.","Fail to reject H0 for the right-tailed test, but reject for the two-tailed test."],"a":1,"e":"1.80 > 1.645, so it falls in the right-tail rejection region. But 1.80 < 1.96, so it is NOT in the two-tailed rejection region. A one-tailed test puts all of alpha in one tail, making it easier to reject in that direction. The tempting 'reject both' ignores the larger two-tailed cutoff.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst runs 20 independent hypothesis tests, each at alpha = 0.05, on data where every null hypothesis is actually true. What is the approximate probability that at least one test produces a false positive?","o":["5%","64%","36%","100%"],"a":1,"e":"P(no false positives) = 0.95^20 = 0.358, so P(at least one) = 1 - 0.358 = 0.642, about 64%. The tempting 5% is the false-positive rate for a single test, but errors accumulate across many tests (the multiple-comparisons problem). It is not 100%, because each test is a separate chance event.","d":3},{"q":"A product team runs an A/B test on a checkout page and checks the p-value every day, stopping the test the first time p drops below 0.05. On day 9 the variation 'wins' with p = 0.04. What is the biggest concern?","o":["Stopping early lowers the power but does not affect the false-positive rate.","A p-value of 0.04 is too large to show any effect.","The test is invalid because it should have been two-tailed.","Repeatedly checking and stopping at the first p below 0.05 inflates the Type I error rate well above 5%."],"a":3,"e":"Every extra look is another chance to cross 0.05 by luck, so the true false-positive rate is much higher than 5%. A fixed sample size (or a proper sequential method) should be set in advance. The tempting 'it only lowers power' misses that the main risk is a spurious win.","d":3},{"q":"A test is designed with alpha = 0.05 and has power = 0.90 to detect a 2-minute improvement in cycle time. If the process truly improved by exactly 2 minutes, which statement is correct?","o":["There is a 10% chance the test fails to detect the improvement (a Type II error).","There is a 5% chance that the improvement is real.","There is a 10% chance of a Type I error.","There is a 90% chance the null hypothesis is true."],"a":0,"e":"Power = 1 - beta, so beta = 0.10: when the effect is real and of that size, the test misses it 10% of the time. A Type I error can only occur when H0 is TRUE, which is not the case here. The tempting '10% Type I error' confuses the two error types.","d":3},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for the difference in mean cycle time (new process minus old process) is (-6.2, -0.8) minutes. What conclusion matches a two-sided test of H0: difference = 0 at alpha = 0.05?","o":["Fail to reject H0, because the interval contains negative numbers.","Reject H0; the new process is slower, because the difference is negative.","Reject H0; the new process is faster on average, because 0 is not in the interval.","Fail to reject H0, because the interval is wide."],"a":2,"e":"Zero (no difference) lies outside the interval, so H0 is rejected at the 5% level. Both endpoints are negative, so new minus old is negative, meaning shorter cycle time, and the new process is faster. The tempting 'negative means slower' misreads the direction because lower cycle time is better.","d":3},{"q":"A one-way ANOVA comparing mean defect rates across three training methods returns a p-value of 0.01 at alpha = 0.05. What can the manager correctly conclude?","o":["All three methods have different mean defect rates.","Method 1 is the best, because it was listed first.","The three methods all have equal mean defect rates.","At least one method's mean defect rate differs from the others, but ANOVA alone does not say which."],"a":3,"e":"A significant ANOVA only says the null (all means equal) is rejected, so at least one mean differs. Identifying which pairs differ requires a follow-up post-hoc comparison. The tempting 'all three differ' goes beyond what the omnibus test shows.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Null hypothesis","Claims no effect or no difference"],["Alpha","Accepted risk of rejecting a true null"],["p-value","Probability of results this extreme under H0"],["Type I error","Rejecting a true null"],["Type II error","Missing a real effect"],["Power","Chance of detecting a real effect"],["ANOVA","Compares three or more means"],["Chi-square test","Tests relationships between categories"]],"traps":["Trap: The p-value is the probability that the null hypothesis is true. Truth: it is the probability of data this extreme if H0 is true; it does not give the probability of H0.","Trap: Failing to reject H0 proves H0 is true. Truth: it means there is not enough evidence against H0; say 'fail to reject', never 'accept' or 'prove'.","Trap: A statistically significant result is automatically important. Truth: with huge samples, trivial effects get tiny p-values; check practical (business) significance too.","Trap: Lowering alpha is a free way to be safer. Truth: it cuts Type I errors but raises Type II errors and lowers power unless sample size increases.","Trap: Peek at the data, then pick one-tailed or keep testing until p is below 0.05. Truth: decide the hypotheses and sample size up front; peeking inflates false positives."],"mnemonic":"If p is low, H0 must go; if p is high, H0 gets a 'not enough evidence' sigh, never a 'proved it' high-five.","cram":["H0 holds equality; Ha is the claim: less, greater, or not equal.","p-value is less than or equal to alpha: reject H0. Otherwise fail to reject.","Type I = rejecting a true H0 (false positive); probability equals alpha.","Type II = missing a false H0 (false negative); probability is beta.","Power = 1 - beta; rises with bigger n, bigger effect, less variability.","One mean vs a value: t-test; before/after on same units: paired t-test.","Three or more means: ANOVA; categorical counts: chi-square; two proportions: z test.","Statistical significance is not practical significance; weigh effect size and cost."],"ytv":[{"title":"p-values: What they are and how to interpret them","channel":"StatQuest with Josh Starmer","id":"vemZtEM63GY","why":"Clear explanation of what a p-value is and how to use it in a hypothesis test."},{"title":"Introduction to Type I and Type II errors","channel":"Khan Academy","id":"Hdbbx7DIweQ","why":"Defines false positives and false negatives with a real scenario."},{"title":"Statistical Power, Clearly Explained!!!","channel":"StatQuest with Josh Starmer","id":"Rsc5znwR5FA","why":"Shows what power means and what makes a test more likely to detect a real effect."}]},{"id":"u9","title":"Correlation & Regression","day":5,"summary":"How to measure and describe the relationship between two numeric variables with scatter plots, the correlation coefficient r, and simple and multiple regression, and how to read the output without being fooled.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Scatter plots: seeing a relationship before you measure it","body":"<p>Businesses constantly ask whether one thing is connected to another. Does more advertising go with more sales? Do older machines go with more downtime? To answer, you collect <strong>paired data</strong>: for every store, week, or machine you record two numbers.</p><p>A <strong>scatter plot</strong> puts one variable on the horizontal axis (the <strong>x</strong> or <em>explanatory/independent</em> variable) and the other on the vertical axis (the <strong>y</strong> or <em>response/dependent</em> variable). Each pair becomes one dot.</p><ul><li><strong>Direction:</strong> dots drifting up to the right mean a positive relationship; dots drifting down to the right mean a negative relationship.</li><li><strong>Strength:</strong> dots hugging a tight line mean a strong relationship; a shapeless cloud means a weak or no relationship.</li><li><strong>Shape:</strong> the pattern may be straight (linear) or curved. The tools in this unit assume a roughly straight pattern.</li><li><strong>Unusual points:</strong> a dot far from the others is an outlier.</li></ul><p><em>Business example:</em> A coffee chain plots weekly advertising spend (x) against weekly sales (y) for 40 stores. The dots rise to the right in a fairly tight band, so the relationship looks positive, linear, and fairly strong. Always draw the scatter plot first. Numbers like r can hide curves and outliers that a picture shows instantly.</p>"},{"h":"2. The correlation coefficient r","body":"<p>The <strong>correlation coefficient</strong>, written <code>r</code>, is a single number that summarizes the <em>strength and direction of a straight-line relationship</em> between two numeric variables.</p><ul><li>r is always between <strong>-1 and +1</strong>.</li><li>The <strong>sign</strong> gives direction: positive means both rise together; negative means one rises as the other falls.</li><li>The <strong>size</strong> (ignoring the sign) gives strength: closer to 1 means a tighter line.</li><li><strong>r = +1</strong> is a perfect upward line, <strong>r = -1</strong> a perfect downward line, and <strong>r = 0</strong> means no linear relationship.</li></ul><p><strong>Rough strength guide</strong> (textbooks vary slightly): absolute value of r from 0 to about 0.3 is weak, about 0.3 to 0.7 is moderate, and about 0.7 to 1.0 is strong. So r = -0.85 is a strong negative relationship, and r = 0.25 is a weak positive one. A negative r is not weaker than a positive r; -0.9 is stronger than +0.6.</p><p>Two cautions. First, r only measures <em>straight-line</em> relationships. A perfect U-shaped pattern can have r near 0. Second, r has no units, so changing from dollars to thousands of dollars does not change it. In Excel, use <code>=CORREL(x_range, y_range)</code>.</p>"},{"h":"3. Correlation does not imply causation","body":"<p>Finding that two variables move together does <strong>not</strong> prove that one causes the other. There are three common reasons a strong r can mislead:</p><ul><li><strong>Reverse direction:</strong> maybe y causes x. Do high-performing stores spend more on ads, or do ads create high performance?</li><li><strong>Lurking (confounding) variable:</strong> a third variable drives both. Ice cream sales and drowning deaths both rise in summer because of hot weather, not because ice cream causes drowning.</li><li><strong>Coincidence or spurious correlation:</strong> with enough data, unrelated things line up by chance. Per-capita cheese consumption can correlate with deaths by bedsheet tangling, which is just a coincidence.</li></ul><p>The best way to establish causation is a <strong>controlled experiment</strong> with random assignment: for example, randomly give half the stores a new checkout design and compare. Observational data alone can show association, not proof of cause.</p><p>On the exam, whenever a scenario says a manager concludes that X causes Y just because they are correlated, the right answer is almost always that correlation does not prove causation and a lurking variable or experiment should be considered.</p>"},{"h":"4. Simple linear regression: the line of best fit","body":"<p><strong>Simple linear regression</strong> fits a straight line through the scatter plot so you can describe and predict the relationship. The line is written:</p><p><code>y-hat = b0 + b1 x</code></p><ul><li><strong>y-hat</strong> is the predicted value of y.</li><li><strong>b0</strong> is the <strong>intercept</strong>: the predicted y when x = 0.</li><li><strong>b1</strong> is the <strong>slope</strong>: the average change in y for each one-unit increase in x.</li></ul><p>The line is chosen by the <strong>least squares</strong> method, which makes the sum of the squared vertical gaps between the dots and the line as small as possible. The formulas are <code>b1 = r times (sy divided by sx)</code> and <code>b0 = y-bar minus b1 times x-bar</code>, so the line always passes through the point of averages. In Excel use <code>SLOPE</code>, <code>INTERCEPT</code>, or the Data Analysis Regression tool.</p><p><strong>Interpreting in business words.</strong> Suppose Sales = 8,000 + 2.4 x Ad Spend (both in dollars per week). The slope says: <em>each additional dollar of advertising is associated with about $2.40 more weekly sales, on average.</em> The intercept says: with zero advertising, predicted weekly sales are $8,000. The intercept is only meaningful if x = 0 is a sensible value; for something like predicting weight from height, an intercept at height zero is meaningless.</p><p><strong>Making a prediction:</strong> just plug in x. For $1,000 of ads: 8,000 + 2.4 x 1,000 = $10,400.</p>"},{"h":"5. Residuals and R squared: how good is the line?","body":"<p>A <strong>residual</strong> is the prediction error for one data point:</p><p><code>residual = actual y minus predicted y</code></p><p>A positive residual means the actual value was higher than the line predicted; negative means lower. Residuals add up to about zero for a least squares line. If a residual plot (residuals against x) shows a random scatter around zero, a straight line is a good model. A curved pattern in the residuals means the line is the wrong shape.</p><p><strong>R squared</strong> (the <em>coefficient of determination</em>) tells you what share of the ups and downs in y is explained by the model. It ranges from 0 to 1 (or 0 percent to 100 percent).</p><ul><li>R squared = 0.64 means about 64 percent of the variation in y is explained by x. The other 36 percent is due to other factors and randomness.</li><li>In <em>simple</em> regression, R squared equals r squared. If r = -0.8, then R squared = 0.64. Taking the square root loses the sign, so check the slope's sign to know whether r is positive or negative.</li><li>A higher R squared means the model fits better, but it is not proof of causation, and a high value does not guarantee good predictions outside the data.</li></ul><p><em>Example:</em> A model predicting delivery time from distance has R squared = 0.90. Distance explains 90 percent of the variation in delivery times; the remaining 10 percent comes from traffic, weather, and so on.</p>"},{"h":"6. Extrapolation, outliers, and influential points","body":"<p><strong>Interpolation</strong> means predicting for x values inside the range of your data. This is generally reasonable. <strong>Extrapolation</strong> means predicting outside that range, and it is dangerous because the relationship may change. If your data covers ad spend from $500 to $5,000 per week, predicting sales for $50,000 per week is a guess, since there may be saturation, diminishing returns, or a different market entirely.</p><p><strong>Outliers</strong> are points that sit far from the pattern. An outlier in the y direction produces a big residual. A point extreme in the x direction has high <strong>leverage</strong> and can pull the whole line toward it. A point that, when removed, noticeably changes the slope, intercept, or r is called an <strong>influential point</strong>.</p><ul><li>First check whether the outlier is a data entry error and fix it.</li><li>If it is real, investigate why it is unusual. It may reveal an important special case.</li><li>Report results with and without the point if it changes the conclusion. Do not delete data just because it is inconvenient.</li></ul><p>Remember also that regression describes an average relationship. Predictions for any single store will have errors, which is exactly what residuals measure.</p>"},{"h":"7. Multiple regression, dummy variables, and reading output","body":"<p><strong>Multiple regression</strong> uses two or more x variables to predict y: <code>y-hat = b0 + b1 x1 + b2 x2 + ...</code>. Each coefficient is read as: the average change in y for a one-unit increase in that x, <em>holding the other x variables constant</em>. Example: Salary = 30,000 + 2,000 x Years + 5,000 x Manager.</p><p>A <strong>dummy variable</strong> turns a category into numbers. Code Manager as 1 for managers and 0 for non-managers. Its coefficient (5,000) means managers earn about $5,000 more than non-managers with the same years of experience. For a category with k choices (for example four regions) you use k - 1 dummy variables; the left-out group is the baseline.</p><p><strong>Reading regression output</strong> (such as Excel's summary):</p><ul><li><strong>Coefficients:</strong> the b values that form the equation.</li><li><strong>p-value for each coefficient:</strong> tests whether that variable really has a relationship with y. If the p-value is below 0.05 (the usual cutoff), the variable is <em>statistically significant</em>. A large p-value (for example 0.40) suggests the variable adds little.</li><li><strong>R squared:</strong> overall share of variation explained. <strong>Adjusted R squared</strong> corrects for the number of predictors; adding a useless variable can raise R squared but not adjusted R squared.</li><li><strong>Significance F:</strong> the p-value for the whole model; small means the model as a whole is useful.</li></ul><p>Watch out for predictors that are highly correlated with each other (multicollinearity); they make individual coefficients unreliable.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["A scatter plot shows paired data as dots, with the explanatory variable on the horizontal axis and the response variable on the vertical axis, so you can see direction, strength, shape, and outliers.","The correlation coefficient r measures the strength and direction of a straight line relationship, and it always falls between negative one and positive one.","A rough guide is that the absolute value of r above about point seven is strong, between about point three and point seven is moderate, and below about point three is weak.","A negative r means the variables move in opposite directions, and a value of negative point nine is a stronger relationship than a value of positive point six.","Correlation does not imply causation, because a lurking variable, reverse direction, or pure coincidence can create a strong correlation between unrelated things.","A controlled experiment with random assignment is the best way to show that one variable actually causes a change in another.","In simple linear regression the predicted value equals the intercept plus the slope times x, and the line is found by least squares, which minimizes the squared vertical gaps.","The slope is the average change in y for each one unit increase in x, and the intercept is the predicted y when x is zero, which is only meaningful if zero is a sensible x value.","A residual is the actual value minus the predicted value, and a random scatter of residuals around zero tells you a straight line is a good fit.","R squared is the share of variation in y explained by the model; in simple regression it equals r squared, so an r of negative point eight gives an R squared of point six four.","Extrapolation, which means predicting outside the range of the data, is risky, and influential outliers can pull the regression line away from the true pattern.","In multiple regression each coefficient is the effect of one variable holding the others constant, dummy variables code categories as zero or one, and a p-value below point oh five means a variable is statistically significant."],"examples":[{"q":"A small retailer records weekly advertising spend x (in $1,000s) and weekly sales y (in $10,000s) for five weeks: (1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 5), (4, 4), (5, 5). Find the regression line, the correlation r, R squared, the predicted sales for $4,000 of advertising, and the residual for the week with x = 2.","steps":["Compute the means. x-bar = (1+2+3+4+5)/5 = 3. y-bar = (2+4+5+4+5)/5 = 4.","Compute deviations from the means. For x: -2, -1, 0, 1, 2. For y: -2, 0, 1, 0, 1.","Sum of products of deviations (Sxy) = (-2)(-2) + (-1)(0) + (0)(1) + (1)(0) + (2)(1) = 4 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 2 = 6.","Sum of squared x deviations (Sxx) = 4 + 1 + 0 + 1 + 4 = 10. Sum of squared y deviations (Syy) = 4 + 0 + 1 + 0 + 1 = 6.","Slope b1 = Sxy / Sxx = 6 / 10 = 0.6. Intercept b0 = y-bar - b1 x x-bar = 4 - 0.6(3) = 2.2. The line is y-hat = 2.2 + 0.6x.","Correlation r = Sxy / square root of (Sxx x Syy) = 6 / square root of 60 = 6 / 7.746 = 0.775. That is a strong positive relationship. R squared = 0.775 squared = 0.60.","Prediction for x = 4: y-hat = 2.2 + 0.6(4) = 4.6, which is $46,000 of sales.","Residual at x = 2: predicted = 2.2 + 0.6(2) = 3.4. Actual = 4. Residual = 4 - 3.4 = +0.6 (actual was $6,000 above the prediction)."],"answer":"y-hat = 2.2 + 0.6x; r is about 0.775; R squared = 0.60; predicted sales at $4,000 ad spend = 4.6 ($46,000); residual at x = 2 is +0.6."},{"q":"A real estate analyst fits Price = 50 + 0.12 x SqFt, where price is in $1,000s and the data covered homes from 900 to 3,200 square feet. R squared is 0.81. (a) Interpret the slope. (b) Predict the price of a 1,500 square foot home. (c) What is r? (d) Is it wise to predict the price of a 10,000 square foot mansion?","steps":["(a) The slope 0.12 is in $1,000s per square foot, so each extra square foot is associated with $120 more in price, on average.","(b) Plug in x = 1,500: 50 + 0.12(1,500) = 50 + 180 = 230. The predicted price is $230,000.","(c) In simple regression R squared = r squared, so |r| = square root of 0.81 = 0.90. Because the slope is positive, r = +0.90, a strong positive correlation.","(d) 10,000 square feet is far outside the 900 to 3,200 range of the data. The line would predict 50 + 1,200 = 1,250, or $1.25 million, but this is extrapolation, and luxury homes may follow a very different pattern, so the prediction is unreliable."],"answer":"(a) About $120 more per extra square foot. (b) $230,000. (c) r = +0.90. (d) No, that is risky extrapolation outside the data range."},{"q":"A company regresses annual salary on Years of experience and Manager (1 = manager, 0 = not) and gets: Salary = 30,000 + 2,000(Years) + 5,000(Manager). The p-value for Years is 0.003 and for Manager is 0.40. Predict the salary of a non-manager with 5 years and a manager with 5 years, and say what the p-values suggest.","steps":["Non-manager with 5 years: 30,000 + 2,000(5) + 5,000(0) = 30,000 + 10,000 + 0 = $40,000.","Manager with 5 years: 30,000 + 2,000(5) + 5,000(1) = 30,000 + 10,000 + 5,000 = $45,000.","The Manager coefficient of 5,000 is the difference between the two groups holding years constant.","p-value for Years = 0.003, which is below 0.05, so years of experience is statistically significant.","p-value for Manager = 0.40, which is above 0.05, so there is not enough evidence that being a manager matters once years are accounted for. The $5,000 gap could easily be due to chance."],"answer":"Non-manager: $40,000. Manager: $45,000. Years is significant (p = 0.003); Manager is not significant (p = 0.40)."}],"videos":[{"title":"Correlation coefficient and scatter plots explained","query":"StatQuest correlation explained"},{"title":"Simple linear regression and least squares","query":"StatQuest linear regression main ideas"},{"title":"Reading Excel regression output (R squared and p-values)","query":"Excel regression analysis output interpretation R squared p-value"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Scatter plot","b":"A graph of paired data where each dot is one (x, y) observation; used to see direction, strength, shape, and outliers."},{"f":"Explanatory (independent) variable vs response (dependent) variable","b":"x is the explanatory variable used to predict; y is the response variable being predicted."},{"f":"Correlation coefficient r","b":"A number from -1 to +1 giving the strength and direction of a linear relationship."},{"f":"What does r = 0 mean?","b":"No linear relationship (there could still be a curved one)."},{"f":"Rough strength cutoffs for |r|","b":"About 0 to 0.3 weak, 0.3 to 0.7 moderate, 0.7 to 1.0 strong."},{"f":"Which is stronger: r = -0.9 or r = +0.6?","b":"-0.9. Strength depends on the absolute value; the sign only shows direction."},{"f":"Excel function for correlation","b":"=CORREL(x_range, y_range)"},{"f":"Correlation vs causation","b":"Correlation shows association only. It does not prove that one variable causes the other."},{"f":"Lurking (confounding) variable","b":"A hidden third variable that influences both x and y, creating a misleading relationship (for example, hot weather behind ice cream sales and drownings)."},{"f":"Spurious correlation","b":"A correlation that arises by coincidence or a hidden cause, with no real link between the variables."},{"f":"How can you establish causation?","b":"A controlled experiment with random assignment."},{"f":"Simple linear regression equation","b":"y-hat = b0 + b1x, where b0 is the intercept and b1 is the slope."},{"f":"Interpret the slope","b":"The average change in y for each one-unit increase in x."},{"f":"Interpret the intercept","b":"The predicted y when x = 0; meaningful only if x = 0 makes sense in context."},{"f":"Least squares method","b":"Chooses the line that minimizes the sum of squared vertical distances (residuals) between points and the line."},{"f":"Residual","b":"Actual y minus predicted y. Positive means the line under-predicted."},{"f":"R squared (coefficient of determination)","b":"The proportion of variation in y explained by the model. In simple regression, R squared = r squared."},{"f":"If R squared = 0.49, what is |r|?","b":"0.70 (the square root of 0.49). The sign comes from the slope."},{"f":"Extrapolation","b":"Predicting outside the range of observed x values. Unreliable because the pattern may change."},{"f":"Influential point","b":"A point (often an x-extreme outlier) whose removal noticeably changes the slope, intercept, or r."},{"f":"Dummy variable","b":"A 0/1 variable coding a category (such as Manager = 1). Its coefficient is the difference from the baseline group, holding other variables constant."},{"f":"p-value rule in regression output","b":"A p-value below 0.05 means the variable is statistically significant; a large p-value means little evidence it matters."}],"questions":[{"q":"An analyst finds a strong positive correlation between monthly ice cream sales and monthly drowning incidents across a city. Which explanation is most appropriate?","o":["Ice cream consumption causes people to drown.","Drowning incidents cause people to buy ice cream.","A lurking variable, such as hot summer weather, probably drives both.","The correlation must be the result of a calculation error."],"a":2,"e":"Hot weather increases both swimming (and so drownings) and ice cream sales. This is a classic lurking variable. The first two options assume a causal link just because of correlation, and a calculation error is not needed to explain a real seasonal pattern.","d":2},{"q":"A plant manager finds r = -0.82 between the age of a machine and its daily output. What is the best interpretation?","o":["A weak positive relationship: older machines produce slightly more.","A strong negative relationship: older machines tend to produce less.","A strong positive relationship: older machines produce more.","Machine age causes an 82 percent drop in output."],"a":1,"e":"The negative sign means output falls as age rises, and an absolute value of 0.82 is strong. The 82 is not a percentage drop, and correlation alone does not prove age causes the drop.","d":2},{"q":"A regression of weekly sales on advertising spend gives R squared = 0.64. Which statement is correct?","o":["About 64 percent of the variation in sales is explained by its linear relationship with advertising spend.","Predictions from the model will be correct 64 percent of the time.","The correlation coefficient r is 0.64.","Advertising causes 64 percent of sales."],"a":0,"e":"R squared is the proportion of variation in y explained by the model. It is not a prediction accuracy rate. Since R squared = r squared, r would be about 0.80 (in absolute value), not 0.64. It also does not prove causation.","d":2},{"q":"A model is Revenue = 8,000 + 2.4 x AdSpend, with both measured in dollars per week. What does the slope mean?","o":["With no advertising, revenue is $2.40.","Revenue will always be 2.4 percent of ad spend.","Revenue starts at $8,000 plus $2.40 each month.","Each additional $1 of advertising is associated with about $2.40 more weekly revenue, on average."],"a":3,"e":"The slope is the average change in y per one-unit change in x. The $8,000 is the intercept (predicted revenue at zero advertising). The slope is not a percentage and not tied to months.","d":2},{"q":"The fitted line for a delivery process is y-hat = 15 + 3x, where x is the number of packages. What is the predicted value when x = 10?","o":["45","30","150","18"],"a":0,"e":"y-hat = 15 + 3(10) = 15 + 30 = 45. The value 30 forgets the intercept; 150 multiplies 15 by 10 and ignores the 3.","d":2},{"q":"For a particular store, actual sales were 52 and the regression model predicted 47. What is the residual?","o":["-5","47","+5","52"],"a":2,"e":"Residual = actual minus predicted = 52 - 47 = +5. The positive sign means the model under-predicted. A residual of -5 results from reversing the subtraction.","d":2},{"q":"A model was built from data in which production volume ranged from 200 to 600 units per day. A manager uses it to predict costs at 5,000 units per day. What is the main problem?","o":["The correlation must be negative.","This is extrapolation far beyond the data range, so the prediction is unreliable.","R squared is too high to use the model.","Residuals can never be used for prediction."],"a":1,"e":"Predicting well outside the range of the observed x values is extrapolation, and the relationship may change (capacity limits, overtime, etc.). A high R squared does not fix that.","d":2},{"q":"Which correlation coefficient indicates the strongest linear relationship?","o":["0.6","-0.9","0.8","-0.3"],"a":1,"e":"Strength depends on the absolute value of r. |-0.9| = 0.9 is the largest. The negative sign only means the relationship is inverse; it does not make it weaker.","d":2},{"q":"A regression of weekly sales on advertising and Urban (1 = urban store, 0 = rural store) yields a coefficient of 4,200 on Urban. What does this mean?","o":["Urban stores have sales 4,200 times higher than rural stores.","Each advertising dollar adds 4,200 to sales.","Urban stores average $4,200 less weekly sales than rural stores.","Holding advertising constant, urban stores average $4,200 more weekly sales than rural stores."],"a":3,"e":"A dummy variable coefficient is the average difference between the coded group and the baseline group, holding other variables constant. The sign is positive, so urban is higher. It is a dollar difference, not a multiplier.","d":2},{"q":"In a multiple regression output, the predictor Price has a p-value of 0.003, using a 0.05 significance level. What is the best conclusion?","o":["Price is statistically significant: there is strong evidence it is related to the response.","Price is not significant because the p-value is so small.","The model explains 0.3 percent of the variation.","Price must be removed from the model."],"a":0,"e":"A p-value below 0.05 indicates statistical significance. A small p-value means strong evidence of a relationship, not weak evidence. The p-value is not R squared.","d":2},{"q":"A utility estimates Bill = 35 + 0.11 x kWh. What does the 35 represent?","o":["The cost of each kilowatt-hour.","The percentage of variation explained by usage.","The predicted bill at zero kilowatt-hours, similar to a fixed monthly charge.","The correlation between usage and bill."],"a":2,"e":"The intercept is the predicted y when x = 0. Here it behaves like a fixed base charge. The cost per kWh is the slope (0.11).","d":2},{"q":"An analyst calculates r = 0.02 between employee shoe size and monthly sales. What should she conclude?","o":["A strong positive linear relationship exists.","There is essentially no linear relationship.","A strong negative linear relationship exists.","Shoe size weakly causes higher sales."],"a":1,"e":"An r near 0 means almost no straight-line relationship. It does not show causation in either direction.","d":2},{"q":"In a regression of sales on advertising, one store has an enormous advertising budget and unusual sales. When the analyst removes this single point, the slope changes dramatically. This point is best described as:","o":["a residual of zero","a lurking variable","a dummy variable","an influential point"],"a":3,"e":"A point whose removal substantially changes the fitted line is influential, often because it has extreme x value (high leverage). A lurking variable is a hidden third factor, and a dummy variable is a 0/1 category code.","d":2},{"q":"A company wants to be confident that a new checkout design causes higher sales. Which approach is strongest?","o":["Compute r between design use and sales across existing stores.","Observe that stores with the new design happen to have higher sales.","Run a controlled experiment that randomly assigns stores to the new or old design and compare sales.","Fit a regression and check that R squared is above 0.9."],"a":2,"e":"Random assignment controls for lurking variables, so differences can be attributed to the design. Correlation or a high R squared in observational data cannot rule out other causes.","d":2},{"q":"The correlation between hours of training and error rate is r = -0.70. What is R squared for the simple regression of error rate on training hours?","o":["-0.49","0.70","0.84","0.49"],"a":3,"e":"R squared = r squared = (-0.70) x (-0.70) = 0.49, which is always non-negative. Taking the square root of 0.70 gives 0.84, which mistakes r for R squared.","d":2},{"q":"A junior analyst's software reports a correlation of r = 1.15 between store size and monthly sales. What should the analyst conclude?","o":["There is a very strong positive relationship, even stronger than a perfect correlation of 1.","There is a strong positive relationship, but it is not quite a perfect one.","The value is impossible, so there is a calculation or data-entry error, because r must fall between -1 and +1.","The relationship is actually negative, because r is greater than 1 in this case."],"a":2,"e":"r is bounded between -1 and +1, so 1.15 signals an error. The tempting option reads 1.15 as 'very strong', but no correlation can be stronger than a perfect 1.","d":1},{"q":"A hospital administrator wants a simple regression model that predicts a patient's length of stay (in days) from the patient's age. In this model, which variable is the dependent (response) variable?","o":["Length of stay (days)","Patient age","The regression slope","The number of patients in the data set"],"a":0,"e":"The dependent variable (y) is the outcome being predicted, here length of stay. Age is the independent (predictor) variable; many students pick it only because it is mentioned first.","d":1},{"q":"A plant's maintenance cost model is Cost = 500 + 0.8 x Hours, where cost is in dollars and Hours is machine operating hours. What is the predicted maintenance cost for 1,200 operating hours?","o":["$960","$1,200","$1,700","$1,460"],"a":3,"e":"500 + 0.8(1,200) = 500 + 960 = 1,460. The $960 answer is tempting because it forgets to add the intercept (fixed cost) of $500.","d":1},{"q":"An analyst finds r = 0.62 between advertising spend (in dollars) and sales (in dollars). She re-expresses both variables in thousands of dollars and recomputes. What happens to r?","o":["It becomes 0.00062, because the values are 1,000 times smaller.","It stays 0.62, because r is unitless and is not affected by a change of scale.","It increases, because smaller numbers produce a tighter fit.","It changes sign, because the units changed."],"a":1,"e":"r is computed from standardized values, so linear rescaling does not change it (the slope and intercept would change, but not r). The tempting 0.00062 confuses r with the slope, which does depend on units.","d":1},{"q":"A retailer's regression of weekly sales on four predictors has R-squared = 0.71 and adjusted R-squared = 0.69. After adding a fifth predictor (parking spaces), R-squared rises to 0.72 but adjusted R-squared falls to 0.68. What is the best conclusion?","o":["The new predictor improves the model, because R-squared increased.","The new predictor adds little real explanatory power, so it probably should be left out of the model.","Adjusted R-squared must be an error, because it can never drop when a variable is added.","The model now explains 68 percentage points more of the variation than before."],"a":1,"e":"Adjusted R-squared penalizes predictors that do not help. R-squared never falls when a variable is added, so its small rise is not evidence of improvement. The first wrong choice is the classic trap of trusting R-squared alone.","d":2},{"q":"A model is Sales (units) = 500 + 12 x Ads ($100s) - 40 x Price ($). Which interpretation of the -40 coefficient is correct?","o":["Each $1 price increase lowers sales by 40 units, no matter what happens to advertising.","Price explains 40 percent of the variation in sales.","A price of $40 would reduce sales to zero.","Holding advertising constant, each $1 increase in price is associated with 40 fewer units sold."],"a":3,"e":"In multiple regression each slope is interpreted holding the other predictors constant. The first wrong option drops the 'holding other variables constant' condition, which is the usual exam trap.","d":2},{"q":"A manufacturer fits a straight line to predict unit cost from production volume. The plot of residuals against fitted values shows a clear U-shaped curve. What does this suggest?","o":["The linear model is appropriate, because the residuals average out to zero.","There are no outliers anywhere in the data set, so no further checks are needed.","The relationship is curved, so a straight-line model fits poorly and a nonlinear term or transformation should be considered.","The R-squared of the model must be exactly 1, because the fit is so systematic."],"a":2,"e":"A good linear model leaves a random, patternless residual plot. A U-shape means a systematic pattern the line misses. Residuals from least squares always average zero, so that fact says nothing about fit, which is why the first wrong option tempts.","d":2},{"q":"A model predicts defects as Defects = 40 - 0.5 x TrainingHours. An employee with 30 training hours actually had 22 defects. What is the residual for this employee?","o":["-3 (the model over-predicted by 3 defects)","+3 (the model under-predicted by 3 defects)","-18","25"],"a":0,"e":"Predicted = 40 - 0.5(30) = 25. Residual = actual - predicted = 22 - 25 = -3. The +3 option reverses the subtraction; 25 is the prediction, not the residual.","d":2},{"q":"A regression output shows a total sum of squares (SST) of 500 and an error (residual) sum of squares (SSE) of 120. What is R-squared?","o":["0.24","0.76","0.87","4.17"],"a":1,"e":"R-squared = 1 - SSE/SST = 1 - 120/500 = 0.76 (equivalently SSR/SST = 380/500). The 0.24 option is the unexplained share SSE/SST, and 0.87 is the square root, which is r.","d":2},{"q":"A regression of error rate on training hours gives a slope of -2.1 with a p-value of 0.21. Using a 0.05 significance level, which conclusion is best?","o":["There is not enough evidence at the 0.05 level of a linear relationship between training hours and error rate, which does not prove there is none.","Each training hour reduces the error rate by 2.1 points, and the result is statistically significant.","The data prove that training has absolutely no effect on the error rate.","The model explains 21 percent of the total variation in the error rate."],"a":0,"e":"p = 0.21 > 0.05, so we fail to reject the null hypothesis of zero slope. 'Fail to reject' is not proof of no effect. The first wrong option reads the slope's size as significance; the last confuses a p-value with R-squared.","d":2},{"q":"An Excel regression output shows R Square = 0.38 and Significance F = 0.0004. Which interpretation is best?","o":["The model is not useful at all, because its R-squared is below 0.5.","There is only a 0.04 percent chance that the model explains 38 percent of the variation.","The model is statistically significant overall, but it explains only about 38 percent of the variation in y.","The model explains 99.96 percent of the total variation in y."],"a":2,"e":"Significance F tests whether the model as a whole beats a no-predictor model; it is not a measure of strength. R-squared of 0.38 says that 38 percent of the variation is explained. The first wrong option applies a made-up 0.5 cutoff and ignores significance.","d":3},{"q":"A firm sets each year's marketing budget at 5 percent of the previous year's revenue. Analysts find a strong positive correlation between annual marketing spend and annual revenue, and the CFO concludes that marketing drives revenue. Which flaw is most evident?","o":["Extrapolation: the model is being used well outside the range of the observed data.","The correlation is too weak to support any conclusion about marketing at all.","The relationship between spend and revenue must be nonlinear, not linear.","Reverse causation: revenue helps determine marketing spend, so the correlation cannot show that marketing causes revenue."],"a":3,"e":"When the budget is a percentage of revenue, causation at least partly runs from revenue to spend. Correlation is symmetric and cannot reveal direction. Extrapolation is a different problem and nothing in the scenario describes it.","d":3},{"q":"In an Excel regression of resale value on machine age (a simple regression), the output lists Multiple R = 0.80 and a coefficient for age of -3.2 (thousand dollars per year). What is the correlation r between age and resale value?","o":["+0.80","-0.80","+0.64","-0.64"],"a":1,"e":"In simple regression Multiple R is the absolute value of r, so r takes the sign of the slope: r = -0.80. The +0.80 option reads Multiple R at face value; 0.64 is R-squared.","d":3},{"q":"The correlation between daily high temperature (standard deviation 10 degrees F) and iced-drink sales (standard deviation 50 cups) is r = 0.80. What is the slope of the regression of sales on temperature?","o":["4 cups per degree F","0.16 cups per degree F","8 cups per degree F","40 cups per degree F"],"a":0,"e":"Slope = r x (s_y / s_x) = 0.80 x (50/10) = 4. The 0.16 option inverts the ratio (0.8 x 10/50), a common mix-up; 40 is just r x s_y.","d":3},{"q":"A real estate model predicts home price from square footage and number of rooms, which are correlated at r = 0.95 with each other. The model's overall R-squared is high, but the coefficient for rooms is negative and not individually significant. What is the most likely explanation?","o":["Heteroscedasticity: the residual variance is not constant across fitted values.","Extrapolation: predictions are being made beyond the range of the data.","Multicollinearity: the predictors overlap so much that their individual coefficients are unstable and hard to interpret.","A single influential outlier is driving the whole model and flipping the sign."],"a":2,"e":"Highly correlated predictors share the same information, so the model cannot separate their effects even though the overall fit is good. Heteroscedasticity affects residual spread, not the sign flips of overlapping predictors.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Scatter plot","Graph of paired x-y data points"],["Correlation r","Strength and direction of linear relationship"],["Slope","Change in y per unit of x"],["Intercept","Predicted y when x equals zero"],["Residual","Actual value minus predicted value"],["R squared","Share of variation explained by model"],["Extrapolation","Predicting outside the observed data range"],["Dummy variable","0/1 code for a category"]],"traps":["Trap: A strong correlation proves one variable causes the other. Truth: Correlation shows association only; causation needs a controlled experiment or ruling out confounders and reverse causation.","Trap: R-squared of 0.64 means r = 0.64. Truth: r = plus or minus the square root of R-squared (here 0.80), and its sign matches the slope.","Trap: r near 0 means the variables are unrelated. Truth: r measures only linear association, so a strong curved relationship can still give r near 0.","Trap: The intercept always has a meaningful business interpretation. Truth: It is the predicted y when x = 0, which is meaningful only if x = 0 lies within the observed data range.","Trap: A significant p-value or a higher R-squared after adding a variable proves the model is better. Truth: Significance is not strength, and adjusted R-squared is needed to judge added predictors."],"mnemonic":"SIR: Slope = change per step, Intercept = start line at x = 0, R-squared = share explained; and r only says 'together', never 'because'.","cram":["r ranges from -1 to +1; its sign matches the slope's sign.","R-squared = r squared: share of variation in y explained by x.","Slope: predicted change in y for each one-unit increase in x.","Intercept: predicted y when x = 0; often not meaningful.","Residual = actual minus predicted; positive means the model under-predicted.","Never extrapolate beyond the observed range of x.","Slope p-value below 0.05 means a statistically significant linear relationship.","Adjusted R-squared penalizes useless predictors; plain R-squared never falls."],"ytv":[{"title":"Linear Regression, Clearly Explained!!!","channel":"StatQuest with Josh Starmer","id":"7ArmBVF2dCs","why":"Walks through fitting a line, R-squared and p-values with intuitive visuals."},{"title":"R-squared, Clearly Explained!!!","channel":"StatQuest with Josh Starmer","id":"2AQKmw14mHM","why":"Explains what R-squared measures and how to interpret it."},{"title":"Correlation Coefficient (r value) Explained","channel":"Dr. Jacob Goodin","id":"bi9J7ZPniVk","why":"Shows how to read the sign and strength of r."}]},{"id":"u10","title":"Business Analytics & Forecasting","day":5,"summary":"The four types of analytics, KPIs, time series components, common forecasting methods and error measures, big data and machine learning concepts, and the Excel tools analysts use.","lesson":[{"h":"1. The four types of business analytics","body":"<p><strong>Business analytics</strong> means using data and quantitative methods to support better decisions. Exam questions love to ask you to recognize which type of analytics a scenario describes. Think of it as a ladder of four steps, each answering a different question:</p><ul><li><strong>Descriptive analytics - What happened?</strong> Summarizes past data with totals, averages, percentages, charts, and dashboards. <em>Example:</em> a report showing last quarter's sales by region.</li><li><strong>Diagnostic analytics - Why did it happen?</strong> Digs into the data to find causes: drill-downs, comparisons, correlations. <em>Example:</em> comparing returns by product, supplier, and carrier to find out why returns jumped in March.</li><li><strong>Predictive analytics - What is likely to happen?</strong> Uses historical data, statistics, and machine learning to forecast. <em>Example:</em> a model that scores each customer's probability of canceling next month.</li><li><strong>Prescriptive analytics - What should we do about it?</strong> Recommends the best action, often using optimization or simulation. <em>Example:</em> software that recommends the staffing schedule that meets demand at the lowest labor cost.</li></ul><p><strong>Memory trick:</strong> Past, Why, Future, Action. Descriptive and diagnostic look backward; predictive looks forward; prescriptive recommends a decision. Value and difficulty generally increase as you climb the ladder.</p>"},{"h":"2. KPIs and metrics","body":"<p>A <strong>metric</strong> is any measurement, such as page views or units shipped. A <strong>key performance indicator (KPI)</strong> is a metric chosen because it directly tracks progress toward an important business goal. Every KPI is a metric, but most metrics are not KPIs.</p><p>Good KPIs are often described as <strong>SMART</strong>: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound. Examples by area:</p><ul><li><strong>Sales:</strong> revenue growth, conversion rate, average order value.</li><li><strong>Customer:</strong> customer satisfaction score, churn rate, net promoter score.</li><li><strong>Operations:</strong> on-time delivery rate, defect rate, cycle time.</li><li><strong>Finance:</strong> gross margin, return on investment, cash flow.</li></ul><p><strong>Leading vs lagging indicators:</strong> a <em>lagging</em> KPI reports results after the fact (quarterly revenue). A <em>leading</em> KPI gives an early signal of future results (number of qualified sales leads this week). A <strong>dashboard</strong> displays a handful of KPIs visually so managers can monitor them at a glance. Always compare a KPI against a target or benchmark; a number alone means little.</p>"},{"h":"3. Time series and its components","body":"<p>A <strong>time series</strong> is a set of observations of the same measure taken at regular time intervals, such as monthly sales for six years. Before forecasting, analysts break a time series into four components:</p><ul><li><strong>Trend:</strong> the long-term upward or downward movement (for example, subscribers growing steadily over eight years).</li><li><strong>Seasonality:</strong> a repeating pattern tied to the calendar that returns at fixed intervals, usually within a year (ski jacket sales peak every November and December; ice cream peaks every summer).</li><li><strong>Cyclical:</strong> longer, irregular up-and-down swings, usually tied to the business or economic cycle, lasting more than a year with no fixed length (recession and expansion).</li><li><strong>Irregular (random):</strong> unpredictable noise, such as a one-time strike, storm, or data glitch.</li></ul><p>The key distinction on the exam: <strong>seasonality has a fixed, known period; cyclical does not.</strong> If a pattern repeats every December, it is seasonal. If sales rise and fall over multi-year economic booms and busts, it is cyclical.</p><p>Plotting a time series as a line chart is always the first step; it shows which components are present and guides the choice of forecasting method.</p>"},{"h":"4. Forecasting methods you can calculate by hand","body":"<p>A <strong>forecast</strong> is a prediction of a future value. Quantitative methods use historical numbers:</p><ul><li><strong>Naive forecast:</strong> next period's forecast equals the most recent actual. If last month's sales were 120, forecast 120. Simple, and a useful baseline to beat.</li><li><strong>Moving average (MA):</strong> the average of the most recent n periods. A 3-period MA forecast for April = (Jan + Feb + Mar) / 3. It smooths random noise but lags behind trends. More periods means smoother but slower to react.</li><li><strong>Weighted moving average (WMA):</strong> like MA, but recent periods get bigger weights (weights sum to 1). For example 0.5 on the latest month, 0.3 on the one before, and 0.2 on the oldest.</li><li><strong>Exponential smoothing:</strong> new forecast = alpha x latest actual + (1 - alpha) x previous forecast. The smoothing constant <strong>alpha</strong> is between 0 and 1. A high alpha (like 0.8) reacts fast to recent changes; a low alpha (like 0.1) gives a smooth, stable forecast.</li><li><strong>Regression trend:</strong> fit a line with time as x (period 1, 2, 3, ...) and sales as y, then plug in a future period number. In Excel: <code>FORECAST</code>/<code>FORECAST.LINEAR</code> or <code>TREND</code>. This captures a steady trend, which a simple moving average cannot.</li></ul><p><strong>Worked example (3-period moving average):</strong> sales were Jan 50, Feb 54, Mar 58, Apr 56, May 62. Forecast for April = (50 + 54 + 58)/3 = 54. Forecast for May = (54 + 58 + 56)/3 = 56. Forecast for June = (58 + 56 + 62)/3 = 58.67.</p>"},{"h":"5. Measuring forecast error: MAD, MSE, MAPE","body":"<p>No forecast is perfect, so we measure how wrong it is. For each period, <strong>error = actual minus forecast</strong>. Errors of +5 and -5 would cancel if simply averaged, so we use measures that remove the sign:</p><ul><li><strong>MAD (Mean Absolute Deviation, also Mean Absolute Error):</strong> the average of the absolute errors. In the same units as the data and easy to explain.</li><li><strong>MSE (Mean Squared Error):</strong> the average of the squared errors. Squaring punishes big misses much more than small ones, so it highlights occasional large errors. Units are squared.</li><li><strong>MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error):</strong> the average of |error| divided by actual, expressed as a percent. It is unit-free, so you can compare accuracy across products of different sizes.</li></ul><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> actual 100, 110, 120, 130; forecast 95, 115, 118, 135. Errors: +5, -5, +2, -5. Absolute errors: 5, 5, 2, 5 so MAD = 17/4 = 4.25. Squared errors: 25, 25, 4, 25 so MSE = 79/4 = 19.75. Percentage errors: 5.00, 4.55, 1.67, 3.85 percent, so MAPE = about 3.76 percent.</p><p><strong>Rule:</strong> lower is better for all three. Use them to compare competing forecasting methods on the same data and pick the one with the smallest error. Also, the average of the signed errors (<em>bias</em>) tells you whether the method systematically over- or under-forecasts.</p>"},{"h":"6. Qualitative forecasting, big data, data mining, and machine learning","body":"<p><strong>Qualitative (judgmental) forecasting</strong> is used when there is no history (a brand new product) or when conditions are changing. Methods include:</p><ul><li><strong>Delphi method:</strong> a panel of experts answers questionnaires anonymously in several rounds, seeing a summary of the group's answers between rounds, until opinions converge. Anonymity reduces the influence of loud or senior voices.</li><li><strong>Expert opinion / executive judgment:</strong> managers or specialists give their best estimates.</li><li><strong>Market research:</strong> surveys, focus groups, and test markets to gauge customer demand.</li><li><strong>Sales force composite:</strong> salespeople estimate demand in their territories.</li></ul><p><strong>Big data</strong> refers to data sets too large or complex for traditional tools, described by the <strong>5 Vs</strong>: <strong>Volume</strong> (how much), <strong>Velocity</strong> (how fast it arrives), <strong>Variety</strong> (structured, text, images, sensor feeds), <strong>Veracity</strong> (how accurate and trustworthy), and <strong>Value</strong> (the worth the data can deliver).</p><p><strong>Data mining</strong> is the process of discovering patterns, clusters, or relationships in large data sets (for example, market basket analysis finding that customers who buy bread often buy butter). <strong>Machine learning</strong> is a branch of artificial intelligence in which algorithms learn patterns from data and improve without being explicitly programmed with rules. In <em>supervised learning</em> the algorithm learns from labeled examples (past customers who did or did not churn) to predict outcomes; in <em>unsupervised learning</em> it finds structure in unlabeled data (grouping customers into segments).</p>"},{"h":"7. Excel tools analysts use","body":"<p>You should recognize what these common spreadsheet features do:</p><ul><li><code>AVERAGE</code>, <code>MEDIAN</code>, <code>MODE</code>: measures of center. <code>STDEV.S</code>: sample standard deviation (the <code>.S</code> means sample; <code>STDEV.P</code> is for a whole population). <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>, <code>COUNT</code>.</li><li><code>CORREL(range1, range2)</code>: correlation coefficient. <code>SLOPE</code>, <code>INTERCEPT</code>, <code>RSQ</code>: regression pieces.</li><li><code>FORECAST.LINEAR</code> (or older <code>FORECAST</code>) and <code>TREND</code>: predict a value from a linear trend.</li><li><code>SUMIF</code>, <code>COUNTIF</code>, <code>AVERAGEIF</code>: calculate only for rows meeting a condition. <code>VLOOKUP</code>/<code>XLOOKUP</code>: look up a value from a table.</li><li><strong>PivotTables:</strong> drag-and-drop summaries that group, count, sum, or average thousands of rows by category (sales by region and month) without writing formulas. Pair them with PivotCharts.</li><li><strong>What-if analysis:</strong> <em>Goal Seek</em> (find the input needed to reach a target output), <em>Data Tables</em> (see results for a range of inputs), and <em>Scenario Manager</em> (compare saved sets of assumptions such as best, expected, and worst case).</li><li><strong>Data Analysis ToolPak:</strong> add-in providing regression, descriptive statistics, histograms, and moving average and exponential smoothing tools.</li></ul><p>Good habits: keep raw data separate from calculations, label units, and check for missing or duplicated values before analysis (garbage in, garbage out).</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Business analytics comes in four types: descriptive analytics tells you what happened, diagnostic explains why it happened, predictive estimates what is likely to happen next, and prescriptive recommends what action to take.","A key performance indicator is a metric chosen because it tracks progress toward an important business goal, and it should be specific, measurable, and compared against a target.","A time series has four components: trend is the long-term direction, seasonality is a repeating pattern at fixed calendar intervals, cyclical swings are longer irregular waves, and irregular variation is random noise.","Seasonality has a fixed known period such as every December, while cyclical movement follows business cycles with no fixed length.","A naive forecast simply uses the last actual value as the next forecast, and it makes a useful baseline for comparing other methods.","A moving average forecast is the average of the most recent periods, and a weighted moving average gives larger weights to more recent periods.","Exponential smoothing combines the latest actual value and the previous forecast, and a higher smoothing constant alpha makes the forecast react faster to recent changes.","Forecast error is the actual value minus the forecast, and the three common summary measures are MAD, the average absolute error, MSE, the average squared error, and MAPE, the average absolute percentage error.","For all forecast error measures, lower is better, and MSE punishes large misses more heavily than MAD does.","Qualitative forecasting methods such as the Delphi method, expert opinion, and market research are used when historical data is missing or unreliable.","Big data is described by the five Vs: volume, velocity, variety, veracity, and value.","Data mining discovers patterns in large data sets, machine learning lets algorithms learn from data, and Excel tools like CORREL, FORECAST, PivotTables, and Goal Seek support everyday analysis."],"examples":[{"q":"A store's monthly unit sales were Jan 50, Feb 54, Mar 58, Apr 56, May 62. (a) Use a 3-month moving average to forecast April, May, and June. (b) Use a weighted moving average with weights 0.5, 0.3, 0.2 (most recent first) to forecast June.","steps":["(a) Forecast for April uses Jan, Feb, Mar: (50 + 54 + 58) / 3 = 162 / 3 = 54.","Forecast for May uses Feb, Mar, Apr: (54 + 58 + 56) / 3 = 168 / 3 = 56.","Forecast for June uses Mar, Apr, May: (58 + 56 + 62) / 3 = 176 / 3 = 58.67.","(b) For June the most recent month is May (62), then April (56), then March (58).","Weighted: 0.5(62) + 0.3(56) + 0.2(58) = 31.0 + 16.8 + 11.6 = 59.4.","The weighted forecast (59.4) is higher than the simple one (58.67) because it counts the latest, higher value more heavily."],"answer":"(a) April 54, May 56, June about 58.67. (b) Weighted June forecast = 59.4."},{"q":"A company's forecasts and actual demand for four months were: Actual = 100, 110, 120, 130 and Forecast = 95, 115, 118, 135. Compute MAD, MSE, and MAPE.","steps":["Errors (actual minus forecast): 100-95 = +5; 110-115 = -5; 120-118 = +2; 130-135 = -5.","Absolute errors: 5, 5, 2, 5. Sum = 17. MAD = 17 / 4 = 4.25 units.","Squared errors: 25, 25, 4, 25. Sum = 79. MSE = 79 / 4 = 19.75.","Absolute percentage errors: 5/100 = 5.00%; 5/110 = 4.55%; 2/120 = 1.67%; 5/130 = 3.85%. Sum = 15.06%.","MAPE = 15.06% / 4 = about 3.76%.","Interpretation: on average the forecast is off by about 4.25 units, or about 3.8 percent of actual demand."],"answer":"MAD = 4.25, MSE = 19.75, MAPE is about 3.76 percent."},{"q":"Using exponential smoothing with alpha = 0.3, last month's forecast was 50 units and last month's actual demand was 60 units. What is this month's forecast? What would it be if alpha = 0.8?","steps":["Formula: new forecast = alpha x actual + (1 - alpha) x old forecast.","With alpha = 0.3: 0.3(60) + 0.7(50) = 18 + 35 = 53.","With alpha = 0.8: 0.8(60) + 0.2(50) = 48 + 10 = 58.","The higher alpha moves the forecast much closer to the latest actual (60), so it reacts faster; the lower alpha stays closer to the old forecast (50) and is smoother."],"answer":"Forecast = 53 with alpha 0.3, and 58 with alpha 0.8."}],"videos":[{"title":"Types of analytics: descriptive, diagnostic, predictive, prescriptive","query":"descriptive diagnostic predictive prescriptive analytics explained"},{"title":"Time series forecasting: moving average and exponential smoothing","query":"moving average and exponential smoothing forecasting explained"},{"title":"Forecast accuracy: MAD, MSE, and MAPE","query":"forecast error MAD MSE MAPE explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Descriptive analytics","b":"Answers 'What happened?' by summarizing past data (reports, dashboards, averages, totals)."},{"f":"Diagnostic analytics","b":"Answers 'Why did it happen?' by drilling into data to find causes."},{"f":"Predictive analytics","b":"Answers 'What is likely to happen?' using models and historical data."},{"f":"Prescriptive analytics","b":"Answers 'What should we do?' by recommending the best action (optimization, simulation)."},{"f":"KPI (key performance indicator)","b":"A metric chosen because it directly tracks progress toward an important business goal."},{"f":"Leading vs lagging indicator","b":"Leading gives an early signal of future results (qualified leads); lagging reports results after the fact (quarterly revenue)."},{"f":"Trend component","b":"Long-term upward or downward movement in a time series."},{"f":"Seasonality","b":"A repeating pattern tied to the calendar at a fixed, known interval (such as every December)."},{"f":"Cyclical component","b":"Longer, irregular swings (over a year, no fixed length) tied to business or economic cycles."},{"f":"Irregular (random) component","b":"Unpredictable noise from one-time events such as storms or strikes."},{"f":"Naive forecast","b":"Next forecast = the most recent actual value."},{"f":"Moving average forecast","b":"The average of the most recent n periods; smooths noise but lags trends."},{"f":"Weighted moving average","b":"A moving average giving larger weights (that sum to 1) to more recent periods."},{"f":"Exponential smoothing formula","b":"New forecast = alpha x latest actual + (1 - alpha) x previous forecast."},{"f":"Effect of a high vs low alpha","b":"High alpha reacts quickly to recent changes; low alpha gives a smoother, slower forecast."},{"f":"MAD","b":"Mean Absolute Deviation: the average of the absolute forecast errors."},{"f":"MSE","b":"Mean Squared Error: the average of squared errors; penalizes large misses heavily."},{"f":"MAPE","b":"Mean Absolute Percentage Error: the average of |error| / actual as a percent; unit-free."},{"f":"Delphi method","b":"Experts answer anonymous questionnaires in rounds with feedback until opinions converge."},{"f":"The 5 Vs of big data","b":"Volume, Velocity, Variety, Veracity, Value."},{"f":"Data mining vs machine learning","b":"Data mining discovers patterns in large data sets; machine learning algorithms learn from data to make predictions."},{"f":"Excel functions: CORREL, FORECAST.LINEAR, STDEV.S","b":"Correlation coefficient; predict from a linear trend; sample standard deviation."}],"questions":[{"q":"A regional manager opens a dashboard that shows total sales and units sold for each region last quarter. Which type of analytics is this?","o":["Descriptive","Predictive","Prescriptive","Diagnostic"],"a":0,"e":"Summarizing what already happened is descriptive analytics. Diagnostic would explain why, predictive would estimate the future, and prescriptive would recommend an action.","d":2},{"q":"Product returns jumped sharply in March. An analyst compares returns by product line, supplier, and shipping carrier to determine what caused the spike. Which type of analytics is this?","o":["Descriptive","Predictive","Diagnostic","Prescriptive"],"a":2,"e":"Investigating why something happened is diagnostic analytics. Simply reporting the spike would be descriptive.","d":2},{"q":"A telecom company builds a model that gives each customer a probability of canceling service within the next 30 days. Which type of analytics is this?","o":["Descriptive","Predictive","Diagnostic","Prescriptive"],"a":1,"e":"Estimating what is likely to happen in the future is predictive analytics. It does not itself recommend an action, which would be prescriptive.","d":2},{"q":"A logistics tool analyzes demand, driver availability, and traffic and then recommends the delivery routes that minimize total cost. Which type of analytics is this?","o":["Descriptive","Diagnostic","Predictive","Prescriptive"],"a":3,"e":"Recommending the best action using optimization is prescriptive analytics, the most advanced of the four.","d":2},{"q":"Ski jacket sales at a retailer peak every November and December year after year. Which time series component does this show?","o":["Trend","Irregular variation","Seasonality","Cyclical variation"],"a":2,"e":"A pattern that repeats at fixed calendar intervals is seasonality. Cyclical swings do not have a fixed period and last longer than a year.","d":2},{"q":"Over eight years, a software company's subscriber count rises steadily from 10,000 to 60,000. Which time series component is this?","o":["Trend","Seasonality","Irregular variation","Cyclical variation"],"a":0,"e":"A long-term, sustained upward movement is a trend.","d":2},{"q":"Monthly sales were 40, 44, and 48 units for the last three months. Using a 3-month moving average, what is the forecast for next month?","o":["40","44","48","132"],"a":1,"e":"(40 + 44 + 48) / 3 = 132 / 3 = 44. The 132 is the sum before dividing. Note the moving average lags a rising trend.","d":2},{"q":"Last month's actual demand was 120 and the forecast for last month was 110. Using the naive forecasting method, what is the forecast for next month?","o":["110","115","130","120"],"a":3,"e":"The naive method uses the most recent actual value as the next forecast, so 120.","d":2},{"q":"A forecaster's errors (actual minus forecast) over four periods were +4, -2, +6, and -4. What is the MAD?","o":["1","16","4","6"],"a":2,"e":"Absolute errors are 4, 2, 6, 4. Their sum is 16 and 16 / 4 = 4. The value 1 is the average of the signed errors, which is bias, not MAD.","d":2},{"q":"Which statement best describes MAPE?","o":["The average of the absolute errors expressed as a percentage of the actual values","The average of the squared errors","The single largest forecast error","The sum of the errors divided by the forecast"],"a":0,"e":"MAPE averages absolute percentage errors, which makes it unit-free and good for comparing accuracy across products with different scales. The average of squared errors is MSE.","d":2},{"q":"A company is launching a completely new product with no sales history. It asks a panel of outside experts to answer questionnaires anonymously over several rounds, sharing a summary of responses after each round. Which method is this?","o":["Regression trend forecasting","Moving average","Naive forecast","Delphi method"],"a":3,"e":"Anonymous, multi-round expert questionnaires with feedback is the Delphi method, a qualitative forecasting technique suited to situations without historical data.","d":2},{"q":"A social media platform ingests millions of new posts every second. Which of the 5 Vs of big data does this most directly describe?","o":["Volume","Velocity","Veracity","Value"],"a":1,"e":"The speed at which data is generated and must be processed is velocity. Volume refers to the sheer amount stored, although both are present here.","d":2},{"q":"Which Excel function returns the correlation coefficient between two ranges of data?","o":["SLOPE","AVERAGEIF","CORREL","STDEV.S"],"a":2,"e":"CORREL(range1, range2) returns r. SLOPE returns the regression slope, AVERAGEIF averages with a condition, and STDEV.S gives the sample standard deviation.","d":2},{"q":"A bank trains an algorithm on thousands of past loans labeled as repaid or defaulted so that it can predict which new applicants are likely to default. This is an example of:","o":["supervised machine learning","descriptive reporting","a PivotTable","the Delphi method"],"a":0,"e":"Learning from labeled historical examples to predict outcomes is supervised machine learning, a tool of predictive analytics.","d":2},{"q":"In exponential smoothing, what is the effect of choosing a high smoothing constant such as alpha = 0.9?","o":["The forecast ignores the most recent actual value.","All past periods receive equal weight.","The forecast becomes smoother and slower to react.","The forecast gives heavy weight to the most recent actual value and reacts quickly to changes."],"a":3,"e":"With alpha near 1 the forecast is dominated by the latest actual. A low alpha produces a smoother, slower forecast.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst has a spreadsheet of 50,000 sales rows and wants to quickly see total sales by region and by month without writing formulas for each combination. Which Excel feature is best?","o":["Goal Seek","PivotTable","Scenario Manager","FORECAST.LINEAR"],"a":1,"e":"PivotTables summarize and cross-tabulate large lists by category. Goal Seek and Scenario Manager are what-if tools, and FORECAST.LINEAR predicts a value from a trend.","d":2},{"q":"A manufacturer wants to analyze structured ERP tables, free-text warranty claims, photos of defective parts, and machine sensor streams together. Which of the 5 Vs of big data does this mix of data types most directly describe?","o":["Volume","Variety","Velocity","Veracity"],"a":1,"e":"Variety refers to the many formats of data (structured, text, images, streams). Volume, the tempting choice, is about sheer quantity, which the stem does not emphasize.","d":1},{"q":"A customer support center's goal is to resolve customer issues faster. Which metric is the most appropriate KPI?","o":["Total number of tickets received this month","Number of agents hired this year","Monthly software license cost","Average time to resolve a ticket (hours)"],"a":3,"e":"A KPI must measure progress toward the stated objective, here speed of resolution. Ticket volume is tempting because it is easy to track, but it measures demand, not resolution speed.","d":1},{"q":"Housing starts in a region rise during multi-year economic expansions and fall during recessions, with peaks several years apart at irregular intervals. Which time series component does this describe?","o":["Cyclical","Seasonal","Trend","Irregular (random)"],"a":0,"e":"Cyclical movements last multiple years and have irregular length. Seasonal patterns repeat at fixed, known intervals within a year, which is the tempting but wrong choice.","d":1},{"q":"Which Excel function fits a straight line through existing x and y data to predict a future y value?","o":["CORREL","VLOOKUP","FORECAST.LINEAR","COUNTIF"],"a":2,"e":"FORECAST.LINEAR returns a predicted y for a given x along the least-squares line. CORREL only returns the correlation coefficient and does not produce a prediction.","d":1},{"q":"A planner uses a 3-month weighted moving average with weights of 0.5 for the most recent month, 0.3 for the month before, and 0.2 for the oldest month. Sales for the last three months (oldest to newest) were 100, 120, and 140 units. What is next month's forecast?","o":["114 units","120 units","126 units","140 units"],"a":2,"e":"0.2(100) + 0.3(120) + 0.5(140) = 20 + 36 + 70 = 126. The 120 option is the simple (unweighted) average, and 114 results from reversing the weights.","d":2},{"q":"Using exponential smoothing with alpha = 0.3, last period's actual demand was 200 and last period's forecast was 180. What is the forecast for the next period?","o":["186","180","194","200"],"a":0,"e":"F(next) = alpha x Actual + (1 - alpha) x Forecast = 0.3(200) + 0.7(180) = 60 + 126 = 186. The 194 option swaps the weights (0.7 on the actual).","d":2},{"q":"A forecaster's errors (actual minus forecast) over four periods were +5, -3, +2, and -6 units. What is the MSE?","o":["4.0","4.3","74","18.5"],"a":3,"e":"MSE averages the squared errors: (25 + 9 + 4 + 36) / 4 = 74 / 4 = 18.5. The 4.0 option is the MAD (average absolute error), 4.3 is the square root of the MSE, and 74 is the total squared error before dividing.","d":2},{"q":"Two periods of forecasts: in period 1 the actual was 50 and the forecast was 40; in period 2 the actual was 100 and the forecast was 110. What is the MAPE?","o":["10%","15%","20%","30%"],"a":1,"e":"Percent errors are 10/50 = 20% and 10/100 = 10%, which average to 15%. The 10% option mistakes the MAD of 10 units for a percentage, and 30% adds the percentages without averaging.","d":2},{"q":"Quarterly sales (units): Year 1 - Q1 80, Q2 90, Q3 85, Q4 130; Year 2 - Q1 90, Q2 100, Q3 95, Q4 140. Which pattern is present?","o":["An upward trend combined with a recurring Q4 seasonal peak","Seasonality only, with no trend","A multi-year cyclical pattern of irregular length","Irregular (random) variation only"],"a":0,"e":"Every quarter is 10 units higher in Year 2 (trend) and Q4 is the high quarter both years (seasonality). Choosing 'seasonality only' overlooks the year-over-year increase.","d":2},{"q":"A retailer wants to automatically group its 200,000 customers into segments with similar buying behavior, but no segment labels exist beforehand. Which approach fits best?","o":["Supervised classification","Time series decomposition","Unsupervised learning (clustering)","Prescriptive optimization"],"a":2,"e":"With no labeled outcome, the algorithm must find structure on its own, which is unsupervised learning. Supervised classification needs labeled examples, which is why it is a tempting but wrong pick.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst uses exponential smoothing with alpha = 0.2. The forecast for period 1 was 100. Actual demand was 110 in period 1 and 90 in period 2. What is the forecast for period 3?","o":["90.0","99.6","100.0","102.0"],"a":1,"e":"F2 = 0.2(110) + 0.8(100) = 102. F3 = 0.2(90) + 0.8(102) = 18 + 81.6 = 99.6. The 102.0 option is only the period 2 forecast, and 90 would be a naive forecast.","d":3},{"q":"Two forecasting methods were tested on the same 12 months of demand. Method A had MAD = 12 and MSE = 400. Method B had MAD = 10 and MSE = 600. Which conclusion is best supported?","o":["Method B is better on every accuracy measure, so it should clearly be chosen.","Method A is worse because its MAD is higher, so the MSE does not matter at all.","The values must be wrong, because an MSE should always be smaller than the MAD.","Method B has the smaller typical error, but its higher MSE suggests a few big misses; if those are costly, A may be better."],"a":3,"e":"MSE squares errors, so it is far more sensitive to large misses than MAD. B wins on MAD but loses on MSE, so 'better on every measure' is false. MSE and MAD are in different units, so MSE can legitimately be larger.","d":3},{"q":"A company forecasts monthly demand for Product X (about 10,000 units per month) and Product Y (about 50 units per month). The planner wants to compare forecast accuracy across the two products. Which measure is most appropriate?","o":["MAPE","MAD","MSE","The sum of the raw errors"],"a":0,"e":"MAPE expresses error as a percentage of actual demand, so it is scale-free and comparable across products. MAD is in units, so Product X would nearly always look worse simply because it is bigger.","d":3},{"q":"A demand planner switches from a 3-month to a 12-month moving average for a product with a strong, steady upward trend. What is the likely effect?","o":["Forecasts react faster to each month's change in sales, so they track the trend closely.","Forecasts become smoother but lag further behind the rising trend, tending to under-forecast.","Forecast errors fall to near zero because so many more data points are used.","The trend is removed, so the forecasts no longer lag behind actual demand."],"a":1,"e":"More periods average more old, lower values, so the forecast is smoother but slower to catch up when demand is rising. The first wrong option has it backward: responsiveness comes from fewer periods.","d":3},{"q":"A subscription company does the following. Step 1: reports that churn last quarter was 8%. Step 2: finds that churn was concentrated among customers with 3 or more unresolved support tickets. Step 3: scores every current customer on the likelihood of canceling in the next 60 days. Step 4: recommends which customers should get a retention discount to maximize net retained revenue. Which sequence correctly labels steps 1 to 4?","o":["Diagnostic, descriptive, prescriptive, predictive","Descriptive, predictive, diagnostic, prescriptive","Descriptive, diagnostic, predictive, prescriptive","Predictive, diagnostic, descriptive, prescriptive"],"a":2,"e":"Step 1 states what happened (descriptive); step 2 explains why (diagnostic); step 3 estimates what will happen (predictive); step 4 recommends an action (prescriptive).","d":3}],"pairs":[["Descriptive","What happened in the past"],["Diagnostic","Why did it happen"],["Predictive","What is likely to happen"],["Prescriptive","What action should we take"],["Seasonality","Repeating pattern within a year"],["MAD","Average of absolute forecast errors"],["Delphi method","Anonymous expert rounds toward consensus"],["Velocity","Speed at which data arrives"]],"traps":["Trap: Descriptive analytics explains why something happened. Truth: Descriptive shows what happened, diagnostic explains why, predictive estimates what will happen, and prescriptive recommends what to do.","Trap: A higher smoothing constant (alpha) produces smoother forecasts. Truth: High alpha weights recent data heavily, so forecasts react quickly and are jumpier; low alpha smooths.","Trap: Seasonal and cyclical patterns are the same thing. Truth: Seasonal repeats at fixed, known intervals within a year; cyclical lasts multiple years with irregular length.","Trap: Averaging the raw forecast errors gives MAD. Truth: Positive and negative errors cancel, so MAD averages absolute errors; MSE squares them and MAPE uses percentages.","Trap: A moving average with more periods responds faster to change. Truth: More periods give a smoother forecast that lags more behind trends."],"mnemonic":"ALPHA = AGILITY: high alpha is agile but jittery, low alpha is calm but laggy. Analytics types follow a doctor: vital signs, diagnosis, prognosis, prescription.","cram":["Descriptive: what happened; diagnostic: why; predictive: what next; prescriptive: what to do.","Time series components: trend, seasonal, cyclical, irregular.","Simple moving average: mean of last n actuals; larger n smooths and lags.","Exponential smoothing: F = alpha x actual + (1 - alpha) x old forecast.","MAD: mean absolute error; MSE: mean squared error; MAPE: mean percent error.","MSE punishes large errors; MAPE compares accuracy across different scales.","5 Vs of big data: volume, velocity, variety, veracity, value.","Excel: CORREL, FORECAST.LINEAR, and PivotTables to summarize large data."],"ytv":[{"title":"Descriptive vs Diagnostic vs Predictive vs Prescriptive Analytics Explained | Data Analytics Types","channel":"Learn with Whiteboard","id":"FI767JZRLqE","why":"Clear whiteboard comparison of the four analytics types with examples."},{"title":"Operations Management: Forecast Errors (MSE, MAD, MAPE)","channel":"The Business Doctor","id":"5W8K7iUJoYc","why":"Shows how to calculate and compare MAD, MSE and MAPE."},{"title":"Demand Forecasting Methods - Moving Averages, Exponential Smoothing and Trend Projection","channel":"My Lean University","id":"4GawJJlOJSs","why":"Worked examples of moving average and exponential smoothing forecasts."}]},{"id":"u11","title":"Quality & Process Improvement","day":6,"summary":"Six Sigma and DMAIC, Lean waste, control charts and process capability, and the classic quality tools such as Pareto charts, fishbone diagrams, 5 Whys, and PDCA.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Quality, variation, and Six Sigma","body":"<p>Every process, from making brake pads to answering support tickets, produces results that vary a little each time. <strong>Quality management</strong> is about delivering what customers need consistently, and the enemy of consistency is <strong>variation</strong>.</p><p><strong>Six Sigma</strong> is a data-driven method for improving quality by reducing variation and defects. The name comes from statistics: sigma is the standard deviation. A process at <strong>six sigma</strong> quality has its specification limits six standard deviations from the process average, which works out to just <strong>3.4 defects per million opportunities (DPMO)</strong> (this figure builds in the common allowance that a process mean can drift by 1.5 sigma over time).</p><ul><li>3 sigma is about 66,807 DPMO; 4 sigma about 6,210; 5 sigma about 233; 6 sigma 3.4.</li><li><strong>DPMO = (defects / (units x opportunities per unit)) x 1,000,000.</strong> Example: 50 defects found in 500 units with 4 opportunities per unit gives 50 / 2,000 = 0.025, or 25,000 DPMO.</li></ul><p>Six Sigma projects are run by trained people with martial-arts-style titles: Champions (sponsor executives), Master Black Belts, Black Belts (lead projects full time), Green Belts (lead smaller projects part time), and Yellow Belts (team members). Projects follow a structured method called DMAIC.</p>"},{"h":"2. DMAIC: the five phases and their tools","body":"<p><strong>DMAIC</strong> (say it \"duh-MAY-ik\") is the Six Sigma roadmap for improving an <em>existing</em> process. Know the phases in order and which tools belong where.</p><ol><li><strong>Define</strong> - Clarify the problem, the customer requirements, and the project goal. Tools: project charter, problem and goal statement, voice of the customer (VOC), SIPOC diagram (Suppliers, Inputs, Process, Outputs, Customers), stakeholder analysis.</li><li><strong>Measure</strong> - Collect data and establish the current baseline performance. Tools: process map/flowchart, data collection plan, check sheets, measurement system analysis, baseline sigma level or DPMO, capability analysis.</li><li><strong>Analyze</strong> - Find the root causes of the problem. Tools: fishbone (cause-and-effect) diagram, 5 Whys, Pareto chart, scatter plots and regression, hypothesis tests, value stream mapping.</li><li><strong>Improve</strong> - Develop, test, and implement solutions. Tools: brainstorming, pilot tests, design of experiments, FMEA (failure modes and effects analysis), mistake-proofing (poka-yoke).</li><li><strong>Control</strong> - Sustain the gains. Tools: control charts, standard operating procedures, control plan, training, ongoing monitoring.</li></ol><p><strong>Quick sorting rule for the exam:</strong> goals and customer needs = Define; collecting data/baseline = Measure; finding causes = Analyze; fixing and testing = Improve; control charts and standardizing = Control. (DMADV is a related method used to design a brand <em>new</em> process.)</p>"},{"h":"3. Lean and the wastes","body":"<p><strong>Lean</strong> comes from the Toyota Production System. Its goal is to maximize customer value while eliminating <strong>waste</strong> (called <em>muda</em> in Japanese): any activity that uses resources but does not add value the customer is willing to pay for. Lean focuses on speed and flow; Six Sigma focuses on variation and defects. <strong>Lean Six Sigma</strong> combines both: faster, cleaner processes with fewer defects.</p><p>The classic wastes can be remembered with the acronym <strong>DOWNTIME</strong> (or TIMWOODS):</p><ul><li><strong>D - Defects:</strong> rework and scrap.</li><li><strong>O - Overproduction:</strong> making more than is needed or sooner than needed.</li><li><strong>W - Waiting:</strong> idle time for people, parts, or approvals.</li><li><strong>N - Non-utilized talent:</strong> not using people's skills and ideas.</li><li><strong>T - Transportation:</strong> unnecessary movement of materials or products.</li><li><strong>I - Inventory:</strong> excess stock, raw materials, or work in process.</li><li><strong>M - Motion:</strong> unnecessary movement of people (walking, reaching, searching for tools).</li><li><strong>E - Extra processing:</strong> doing more work than the customer requires (over-processing).</li></ul><p>TIMWOODS lists the same ideas: Transportation, Inventory, Motion, Waiting, Overproduction, Over-processing, Defects, Skills (under-used). <strong>Distinguish Motion (people moving) from Transportation (product/material moving).</strong> Other Lean tools: 5S workplace organization, kaizen (continuous improvement), just-in-time, value stream mapping, kanban.</p>"},{"h":"4. Control charts: telling signal from noise","body":"<p>A <strong>control chart</strong> plots a process measure over time with three lines: a <strong>center line</strong> (the process average), an <strong>upper control limit (UCL)</strong> and a <strong>lower control limit (LCL)</strong>. The limits are usually set at <strong>three standard deviations</strong> (plus and minus 3 sigma) from the center line, based on the process's own data. They are <em>not</em> the customer's specification limits.</p><p>Variation comes in two kinds:</p><ul><li><strong>Common-cause variation:</strong> natural, always-present randomness built into the process. Points stay inside the limits with no pattern. The process is <strong>in control</strong> (stable and predictable). Reducing it requires changing the system, which is management's responsibility.</li><li><strong>Special-cause (assignable) variation:</strong> unusual events such as a broken tool, a bad batch of material, or an untrained operator. The process is <strong>out of control</strong>. Investigate and eliminate the specific cause.</li></ul><p><strong>Out-of-control signals:</strong></p><ul><li>One or more points beyond the UCL or LCL.</li><li>A long run (about 7 to 9 depending on the rule set) of consecutive points all on one side of the center line, which signals a shift.</li><li>A steady trend of many points climbing or falling.</li><li>Other non-random patterns, such as cycles or points hugging one limit.</li></ul><p><strong>Do not tamper:</strong> reacting to every random wiggle inside the limits (adjusting a machine because one reading was a little high) adds variation and makes things worse.</p>"},{"h":"5. Types of control charts and process capability","body":"<p>Choose the chart by the type of data:</p><ul><li><strong>Variables data</strong> (measured on a scale: length, weight, time): use an <strong>X-bar chart</strong> to track the sample <em>average</em> (monitors the process center) and an <strong>R chart</strong> to track the sample <em>range</em> (highest minus lowest; monitors the spread). They are used together. For samples of 5, the limits are X-double-bar plus or minus 0.577 x R-bar for the X-bar chart; and for the R chart UCL = 2.114 x R-bar, LCL = 0.</li><li><strong>Attribute data</strong> (counted: defective or not): use a <strong>p chart</strong> for the <em>proportion</em> of defective items in each sample. Limits: p-bar plus or minus 3 x square root of (p-bar x (1 - p-bar) / n). If the lower limit calculates below zero, use 0. A <strong>c chart</strong> counts defects per unit (for example scratches on a panel).</li></ul><p><strong>Process capability</strong> asks a different question: is the process able to meet the customer's <em>specification limits</em> (USL and LSL)? Control charts say if a process is stable; capability says if a stable process is good enough.</p><ul><li><strong>Cp = (USL - LSL) / (6 x sigma)</strong>: the spec width compared with the natural process spread, ignoring centering. Cp of 1.0 just fits; above 1.33 is generally considered capable.</li><li><strong>Cpk = the smaller of (USL - mean) / (3 sigma) and (mean - LSL) / (3 sigma)</strong>: also accounts for how well centered the process is. Cpk is always less than or equal to Cp; if they are far apart, the process is off-center. Cpk below 1 means the process is producing out-of-spec items.</li></ul>"},{"h":"6. Pareto, fishbone, 5 Whys, check sheets, and flowcharts","body":"<p>These are the 'basic quality tools' (often called the seven basic tools, along with histograms, scatter diagrams, and control charts):</p><ul><li><strong>Check sheet:</strong> a simple form for recording how often events or defects occur as data is gathered. A tally sheet is the typical example (a Measure-phase tool).</li><li><strong>Pareto chart:</strong> a bar chart with categories sorted from largest to smallest and a cumulative percent line. It reflects the <strong>80/20 rule</strong> (the Pareto principle): roughly 80 percent of problems come from about 20 percent of causes, the 'vital few' versus the 'trivial many.' Use it to decide where to focus first. <em>Example:</em> scratches 50, dents 25, misalignment 15, other 10 (total 100): scratches are 50 percent, scratches plus dents are 75 percent, plus misalignment 90 percent. Fix scratches and dents first.</li><li><strong>Fishbone (Ishikawa, cause-and-effect) diagram:</strong> the problem is the 'head'; major cause categories are the 'bones.' In manufacturing the categories are the <strong>6 Ms</strong>: Manpower (people), Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, and Mother Nature (environment). Service versions use the 4 Ps or 8 Ps.</li><li><strong>5 Whys:</strong> keep asking 'why?' (about five times) until you reach the root cause rather than a symptom. <em>Example:</em> Machine stopped. Why? Fuse blew. Why? Bearing overheated. Why? No lubrication. Why? Pump worn. Why? No maintenance schedule. The root cause is the missing maintenance schedule.</li><li><strong>Flowchart / process map:</strong> a diagram of the steps in a process, using standard shapes (ovals start/end, rectangles steps, diamonds decisions). It reveals waste, delays, and rework loops.</li></ul>"},{"h":"7. PDCA, root cause analysis, and total quality management","body":"<p><strong>PDCA</strong> (Plan-Do-Check-Act, also called the Deming or Shewhart cycle) is a repeating loop for continuous improvement:</p><ol><li><strong>Plan:</strong> identify the problem, analyze it, and plan a change.</li><li><strong>Do:</strong> carry out the change on a small scale (a pilot).</li><li><strong>Check (Study):</strong> measure results and compare them with expectations.</li><li><strong>Act:</strong> if it worked, standardize it; if not, adjust and cycle again.</li></ol><p><strong>Root cause analysis (RCA)</strong> is the broad practice of finding the underlying reason a problem occurs so it can be fixed permanently, instead of treating symptoms. Fishbone diagrams, 5 Whys, Pareto charts, and fault trees are RCA tools. A quick fix that does not address the root cause means the problem will return.</p><p><strong>Total Quality Management (TQM)</strong> is an organization-wide philosophy in which everyone is responsible for quality. Core principles: <strong>customer focus</strong> (quality is defined by the customer), <strong>continuous improvement</strong> (kaizen), <strong>employee involvement and empowerment</strong>, <strong>process approach</strong>, <strong>decisions based on data</strong>, and strong <strong>supplier relationships</strong>, all supported by <strong>leadership commitment</strong>. Six Sigma and Lean can be viewed as structured toolkits for achieving TQM goals. Famous quality thinkers include W. Edwards Deming (PDCA, reduce variation), Joseph Juran (Pareto, quality trilogy), and Philip Crosby (zero defects).</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Six Sigma is a data driven method for reducing variation and defects, and a six sigma process produces only three point four defects per million opportunities.","DMAIC stands for Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, and Control, and it is the five phase roadmap used to improve an existing process.","In the Define phase you set goals and customer needs, in Measure you collect data and set a baseline, in Analyze you find root causes, in Improve you test solutions, and in Control you sustain the gains.","Lean focuses on eliminating waste and improving flow, and the wastes can be remembered with the acronym DOWNTIME, which stands for defects, overproduction, waiting, non utilized talent, transportation, inventory, motion, and extra processing.","Lean Six Sigma combines Lean's focus on speed and waste with Six Sigma's focus on variation and defects.","A control chart has a center line and upper and lower control limits, usually set at three standard deviations from the center line.","Common cause variation is the natural random variation in a stable process, while special cause variation comes from unusual events that should be investigated.","A point outside the control limits, or a long run of points on one side of the center line, signals that a process is out of control.","X bar and R charts are used for measured data, tracking the sample average and the sample range, while a p chart tracks the proportion of defective items.","Process capability compares the process spread to the customer's specification limits, and a Cpk below one means the process is making out of specification products.","A Pareto chart ranks causes from largest to smallest to show the vital few, following the eighty twenty rule, and a fishbone diagram organizes possible causes into categories such as the six Ms.","The five whys technique finds root causes by repeatedly asking why, and PDCA, which means Plan, Do, Check, Act, is a continuous improvement cycle at the heart of total quality management."],"examples":[{"q":"A packaging process fills boxes. Samples of n = 9 boxes are weighed each hour. The process standard deviation is 6 grams and the long-run average weight is 100 grams. (a) Find the center line, UCL, and LCL of the X-bar chart. (b) The next sample averages 107 grams. Is the process in control?","steps":["The standard deviation of sample means = sigma / square root of n = 6 / square root of 9 = 6 / 3 = 2 grams.","Center line = 100 grams.","UCL = 100 + 3(2) = 106 grams.","LCL = 100 - 3(2) = 94 grams.","A sample mean of 107 is above the UCL of 106, so it is outside the control limits.","That is a special-cause signal: stop and investigate (for example a miscalibrated scale or a change in material) rather than adjusting blindly."],"answer":"Center line 100 g, UCL 106 g, LCL 94 g. A mean of 107 g is above the UCL, so the process is out of control and a special cause should be investigated."},{"q":"A call center checks 100 calls per day for compliance errors. Over many days the average proportion with an error is p-bar = 0.04. Build the p chart limits. One day has 12 errors in 100 calls. Is it out of control?","steps":["Standard error = square root of (p-bar x (1 - p-bar) / n) = square root of (0.04 x 0.96 / 100) = square root of 0.000384 = 0.0196.","UCL = 0.04 + 3(0.0196) = 0.04 + 0.0588 = 0.0988 (about 9.9 percent).","LCL = 0.04 - 0.0588 = -0.0188, which is below zero, so set LCL = 0.","The day with 12 errors has p = 12 / 100 = 0.12 (12 percent).","0.12 is greater than the UCL of 0.0988, so the point is above the upper control limit."],"answer":"Center line 0.04, UCL about 0.0988, LCL 0. A day at 0.12 is above the UCL, so it is out of control (special cause)."},{"q":"A machining process makes shafts with specification limits of 9.4 mm (LSL) and 10.6 mm (USL). The process mean is 10.1 mm and the standard deviation is 0.1 mm. Calculate Cp and Cpk and interpret them.","steps":["Cp = (USL - LSL) / (6 sigma) = (10.6 - 9.4) / (6 x 0.1) = 1.2 / 0.6 = 2.0.","Upper side: (USL - mean) / (3 sigma) = (10.6 - 10.1) / 0.3 = 0.5 / 0.3 = 1.67.","Lower side: (mean - LSL) / (3 sigma) = (10.1 - 9.4) / 0.3 = 0.7 / 0.3 = 2.33.","Cpk = the smaller of the two = 1.67.","Interpretation: Cp = 2.0 says the natural spread fits twice over in the spec window. Cpk = 1.67 is lower because the process is slightly off-center (mean 10.1 instead of the midpoint 10.0), but it is still above 1.33, so the process is capable."],"answer":"Cp = 2.0 and Cpk is about 1.67. The process is capable but slightly off-center (centering would raise Cpk up to 2.0)."}],"videos":[{"title":"What is Six Sigma and DMAIC?","query":"What is Six Sigma DMAIC explained"},{"title":"Control charts explained: common cause vs special cause variation","query":"control charts explained common cause special cause variation"},{"title":"Lean 8 wastes (DOWNTIME) explained","query":"Lean manufacturing 8 wastes DOWNTIME explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Six Sigma","b":"A data-driven method to reduce variation and defects; a six sigma process yields 3.4 defects per million opportunities."},{"f":"DPMO formula","b":"Defects / (units x opportunities per unit) x 1,000,000."},{"f":"DMAIC","b":"Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control: the Six Sigma roadmap for improving an existing process."},{"f":"Define phase: purpose and tools","b":"Clarify problem, goals, and customer needs. Tools: project charter, SIPOC, voice of the customer."},{"f":"Measure phase: purpose and tools","b":"Collect data and set a baseline. Tools: process map, check sheets, data collection plan, baseline sigma."},{"f":"Analyze phase: purpose and tools","b":"Find root causes. Tools: fishbone diagram, 5 Whys, Pareto chart, regression, hypothesis tests."},{"f":"Improve phase: purpose and tools","b":"Develop and test solutions. Tools: brainstorming, pilots, design of experiments, FMEA, mistake-proofing."},{"f":"Control phase: purpose and tools","b":"Sustain the gains. Tools: control charts, SOPs, control plan, training."},{"f":"Lean","b":"An approach (from Toyota) that maximizes customer value by eliminating waste and improving flow."},{"f":"Lean wastes: DOWNTIME","b":"Defects, Overproduction, Waiting, Non-utilized talent, Transportation, Inventory, Motion, Extra processing."},{"f":"Lean Six Sigma","b":"Combines Lean (speed, waste removal) with Six Sigma (variation and defect reduction)."},{"f":"Control chart parts","b":"Center line (average), UCL and LCL (usually plus or minus 3 sigma), and points plotted over time."},{"f":"Common-cause variation","b":"Natural, always-present random variation in a stable (in-control) process."},{"f":"Special-cause variation","b":"Unusual, assignable events (bad batch, broken tool) that make a process out of control; investigate them."},{"f":"Out-of-control signals","b":"A point beyond the UCL or LCL, a long run of points on one side of the center line, or a steady trend."},{"f":"X-bar and R charts vs p chart","b":"X-bar tracks sample means and R tracks ranges for measured data; a p chart tracks the proportion defective for counted data."},{"f":"Cp and Cpk","b":"Cp = (USL - LSL) / 6 sigma (potential, ignores centering); Cpk uses the nearest spec limit (accounts for centering) and is at most Cp."},{"f":"Pareto chart and 80/20 rule","b":"Bars sorted from largest to smallest; about 80 percent of effects come from 20 percent of causes (vital few)."},{"f":"Fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram and the 6 Ms","b":"Cause-and-effect diagram; Manpower, Machine, Method, Material, Measurement, Mother Nature."},{"f":"5 Whys","b":"Ask 'why?' repeatedly (about five times) to move from a symptom to the root cause."},{"f":"PDCA cycle","b":"Plan, Do, Check, Act: a repeating loop for continuous improvement (Deming/Shewhart)."},{"f":"Total Quality Management (TQM)","b":"Organization-wide approach emphasizing customer focus, continuous improvement, employee involvement, and data-based decisions."}],"questions":[{"q":"A manufacturer sets a goal of reaching Six Sigma quality on a key product. Approximately how many defects per million opportunities does a six sigma process allow?","o":["66,807","233","34","3.4"],"a":3,"e":"Six sigma corresponds to 3.4 DPMO. 66,807 is roughly 3 sigma and 233 is roughly 5 sigma.","d":2},{"q":"A team has fixed the cause of a recurring packaging defect and now wants to prevent it from returning by monitoring the process with control charts and writing a standard procedure. Which DMAIC phase is this?","o":["Control","Define","Analyze","Measure"],"a":0,"e":"Sustaining improvements with control charts, SOPs, and monitoring is the Control phase. Analyze is where causes are identified.","d":2},{"q":"In a DMAIC project, the team builds a fishbone diagram and performs a 5 Whys analysis to determine why late deliveries happen. Which phase are they most likely in?","o":["Define","Improve","Analyze","Control"],"a":2,"e":"Fishbone diagrams and 5 Whys are root cause tools used in the Analyze phase.","d":2},{"q":"At the start of a project, a team writes a project charter and gathers voice-of-the-customer data to clarify what customers need. Which DMAIC phase is this?","o":["Measure","Define","Improve","Control"],"a":1,"e":"Project charters, problem statements, and VOC belong to the Define phase.","d":2},{"q":"In a warehouse, pickers walk back and forth across the building several times per shift to fetch tools that are stored far from their workstations. Which Lean waste is this?","o":["Overproduction","Transportation","Motion","Inventory"],"a":2,"e":"Unnecessary movement of people is Motion waste. Transportation refers to unnecessary movement of materials or products.","d":2},{"q":"A quality analyst tallies defects by type and discovers that two defect types account for about 80 percent of all defects. Which tool best displays this so the team can decide where to focus?","o":["Pareto chart","Flowchart","Scatter diagram","Check sheet only"],"a":0,"e":"A Pareto chart ranks causes from largest to smallest with a cumulative line to show the vital few. A check sheet collects the tallies, but the Pareto chart reveals the 80/20 pattern.","d":2},{"q":"On an X-bar chart, one sample mean falls above the upper control limit. What is the most appropriate interpretation?","o":["The process is in control and no action is needed.","A special cause is likely present, so the team should investigate.","The customer specification has been violated.","The control limits must be wrong and should be widened."],"a":1,"e":"A point beyond the control limits signals special-cause variation. Control limits come from the process data, not from the customer's specs, and widening limits to make signals disappear defeats the purpose.","d":2},{"q":"Which statement best describes common-cause variation?","o":["Variation caused by a broken machine part","Variation that results from a mistake by one operator on one day","Variation that always indicates an out-of-control process","Natural random variation that is always present in a stable process"],"a":3,"e":"Common-cause variation is the inherent noise in a stable process. Broken parts and one-time mistakes are special causes.","d":2},{"q":"An X-bar chart has a center line of 50 and the standard deviation of the sample means is 2. Using plus or minus 3 sigma limits, what is the UCL?","o":["52","54","56","58"],"a":2,"e":"UCL = 50 + 3(2) = 56, and the LCL would be 44. The value 52 uses only one sigma.","d":2},{"q":"A hospital tracks the proportion of patient charts with missing information in a sample of 200 charts each day. Which control chart is most appropriate?","o":["p chart","X-bar chart","R chart","Pareto chart"],"a":0,"e":"The data are counts of defective items turned into a proportion, which is attribute data. That calls for a p chart. X-bar and R charts are for measured variables data, and a Pareto chart is not a control chart.","d":2},{"q":"A team repeatedly asks 'Why?' after each answer: the line stopped, the fuse blew, a bearing overheated, there was no lubrication, there was no maintenance schedule. What technique are they using?","o":["SIPOC","Check sheet","Scatter plot","5 Whys"],"a":3,"e":"Asking why repeatedly to move from a symptom to the root cause is the 5 Whys technique.","d":2},{"q":"A team pilots a new scheduling method in one department, compares the results against the target, and then decides whether to roll it out. Comparing results with the target corresponds to which PDCA step?","o":["Do","Check","Plan","Act"],"a":1,"e":"Measuring the results of the pilot and comparing them to expectations is the Check (Study) step. Running the pilot is Do, and deciding to standardize or adjust is Act.","d":2},{"q":"A process has Cp = 1.8 and Cpk = 0.7. What is the best interpretation?","o":["The process is highly capable and well centered.","The process variation is too large, and centering is irrelevant.","The process has low enough spread to fit the specs, but it is off-center and producing some out-of-spec items.","The process is out of statistical control."],"a":2,"e":"A high Cp shows the natural spread would fit inside the specs, while a much lower Cpk shows the mean is shifted toward one limit. Cpk below 1 means out-of-spec output. Capability indices do not say whether a process is in statistical control.","d":2},{"q":"Which statement best describes Lean Six Sigma?","o":["It combines Lean's focus on waste and flow with Six Sigma's focus on reducing variation and defects.","It is a method used only to design brand new products.","It replaces control charts with inspection of every item.","It applies only to manufacturing, never to services."],"a":0,"e":"Lean Six Sigma merges the two approaches, and it is used in services such as healthcare and banking as well as manufacturing.","d":2},{"q":"In a fishbone diagram for late shipments, the team lists 'worn cutting blade on the saw' under a category. Which of the 6 Ms does it belong to?","o":["Manpower","Method","Measurement","Machine"],"a":3,"e":"A worn blade on equipment falls under Machine. Manpower is people, Method is procedures, and Measurement covers gauges and data.","d":2},{"q":"On a control chart, nine consecutive points fall above the center line, though all are inside the control limits. What should an analyst conclude?","o":["The process is in control because no point exceeds the limits.","The process is probably out of control, because a long run on one side of the center line suggests a shift.","The process is perfectly centered.","The process is capable of meeting specifications."],"a":1,"e":"A run of points on one side of the center line is a non-random pattern that signals a shift in the process mean, even if no single point crosses a limit.","d":2},{"q":"Which activity belongs in the Measure phase of a DMAIC project?","o":["Running a gauge R&R study and collecting baseline defect data","Brainstorming solutions and piloting the best one","Writing the project charter and defining scope","Building control charts and handing off a standard operating procedure"],"a":0,"e":"Measure establishes a trustworthy baseline: validate the measurement system and collect current performance data. The charter belongs to Define, piloting to Improve, and control charts and SOPs to Control.","d":1},{"q":"A bakery bakes 500 croissants every morning, although average daily demand is only about 350. The extra pastries are thrown away at closing. Which Lean waste is most directly illustrated?","o":["Waiting","Inventory","Overproduction","Defects"],"a":2,"e":"Overproduction means making more, or sooner, than the customer demands. Inventory is tempting because unsold pastries pile up, but that stock is a result; the root waste is producing too many.","d":1},{"q":"In the Define phase, which tool gives a high-level, one-page view of a process from its suppliers through to its customers?","o":["Pareto chart","SIPOC diagram","Fishbone diagram","X-bar control chart"],"a":1,"e":"SIPOC (Suppliers, Inputs, Process, Outputs, Customers) scopes a process at a high level. A fishbone diagram is an Analyze tool for causes, not a process overview.","d":1},{"q":"A factory replaces parts at a station only when an empty bin or card signals that the downstream station needs more, instead of building to a forecast. Which Lean tool is this?","o":["5S","Andon","Poka-yoke","Kanban (a pull system)"],"a":3,"e":"Kanban uses visual signals to pull production only when downstream demand exists. Andon is tempting because it is also a signal, but it alerts people to a problem on the line rather than controlling replenishment.","d":1},{"q":"A team uses FMEA with RPN = Severity x Occurrence x Detection. Failure mode A has S = 8, O = 5, D = 3; failure mode B has S = 6, O = 7, D = 4; failure mode C has S = 9, O = 2, D = 6. Which should be addressed first?","o":["Failure mode A (RPN = 120)","Failure mode B (RPN = 168)","Failure mode C (RPN = 108)","All three equally, because each has at least one rating of 6 or more"],"a":1,"e":"RPNs are A = 120, B = 168, C = 108, so B has the highest priority. C has the highest severity rating, which is tempting, but RPN combines all three factors.","d":2},{"q":"An X-bar chart uses subgroups of n = 5. The grand mean of the sample means is 100 and the average range (R-bar) is 10. Using UCL = grand mean + A2 x R-bar with A2 = 0.577, what is the UCL?","o":["94.23","110.00","117.31","105.77"],"a":3,"e":"UCL = 100 + 0.577(10) = 105.77. The 94.23 option is the LCL, and 110 simply adds R-bar without the A2 factor.","d":2},{"q":"Specification limits for a shaft diameter are 40 mm and 60 mm. The process standard deviation is 2.5 mm. What is Cp?","o":["1.33","0.75","2.67","8.00"],"a":0,"e":"Cp = (USL - LSL) / 6 sigma = 20 / 15 = 1.33. The 0.75 option inverts the ratio, and 2.67 divides by 3 sigma instead of 6 sigma.","d":2},{"q":"A quality team records 250 defects: scratches 120, dents 60, paint flaws 40, cracks 20, other 10. Sorted from most to least frequent on a Pareto chart, what is the cumulative percentage after the two most frequent categories?","o":["48%","80%","72%","24%"],"a":2,"e":"(120 + 60) / 250 = 72%. The 80% option applies the '80/20 rule' as if it were a calculation, which is the tempting trap. 48% is scratches alone.","d":2},{"q":"A fixture is redesigned so that a part can only be loaded in the correct orientation, making the wrong placement physically impossible. Which concept does this illustrate?","o":["Poka-yoke (mistake-proofing)","Kaizen event","Heijunka (level scheduling)","Gemba walk"],"a":0,"e":"Poka-yoke designs the process so errors cannot occur. Kaizen is continuous improvement in general, which is why it tempts, but it does not describe a physical error-prevention design.","d":2},{"q":"A process produces 5,000 units, and each unit has 4 opportunities for a defect. Inspectors find 60 defects. What is the DPMO?","o":["300","12,000","3,000","15,000"],"a":2,"e":"DPMO = defects / (units x opportunities) x 1,000,000 = 60 / 20,000 x 1,000,000 = 3,000. The 12,000 option is defects per million units (it ignores the 4 opportunities per unit).","d":2},{"q":"A process has specification limits of 50 and 80, a mean of 74, and a standard deviation of 2. What is Cpk?","o":["2.50","1.00","4.00","6.00"],"a":1,"e":"Cpu = (80 - 74) / (3 x 2) = 1.00 and Cpl = (74 - 50) / 6 = 4.00. Cpk is the smaller, 1.00. The 2.50 option is Cp, which ignores that the mean is off-center.","d":3},{"q":"A p-chart tracks the proportion of defective invoices in samples of n = 100, with an average proportion p-bar = 0.04. Using 3-sigma limits, what is the lower control limit (LCL)?","o":["-0.019 (the raw calculated value)","0.019 (the absolute value of the result)","0.040 (the center line)","0 (a negative LCL is set to zero)"],"a":3,"e":"Sigma = sqrt(0.04 x 0.96 / 100) = 0.0196, so p-bar - 3 sigma = -0.019. A proportion cannot be negative, so the LCL is set to 0. The raw -0.019 is the tempting but impossible answer.","d":3},{"q":"An operator adjusts the machine settings every time a measurement lands above or below the target, even though the control chart shows only random variation inside the limits. What is the most likely effect?","o":["The process becomes more centered and its variation falls over time.","There is no real effect, because the adjustments cancel each other out.","Process variation increases, because the unnecessary adjustments add variation (tampering).","The control limits automatically narrow to match the new adjustments."],"a":2,"e":"Reacting to common-cause variation as if it were special-cause (tampering) pushes the process around and increases variability. The first wrong option sounds sensible, which is why it tempts.","d":3},{"q":"Every subgroup mean and range on a process's control charts falls within the control limits with no patterns, yet 8 percent of parts fail customer specifications. What is the best interpretation?","o":["The process is stable but not capable of meeting specifications, so re-center it or reduce its common-cause variation.","The process is out of control, so special causes must be present and removed first.","The control limits are wrong and should be reset to equal the specification limits.","No conclusion is possible, because control charts say nothing about product quality."],"a":0,"e":"Control limits describe what the process actually does (voice of the process); specification limits describe what the customer needs. A stable process can still be incapable. The first wrong option confuses 'in control' with 'meets specifications'.","d":3},{"q":"On an X-bar and R chart pair, the R chart shows a point above its UCL while the X-bar chart looks in control. What should the team do first?","o":["Ignore the R chart, because the X-bar chart shows the process mean is on target.","Investigate the special cause behind the unstable range, because the X-bar limits depend on a stable range.","Recalculate the X-bar limits using the customer specification limits instead.","Conclude the process is capable because the X-bar chart looks in control."],"a":1,"e":"The R chart tracks within-subgroup spread, and the X-bar limits are computed from the average range. If the range is out of control, the X-bar limits are unreliable, so fix the variability first. Ignoring the R chart is the tempting error.","d":3}],"pairs":[["DPMO","Defects per million opportunities"],["Define","First DMAIC phase, set goals"],["Pareto chart","Bars ranked to show vital few"],["Fishbone","Cause-and-effect diagram with 6 Ms"],["UCL","Three sigma above center line"],["Special cause","Unusual, assignable source of variation"],["PDCA","Plan, Do, Check, Act cycle"],["Muda","Japanese term for waste"]],"traps":["Trap: Points inside the control limits mean the process is fine. Truth: Runs, trends and other non-random patterns inside the limits still signal special causes.","Trap: Control limits and specification limits are the same. Truth: Control limits come from process data (voice of the process); specification limits come from the customer (voice of the customer).","Trap: Adjust the process whenever a point is off target. Truth: Reacting to common-cause variation (tampering) increases variation.","Trap: Cp and Cpk always tell the same story. Truth: Cp ignores centering, Cpk uses the nearer limit, and Cpk equals Cp only when the process is centered.","Trap: A Pareto chart reveals root causes. Truth: It only prioritizes categories by frequency; fishbone diagrams and 5 Whys dig into causes."],"mnemonic":"DMAIC = Charter, Count, Cause, Cure, Control. And Cpk = Cp 'Kept honest' by the closer spec limit.","cram":["DMAIC: Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control.","Six Sigma allows 3.4 defects per million opportunities.","DPMO = defects / (units x opportunities) x 1,000,000.","Control limits are mean plus or minus 3 sigma; they are not specification limits.","Cp = (USL-LSL)/6 sigma; Cpk = nearer-limit gap/3 sigma.","Pareto prioritizes; fishbone and 5 Whys find causes.","DOWNTIME wastes: defects, overproduction, waiting, talent, transport, inventory, motion, extra-processing.","p-chart tracks proportion defective; c-chart tracks defect counts."],"ytv":[{"title":"DMAIC Methodology - Lean Six Sigma Training","channel":"GoSkills.com","id":"CZfSpkL4-fE","why":"Overview of the five DMAIC phases and the tools used in each."},{"title":"What's The Difference Between Common and Special Cause Variation?","channel":"Green Belt Academy","id":"vWwTDRcXgT8","why":"Clarifies common vs special cause variation, the basis for reading control charts."},{"title":"8 Wastes of Lean Manufacturing Explained","channel":"GMP GUIDE","id":"TKbC6rD6D1c","why":"Walks through the eight Lean wastes (DOWNTIME) with examples."}]},{"id":"u12","title":"Decision-Making Tools","day":6,"summary":"The decision-making process, expected monetary value, decision trees, uncertainty criteria, cost-benefit, ROI and break-even analysis, what-if analysis, SWOT, cognitive biases, data storytelling, and ethical data use.","lesson":[{"h":"1. The decision-making process and decision environments","body":"<p>Good decisions follow a repeatable process instead of a gut reaction. A standard seven-step version:</p><ol><li><strong>Identify the problem</strong> or opportunity clearly (a vague problem leads to a vague answer).</li><li><strong>Gather data</strong> and information that is relevant and reliable.</li><li><strong>Generate alternatives</strong> - more than one realistic option.</li><li><strong>Evaluate the alternatives</strong> against criteria such as cost, risk, and benefit, using tools like those in this unit.</li><li><strong>Choose</strong> the best alternative.</li><li><strong>Implement</strong> the decision with a clear plan and owner.</li><li><strong>Review</strong> the results and learn from them, adjusting if needed.</li></ol><p>The right tool depends on how much you know about outcomes. There are three decision environments:</p><ul><li><strong>Certainty:</strong> you know exactly what will happen for each alternative (a fixed-price supplier contract). Just choose the best outcome.</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> you do not know which outcome will occur, but you <em>know the probabilities</em> (historical data says a 60 percent chance of strong demand). Use expected value and decision trees.</li><li><strong>Uncertainty:</strong> you know the possible outcomes but <em>cannot assign probabilities</em> (a never-before-seen product category). Use criteria such as maximax, maximin, and minimax regret.</li></ul>"},{"h":"2. Payoff tables and expected monetary value (EMV)","body":"<p>A <strong>payoff table</strong> lists the alternatives in rows, the possible <strong>states of nature</strong> (things outside your control, such as strong or weak demand) in columns, and the payoff (profit or gain) in each cell.</p><p>The <strong>expected monetary value (EMV)</strong> of an alternative is its <em>probability-weighted average payoff</em>: multiply each payoff by the probability of its state and add them up. Under risk, you generally choose the alternative with the <strong>highest EMV</strong> (or lowest expected cost if the numbers are costs).</p><p><strong>Example.</strong> Probabilities: strong demand 0.6, weak demand 0.4. Payoffs in $1,000s:</p><ul><li>Build a large plant: 200 if strong, -60 if weak. EMV = 0.6(200) + 0.4(-60) = 120 - 24 = <strong>96</strong>.</li><li>Build a small plant: 100 if strong, 20 if weak. EMV = 0.6(100) + 0.4(20) = 60 + 8 = <strong>68</strong>.</li><li>Do nothing: 0 and 0. EMV = <strong>0</strong>.</li></ul><p>The large plant has the highest EMV ($96,000), so it is the best choice under this criterion. Remember that EMV is a long-run average. It will never be the actual payoff of a single decision (the large plant will pay either +200 or -60, never 96). Companies that make many similar decisions benefit most from EMV; a one-time bet that could bankrupt you deserves extra caution.</p><p><strong>Expected value of perfect information (EVPI)</strong> is the most you should pay for a perfect forecast: EV with perfect information minus the best EMV. Here: 0.6(200) + 0.4(20) = 128; EVPI = 128 - 96 = 32 ($32,000).</p>"},{"h":"3. Decision trees","body":"<p>A <strong>decision tree</strong> is a left-to-right diagram of a decision that unfolds over time. It uses two kinds of nodes:</p><ul><li><strong>Square = decision node.</strong> A choice <em>you</em> control. Branches are the alternatives.</li><li><strong>Circle = chance node.</strong> An uncertain event you do not control. Branches are the possible outcomes, each labeled with a probability (the probabilities leaving a circle must add to 1.0). The branch ends show the payoffs.</li></ul><p>You solve a tree by <strong>folding back</strong> (rolling back) from right to left:</p><ol><li>At each <strong>chance node</strong>, compute the EMV (sum of probability times payoff) and write it on the circle.</li><li>At each <strong>decision node</strong>, pick the branch with the best value (highest EMV for profits) and cross out the others.</li><li>The value at the first square is the EMV of the best overall strategy, and the path of uncrossed branches is the recommended plan.</li></ol><p><em>Example.</em> A firm can (A) launch a product (net payoff +150 with probability 0.4 if it succeeds, -30 with probability 0.6 if it fails; amounts in $1,000s), (B) license it to another company for a sure +15, or (C) do nothing for 0. The chance node for A has EMV = 0.4(150) + 0.6(-30) = 60 - 18 = 42. Comparing at the decision square: A = 42, B = 15, C = 0, so launch. Trees handle multi-stage problems (for example, 'pay for market research first, then decide') that a single payoff table cannot.</p>"},{"h":"4. Decisions under uncertainty: maximax, maximin, minimax regret","body":"<p>When you cannot assign probabilities, you apply a decision attitude. Use the same payoff table from earlier (in $1,000s):</p><ul><li>Large plant: strong 200, weak -60.</li><li>Small plant: strong 100, weak 20.</li><li>Do nothing: strong 0, weak 0.</li></ul><p><strong>Maximax (the optimist).</strong> Find the best possible payoff for each alternative, then pick the largest. Best cases: 200, 100, 0. Choose the <strong>large plant</strong>.</p><p><strong>Maximin (the pessimist).</strong> Find the worst payoff for each alternative, then pick the largest of those (the best of the worst). Worst cases: -60, 20, 0. Choose the <strong>small plant</strong> (20).</p><p><strong>Minimax regret (the 'don't be sorry' approach).</strong> <em>Regret</em> (opportunity loss) is how much better you could have done had you known the true state: the best payoff in that column minus your payoff. In the strong column the best is 200, so regrets are 0 (large), 100 (small), 200 (nothing). In the weak column the best is 20, so regrets are 80 (large), 0 (small), 20 (nothing). The maximum regret for each alternative: large 80, small 100, nothing 200. Pick the smallest maximum regret: the <strong>large plant</strong> (80).</p><p>Notice that different criteria can recommend different options. That is normal; the criterion should match the decision maker's attitude toward risk.</p>"},{"h":"5. Cost-benefit analysis, ROI, and break-even analysis","body":"<p><strong>Cost-benefit analysis (CBA)</strong> compares the total expected costs of a project with its total expected benefits, converted to money. If benefits exceed costs (net benefit greater than 0, or benefit-cost ratio greater than 1), the project is financially attractive. Because money received later is worth less, careful analyses discount future amounts to present value (net present value, NPV). Remember to include <em>all</em> costs (including hidden or ongoing ones) and to consider intangible benefits and risks.</p><p><strong>Return on investment (ROI)</strong> measures gain relative to cost:</p><p><code>ROI = (gain from investment - cost of investment) / cost of investment</code>, usually expressed as a percent. Spend $40,000 and earn back $52,000: ROI = (52,000 - 40,000) / 40,000 = 0.30 = <strong>30 percent</strong>. <strong>Payback period</strong> is how long it takes to recoup the investment: $40,000 cost with $10,000 of benefit per year gives 4 years.</p><p><strong>Break-even analysis</strong> finds the sales volume at which total revenue equals total cost (profit is zero):</p><p><code>Break-even units = fixed costs / (price per unit - variable cost per unit)</code></p><p>The denominator is the <strong>contribution margin</strong> per unit, the amount each sale contributes toward covering fixed costs. <em>Example:</em> fixed costs $60,000, price $50, variable cost $30 per unit. Contribution margin = $20. Break-even = 60,000 / 20 = <strong>3,000 units</strong> (revenue of $150,000). To earn a profit of $20,000: (60,000 + 20,000) / 20 = 4,000 units. Selling fewer than the break-even quantity means a loss; more means a profit.</p>"},{"h":"6. Sensitivity analysis, scenario analysis, and SWOT","body":"<p>Every analysis rests on assumptions that might be wrong. Two tools test how much that matters:</p><ul><li><strong>Sensitivity (what-if) analysis:</strong> change <em>one</em> input at a time (price, demand, cost, interest rate) and see how much the result changes. Inputs that swing the answer the most are the ones to estimate carefully. In Excel you can use Data Tables or Goal Seek. If the best decision stays the same across a wide range of inputs, it is <em>robust</em>.</li><li><strong>Scenario analysis:</strong> change <em>several</em> inputs together to build coherent stories such as best case, most likely case, and worst case, then compare outcomes. Excel's Scenario Manager stores these sets of assumptions.</li></ul><p><strong>SWOT analysis</strong> is a strategic planning framework that organizes the situation into four boxes:</p><ul><li><strong>Strengths</strong> (internal, helpful): a strong brand, skilled staff.</li><li><strong>Weaknesses</strong> (internal, harmful): outdated systems, limited cash.</li><li><strong>Opportunities</strong> (external, helpful): a growing market, a favorable regulation.</li><li><strong>Threats</strong> (external, harmful): a new competitor, an economic downturn.</li></ul><p>The memory trick: <strong>S and W are inside the company; O and T are outside.</strong> SWOT organizes thinking but does not by itself pick a winner; follow it with quantitative evaluation.</p>"},{"h":"7. Cognitive biases, data storytelling, and ethics","body":"<p><strong>Cognitive biases</strong> are systematic thinking errors that distort even data-driven decisions:</p><ul><li><strong>Anchoring:</strong> relying too heavily on the first number or piece of information (the first price quoted in a negotiation).</li><li><strong>Confirmation bias:</strong> seeking and favoring evidence that supports what you already believe while ignoring contrary evidence.</li><li><strong>Sunk cost fallacy:</strong> continuing a project because of what has already been spent, even though that money is gone. Only future costs and benefits should matter.</li><li><strong>Overconfidence:</strong> overestimating the accuracy of your own judgments and forecasts, and underestimating risks.</li><li><strong>Groupthink:</strong> a cohesive group suppressing dissent to keep harmony, so flawed plans go unchallenged. Counter it with devil's advocates and anonymous input.</li><li><strong>Availability bias:</strong> overweighting examples that come easily to mind (a recent, vivid failure) rather than the actual statistics.</li></ul><p><strong>Communicating findings (data storytelling).</strong> Analysis only creates value if decision makers understand and act on it. Know your audience; lead with the key finding and recommendation; give context (compared with what?); use simple, honest visuals (one message per chart, clear labels, no distorted axes); explain the 'so what' and the next steps; be transparent about assumptions and uncertainty.</p><p><strong>Ethics and responsible data use.</strong> Collect and use data with consent and for the stated purpose, protect privacy and security, avoid biased data or algorithms that unfairly harm groups of people, do not cherry-pick or misleadingly present results, be transparent about limitations, and follow laws and regulations (for example, privacy laws).</p>"}],"keyPoints":["A sound decision process has seven steps: identify the problem, gather data, generate alternatives, evaluate them, choose one, implement it, and review the results.","Under certainty you know the outcome of each choice, under risk you know the probabilities of the outcomes, and under uncertainty you know the possible outcomes but not their probabilities.","Expected monetary value is the probability weighted average of an alternative's payoffs, and under risk you generally choose the alternative with the highest expected monetary value.","In a decision tree, squares are decisions you control and circles are chance events, and you solve the tree by folding back from right to left.","Maximax is the optimist's rule that picks the alternative with the best possible payoff, and maximin is the pessimist's rule that picks the alternative with the best worst case payoff.","Minimax regret picks the alternative whose largest opportunity loss is the smallest.","Return on investment equals the gain minus the cost, divided by the cost, and cost benefit analysis compares total expected benefits against total expected costs.","Break even quantity equals fixed costs divided by the price per unit minus the variable cost per unit, and selling more than that quantity produces a profit.","Sensitivity analysis changes one input at a time to see how much the result changes, while scenario analysis changes several inputs together to build best case, likely case, and worst case stories.","In a SWOT analysis, strengths and weaknesses are internal to the organization, while opportunities and threats are external.","Common cognitive biases include anchoring on the first number seen, confirmation bias, the sunk cost fallacy, overconfidence, groupthink, and availability bias.","Good data storytelling leads with the key finding and recommendation for the audience, and ethical data use means respecting privacy and consent, avoiding bias, and presenting results honestly."],"examples":[{"q":"A company can build a large plant, a small plant, or do nothing. The probability of strong demand is 0.6 and weak demand is 0.4. Payoffs in $1,000s: Large plant = 200 (strong), -60 (weak); Small plant = 100 (strong), 20 (weak); Do nothing = 0, 0. Which alternative has the highest EMV? What is the expected value of perfect information?","steps":["EMV(Large) = 0.6(200) + 0.4(-60) = 120 - 24 = 96.","EMV(Small) = 0.6(100) + 0.4(20) = 60 + 8 = 68.","EMV(Do nothing) = 0.6(0) + 0.4(0) = 0.","Highest EMV is the large plant at 96 ($96,000).","With perfect information you would pick Large if you knew demand was strong (200) and Small if you knew it was weak (20). Expected value with perfect information = 0.6(200) + 0.4(20) = 120 + 8 = 128.","EVPI = 128 - 96 = 32, so you should never pay more than $32,000 for a perfect forecast."],"answer":"Build the large plant (EMV = $96,000). EVPI = $32,000."},{"q":"Decision tree: Alternative A is to launch a new product. If it succeeds (probability 0.4) the net payoff is +$150,000; if it fails (probability 0.6) the net payoff is -$30,000. Alternative B is to license the idea for a sure +$15,000. Alternative C is to do nothing ($0). Which should the firm choose?","steps":["Draw a square (decision) with three branches: launch, license, do nothing.","The launch branch leads to a circle (chance node) with two branches: success (0.4, +150,000) and failure (0.6, -30,000). Check that 0.4 + 0.6 = 1.0.","Fold back the circle: EMV = 0.4(150,000) + 0.6(-30,000) = 60,000 - 18,000 = 42,000.","License = 15,000 for certain. Do nothing = 0.","At the decision square compare 42,000, 15,000, and 0. The highest is 42,000, so choose launch.","Note the risk: there is a 60 percent chance of losing $30,000, so a risk-averse firm might still prefer licensing."],"answer":"Launch the product, with an EMV of $42,000 (better than licensing at $15,000 or doing nothing at $0)."},{"q":"A start-up sells a product at $50 per unit. Variable cost is $30 per unit and fixed costs are $60,000. (a) What is the break-even quantity? (b) How many units are needed to earn a $20,000 profit? (c) A separate $40,000 equipment purchase returns $52,000. What is its ROI?","steps":["(a) Contribution margin per unit = 50 - 30 = $20. Break-even = 60,000 / 20 = 3,000 units (revenue at break-even = 3,000 x 50 = $150,000).","(b) Units for target profit = (fixed costs + target profit) / contribution margin = (60,000 + 20,000) / 20 = 4,000 units.","(c) ROI = (gain - cost) / cost = (52,000 - 40,000) / 40,000 = 12,000 / 40,000 = 0.30 = 30 percent."],"answer":"(a) 3,000 units. (b) 4,000 units. (c) ROI = 30 percent."}],"videos":[{"title":"Expected monetary value and decision trees","query":"expected monetary value decision tree explained"},{"title":"Maximax, maximin, and minimax regret","query":"maximax maximin minimax regret decision making under uncertainty"},{"title":"Break-even analysis and cognitive biases in decisions","query":"break-even analysis explained contribution margin"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Steps of the decision-making process","b":"Identify the problem, gather data, generate alternatives, evaluate, choose, implement, review."},{"f":"Decision under certainty","b":"The outcome of each alternative is known for sure."},{"f":"Decision under risk","b":"Outcomes are uncertain but their probabilities are known; use EMV and decision trees."},{"f":"Decision under uncertainty","b":"Possible outcomes are known but probabilities are not; use maximax, maximin, or minimax regret."},{"f":"Payoff table","b":"Table with alternatives as rows, states of nature as columns, and payoffs in the cells."},{"f":"Expected monetary value (EMV)","b":"The sum of each payoff times its probability; choose the highest EMV when maximizing profit."},{"f":"Does EMV equal the actual payoff of one decision?","b":"No. It is a long-run average; any single outcome will differ."},{"f":"EVPI (expected value of perfect information)","b":"EV with perfect information minus the best EMV; the maximum worth of a perfect forecast."},{"f":"Decision tree: square vs circle","b":"Square = decision node (you choose); circle = chance node (uncertain event with probabilities)."},{"f":"Folding back a decision tree","b":"Work right to left: compute EMV at chance nodes, pick the best branch at decision nodes."},{"f":"Maximax","b":"Optimist's rule: choose the alternative with the largest possible payoff."},{"f":"Maximin","b":"Pessimist's rule: choose the alternative whose worst payoff is the best."},{"f":"Minimax regret","b":"Compute regret (best payoff in a column minus your payoff), find each alternative's maximum regret, pick the smallest."},{"f":"Cost-benefit analysis","b":"Compare total expected costs and benefits in money terms; proceed if benefits exceed costs."},{"f":"ROI formula","b":"(Gain from investment - cost of investment) / cost of investment."},{"f":"Break-even point formula","b":"Fixed costs / (price per unit - variable cost per unit)."},{"f":"Contribution margin","b":"Price per unit minus variable cost per unit; the amount each unit contributes toward fixed costs and profit."},{"f":"Sensitivity analysis vs scenario analysis","b":"Sensitivity changes one input at a time; scenario analysis changes several inputs together (best, likely, worst case)."},{"f":"SWOT","b":"Strengths and Weaknesses (internal); Opportunities and Threats (external)."},{"f":"Anchoring bias","b":"Over-relying on the first piece of information encountered, such as an initial price."},{"f":"Confirmation bias and sunk cost fallacy","b":"Favoring evidence that fits existing beliefs; continuing a project because of past spending that cannot be recovered."},{"f":"Groupthink and overconfidence","b":"Groupthink: a group suppresses dissent to keep harmony. Overconfidence: overestimating your own accuracy and underestimating risk."}],"questions":[{"q":"A retailer has ten years of data showing a 30 percent chance of high demand, 50 percent chance of moderate demand, and 20 percent chance of low demand for a product line. Which kind of decision environment is this?","o":["Decision under certainty","Decision under risk","Decision under uncertainty","Sensitivity analysis"],"a":1,"e":"The outcomes are not known in advance but their probabilities are, which defines decision making under risk. Under uncertainty, probabilities are unknown.","d":2},{"q":"A project has three possible payoffs: $100,000 with probability 0.3, $40,000 with probability 0.5, and -$20,000 with probability 0.2. What is the EMV?","o":["$46,000","$40,000","$60,000","$120,000"],"a":0,"e":"EMV = 0.3(100,000) + 0.5(40,000) + 0.2(-20,000) = 30,000 + 20,000 - 4,000 = $46,000. The $120,000 figure is the unweighted sum of the payoffs.","d":2},{"q":"In a decision tree, what do the square nodes represent?","o":["Uncertain events with assigned probabilities","Final payoffs only","Decisions that the decision maker controls","Sunk costs"],"a":2,"e":"Squares are decision nodes, where you choose among alternatives. Circles are chance nodes representing uncertain events.","d":2},{"q":"A cautious manager does not know the probabilities of the possible outcomes and wants to protect against the worst. For each alternative she finds the worst possible payoff and picks the alternative with the best of those. Which criterion is she using?","o":["Maximax","Minimax regret","Expected monetary value","Maximin"],"a":3,"e":"Choosing the alternative with the best worst-case payoff is the maximin criterion, the pessimist's rule.","d":2},{"q":"An optimistic executive picks the alternative that offers the single largest possible payoff in the payoff table, ignoring the downside. Which criterion is this?","o":["Maximin","Maximax","Minimax regret","Expected monetary value"],"a":1,"e":"Maximax chooses the alternative with the maximum of the maximum payoffs - the optimist's rule.","d":2},{"q":"Which description matches the minimax regret criterion?","o":["Choose the alternative with the highest possible payoff.","Choose the alternative with the highest probability-weighted payoff.","Choose the alternative whose largest opportunity loss (regret) is the smallest.","Choose the alternative with the best worst-case payoff."],"a":2,"e":"Minimax regret minimizes the maximum regret. The first option describes maximax, the second EMV, and the last maximin.","d":2},{"q":"A company has fixed costs of $24,000. Its product sells for $20 per unit and has a variable cost of $12 per unit. What is the break-even quantity?","o":["1,200 units","2,000 units","1,500 units","3,000 units"],"a":3,"e":"Break-even = fixed costs / (price - variable cost) = 24,000 / (20 - 12) = 24,000 / 8 = 3,000 units. Dividing by the price alone (1,200) ignores variable costs.","d":2},{"q":"A firm invests $50,000 in new equipment and receives $65,000 in total returns. What is the ROI?","o":["30%","15%","23%","130%"],"a":0,"e":"ROI = (65,000 - 50,000) / 50,000 = 0.30 = 30 percent. The 130 percent figure divides total return by cost without subtracting the cost.","d":2},{"q":"A company has already spent $2 million on a software project that is far behind schedule and unlikely to succeed. An executive insists on continuing because 'we have already invested too much to quit.' Which bias does this illustrate?","o":["Anchoring","Confirmation bias","Sunk cost fallacy","Groupthink"],"a":2,"e":"Letting past, unrecoverable spending drive the decision is the sunk cost fallacy. Only future costs and benefits should be compared.","d":2},{"q":"During a salary negotiation, an applicant's expectations are heavily influenced by the first figure the recruiter mentioned, even though the market data suggest a very different figure. Which bias is at work?","o":["Availability bias","Anchoring","Overconfidence","Sunk cost fallacy"],"a":1,"e":"Over-relying on the first number encountered is anchoring.","d":2},{"q":"A marketing director believes a new campaign is working. She reads only the reports that show positive results and dismisses reports that show weak sales. Which bias is this?","o":["Anchoring","Groupthink","Availability bias","Confirmation bias"],"a":3,"e":"Seeking out and favoring information that supports an existing belief while ignoring contradicting evidence is confirmation bias.","d":2},{"q":"Members of a tight-knit planning committee privately doubt a risky proposal but say nothing in order to keep harmony, and the plan is approved unanimously without any challenge. Which bias is this?","o":["Groupthink","Anchoring","Sunk cost fallacy","Availability bias"],"a":0,"e":"Suppressing dissent to maintain group harmony is groupthink. Devil's advocate roles and anonymous feedback can help prevent it.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst changes the forecast selling price by plus or minus 10 percent, one input at a time, to see how much total profit changes. What is this technique called?","o":["Pareto analysis","Delphi method","Sensitivity (what-if) analysis","SWOT analysis"],"a":2,"e":"Changing one input at a time to see its effect on the result is sensitivity analysis. Scenario analysis changes several inputs together.","d":2},{"q":"In a SWOT analysis of a regional bakery, the arrival of a large national chain in its market would be classified as which element?","o":["Weakness","Threat","Strength","Opportunity"],"a":1,"e":"A competitor entering the market is an external, harmful factor, which is a Threat. Strengths and weaknesses are internal.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst must present results to senior executives who have ten minutes. Which approach is best practice for communicating the findings?","o":["Show all the raw data tables first so executives can reach their own conclusions.","Use as many different chart types as possible to demonstrate technical skill.","Explain the full statistical methodology before revealing the results.","Start with the key finding and recommendation, then support it with a few clear visuals tailored to the audience."],"a":3,"e":"Executives need the main message, the 'so what', and the recommended action first, with supporting evidence kept simple and relevant.","d":2},{"q":"A retailer collected customers' email addresses to send order receipts and now sells the list to a third-party advertiser without telling customers. Which statement is most accurate?","o":["This violates privacy and ethical data-use principles because data was used beyond the purpose customers consented to.","This is acceptable because the company owns all data it collects.","This is acceptable as long as the list is sold at a fair price.","This is only a problem if the list contains more than a million addresses."],"a":0,"e":"Responsible data use requires consent and using data for the stated purpose. Using personal data in ways customers did not agree to breaches privacy expectations and often laws.","d":2},{"q":"Which is the correct first step in a structured decision-making process?","o":["Select the alternative with the best expected outcome","Identify and clearly define the problem or decision to be made","Implement the chosen course of action","Evaluate the results of the decision"],"a":1,"e":"A good decision starts with a clear problem definition; the later steps (generate and evaluate alternatives, choose, implement, review) depend on it. Jumping to choosing the best option is a common shortcut.","d":1},{"q":"A company is entering a market with a technology so new that managers cannot assign credible probabilities to any outcome. Which decision environment is this?","o":["Decision making under certainty","Decision making under risk","Decision making under competition","Decision making under uncertainty"],"a":3,"e":"Under uncertainty, outcomes are known but probabilities are not, so criteria like maximin or minimax regret are used. Under risk the probabilities are known, which is the tempting but wrong choice.","d":1},{"q":"A product sells for $25 per unit and has a variable cost of $15 per unit. What is the contribution margin per unit?","o":["$10","$15","$25","$40"],"a":0,"e":"Contribution margin = price - variable cost = 25 - 15 = $10. The $15 is the variable cost itself, and $40 adds the two figures.","d":1},{"q":"A plant has one production line and can make Product A (profit $50,000) or Product B (profit $65,000). The manager chooses Product B. What is the opportunity cost of this choice?","o":["$15,000","$65,000","$50,000","$115,000"],"a":2,"e":"Opportunity cost is the value of the best alternative given up, here the $50,000 from Product A. The $15,000 is the net gain from choosing B, which is the tempting option.","d":1},{"q":"Option A has a 60% chance of earning $50,000 and a 40% chance of losing $10,000. Option B is a certain $20,000. Which option has the higher EMV, and by how much?","o":["Option B, by $6,000","Option A, by $26,000","Option A, by $6,000","They are equal"],"a":2,"e":"EMV(A) = 0.6(50,000) + 0.4(-10,000) = 26,000, compared with 20,000 for B, so A is higher by $6,000. The 'B' option ignores that EMV weights outcomes by probability.","d":2},{"q":"Launching a product costs $30,000. There is a 0.6 chance of success paying $100,000 and a 0.4 chance of failure paying $10,000. Not launching yields $0. What is the net EMV of launching, and what should the firm do?","o":["EMV = $34,000, so launch","EMV = $64,000, so launch","EMV = $70,000, so launch","EMV = $34,000, but do not launch because failure is possible"],"a":0,"e":"Expected payoff = 0.6(100,000) + 0.4(10,000) = 64,000; subtract the $30,000 cost to get $34,000, which beats $0. The $64,000 figure forgets the launch cost; the last option rejects the EMV rule.","d":2},{"q":"A company has fixed costs of $60,000 and a contribution margin ratio of 40 percent. What sales revenue is needed to break even?","o":["$24,000","$100,000","$240,000","$150,000"],"a":3,"e":"Break-even revenue = fixed costs / contribution margin ratio = 60,000 / 0.40 = 150,000. The $24,000 figure multiplies instead of dividing.","d":2},{"q":"An analyst must show executives (a) how weekly sales trended over the past two years and (b) whether store size is related to sales across 60 stores. Which pair of visuals is most appropriate?","o":["(a) a pie chart; (b) a line chart","(a) a line chart; (b) a scatter plot","(a) a scatter plot; (b) a pie chart","(a) a stacked column chart; (b) a stacked column chart"],"a":1,"e":"A line chart shows change over time, and a scatter plot shows the relationship between two numeric variables. Pie charts show parts of a whole and cannot show trend or relationship.","d":2},{"q":"After a widely reported data breach at a competitor, a manager overestimates the likelihood of a breach at her own firm and diverts most of the budget to security, ignoring the firm's low historical incident rate. Which bias is this?","o":["Availability bias","Anchoring bias","Confirmation bias","Sunk cost fallacy"],"a":0,"e":"Availability bias is overweighting information that is vivid and easy to recall, such as a recent news story, instead of base rates. Confirmation bias would require her to seek only evidence that agrees with her prior belief.","d":2},{"q":"A regional hospital system has a strong reputation, a growing elderly population in its service area, outdated electronic health record software, and a proposed state law that would cut reimbursement rates. Which SWOT classification is correct?","o":["Reputation = Opportunity; aging population = Strength; outdated software = Threat; reimbursement cut = Weakness","Reputation = Strength; aging population = Threat; outdated software = Weakness; reimbursement cut = Opportunity","Reputation = Strength; aging population = Opportunity; outdated software = Weakness; reimbursement cut = Threat","Reputation = Strength; aging population = Opportunity; outdated software = Threat; reimbursement cut = Weakness"],"a":2,"e":"Strengths and weaknesses are internal; opportunities and threats are external. The last wrong option mixes up internal and external, calling the internal software issue a threat and the external law a weakness.","d":2},{"q":"A firm choosing a plant size faces three demand scenarios. Payoffs ($000) for Weak, Average, Strong demand are: Large plant -40, 30, 120; Medium plant -10, 40, 80; Small plant 10, 25, 40; No expansion 0, 0, 0. Which alternative does the minimax regret criterion select?","o":["Large plant","Medium plant","Small plant","No expansion"],"a":1,"e":"The best payoffs per scenario are 10, 40, and 120, so the maximum regrets are Large 50, Medium 40, Small 80, None 120. The smallest maximum regret is Medium (40). Large is the maximax choice and Small the maximin choice, which are the tempting mix-ups.","d":3},{"q":"A firm can accept a guaranteed $30,000 licensing fee, or launch its own product that pays $100,000 if it succeeds and loses $20,000 if it fails. At what probability of success is the firm indifferent between the two options?","o":["About 30%","About 50%","About 58.3%","About 41.7%"],"a":3,"e":"Set p(100,000) + (1 - p)(-20,000) = 30,000, so 120,000p = 50,000 and p = 0.4167. The 58.3% option is the complement (1 - p), and 50% is a guess from the midpoint.","d":3},{"q":"A product sells for $50 with a variable cost of $30 and fixed costs of $40,000, so the break-even point is 2,000 units. A supplier raises the variable cost to $35 per unit, while a new lease cuts fixed costs to $27,000. What is the new break-even point?","o":["900 units","1,350 units","1,800 units","2,000 units"],"a":2,"e":"New contribution margin = 50 - 35 = 15, so break-even = 27,000 / 15 = 1,800 units. The 1,350 option applies the old $20 margin, and 900 divides by the old variable cost of $30.","d":3},{"q":"A $200,000 machine will reduce costs by $50,000 per year for 5 years. What is the total ROI over the 5 years?","o":["25%","5%","20%","125%"],"a":0,"e":"Total benefit = 5 x 50,000 = 250,000. ROI = (250,000 - 200,000) / 200,000 = 25%. The 5% option spreads that 25% across the 5 years, and 125% forgets to subtract the investment.","d":3},{"q":"Managers must choose a plan for 600 jobs at risk. Plan A: 200 jobs are saved for sure. Plan B: a 1/3 chance all 600 are saved and a 2/3 chance none are saved. Most managers choose A. When the same options are worded as Plan C: 400 jobs are lost for sure, and Plan D: a 1/3 chance no jobs are lost and a 2/3 chance all 600 are lost, most choose D. Which bias explains the reversal?","o":["Anchoring bias","Framing effect","Overconfidence bias","Groupthink"],"a":1,"e":"The plans are mathematically identical (A = C, B = D; the expected outcome is 200 saved), but gain wording leads people to avoid risk and loss wording leads them to seek it. Anchoring involves an initial number biasing an estimate, which does not occur here.","d":3}],"pairs":[["EMV","Probability-weighted average of payoffs"],["Decision node","Square: a choice you control"],["Chance node","Circle: uncertain outcome"],["Maximin","Pick best of the worst cases"],["Break-even point","Where revenue equals total cost"],["ROI","Net gain divided by cost"],["Anchoring","Over-relying on first number seen"],["Sunk cost","Past spending that cannot be recovered"]],"traps":["Trap: Maximin and minimax regret always select the same alternative. Truth: Maximin protects against the lowest payoff; minimax regret minimizes the largest opportunity loss, and they can differ.","Trap: The highest EMV guarantees the best result. Truth: EMV is a long-run average; a single decision can still lose money, so risk tolerance matters.","Trap: Break-even units equal fixed costs divided by the selling price. Truth: Divide fixed costs by the contribution margin per unit (price minus variable cost).","Trap: Money already spent should influence whether to continue a project. Truth: Sunk costs are unrecoverable and irrelevant; only future costs and benefits count.","Trap: Any internal or external factor can be called a strength or threat. Truth: In SWOT, strengths and weaknesses are internal; opportunities and threats are external."],"mnemonic":"Maximax = Mr. Optimist (best of the best); Maximin = Ms. Cautious (best of the worst); Regret = Monday-morning quarterback (minimize the biggest 'I should have').","cram":["Certainty: outcome known; risk: probabilities known; uncertainty: probabilities unknown.","EMV = sum of probability x payoff; choose the highest EMV.","Decision trees: squares are decisions, circles are chance events; fold back right to left.","Maximax = best of the best; maximin = best of the worst.","Regret = best payoff in a state minus your payoff; minimize maximum regret.","Break-even units = fixed costs / (price - variable cost per unit).","ROI = (gain - cost) / cost.","Sunk costs are irrelevant; SWOT strengths and weaknesses are internal."],"ytv":[{"title":"Decision Tree and Expected Monetary Value for the PMP Exam","channel":"CorneliusFichtner","id":"uC2XBGpIcAs","why":"Shows how to compute EMV and fold back a decision tree."},{"title":"MiniMax, MaxiMin, MiniMax Regret: Clearly Explained","channel":"Andrew Scarffe, PhD MBA","id":"ecFPqsblCUs","why":"Explains the decision-under-uncertainty criteria with a payoff table."},{"title":"Contribution Margin & Break-Even Analysis Explained","channel":"Prof John K","id":"-y6JlCtwiw4","why":"Covers contribution margin and break-even calculations step by step."}]}];